{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system","id":1229940,"title":"Crafting system","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-06-24T22:42:56-07:00","id":1229940,"title":"Crafting system","content":"I've been putting off reading the crafting rules since getting the 3e book due to their bad rep, and I've just tried making sense of them. <br /> <br /> Are there any good home brew systems out there for 3e crafting?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229925-hello-i-did-up-a-scavenger-lands-map-based-on-3e\" target=\"_blank\">3e Scavenger Lands map</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/a/NQewCv0\" target=\"_blank\">3e Combat tracker card</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/uGrZk2r\" target=\"_blank\">2e Nexus map</a>","edited":{"userid":1079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1079-megapope","user":"Megapope","date":".  Dialled back outrage lol 06-25-2018, 01:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1079-megapope","name":"Megapope","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-24T23:09:35-07:00","id":1229942,"title":"","content":"Exalted 3e crafting system replacements are something of a dime a dozen. It depends on what you don't like about what is. Some want to chop down the Solar charm tree to like 10 charms, some want to eliminate what they see as 'grind' (integrating a crafter's story as a crafter into the narrative of the game so there's more than just &quot;I feel like making a legendary daiklave even though I haven't made so much as a butter knife on screen&quot;), etc. Have you determined what you don't like about the 3e system?<br /> <br /> V: and yeah, this. Craft in core is designed to reward the guy who looks at every situation and thinks, &quot;what can I make to make things better?&quot;. If the preference is &quot;I never craft unless I'm making something that is Big and Important and the Focus Of An Entire Story&quot; it won't work as well for you, so that's a thing to tackle. (I conceptually at least love the idea of it so, I never had a big issue with it)<br /> <br /> Anything that creates an emotional intimacy in someone, gets you a material benefit, or serves one of your intimacies, will get you craft XP. Without limit. Once you realize this, it goes from &quot;I have to make ten thousand stupid things to get anywhere&quot; to &quot;holy crap I am swimming in craft XP by making the world a better or worse place as befits my concept&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","user":"Meianno Yuurei","date":". 06-25-2018, 09:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-24T23:38:04-07:00","id":1229945,"title":"","content":"The crafting system's craft xp (silver, gold, white), and the project slots are the two chunkiest parts of the sub-system.<br /> <br /> The craft xp is designed to reward most someone who is always crafting (Always Be Crafting is the base line i tell anyone wanting supernal Craft). Have them think like an engineer or MacGuyver and they will make plenty of stuff and generate plenty of silver xp, and some gold to boot. Also keep in mind repair work counts too<br /> <br /> If you want a person crafting just to churn out Artifacts, then reconfigure how you see the Craft Xp. This is also the material you need to make the Artifact just as much as the creative output. So have the group go adventuring to gather half the needed xp in the form of the needed exotic component, or just buy one or two things via some bureaucracy maneuvers.<br /> <br /> Slots are not as bad, they are how well you can multitask multiple jobs. The bigger the slot, the more of your life it takes up. Making more than one Artifact level project at once seems extreme, but the rules are there for it just in case.<br /> <br /> Its a system built to look terrible in a white room scenario, but great for the MacGuyver crafter who punches rocks into statues and always leaves a town running smoother than they arrived. If you can reconfigure the big stuff into a slow progress bar in the background of your game when speedy and meaty chunks when in downtime. I think you'd be surprised with how well it runs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-25T03:01:03-07:00","id":1229959,"title":"","content":"I currently use the system mostly as written with the definition of basic projects <a href=\"https://ericminton.wordpress.com/2015/10/29/exalted-3e-sample-basic-projects/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> save for a few key differences.<br /> - Craft Xp is renamed Inspiration because that's less confusing and gives me an excuse to reward a bunch of it to a player who'd been struggling to get involved.<br /> - The existence of Project Slots is ignored until Superior and Legendary.<br /> <br /> Not a house rule as such, but my introduction/cheat sheet to the craft system only covers Basic and Major because Superior steps up the complexity a lot by introducing Goal Number and Terminus. My new player whose actually using the system is a logistics officer/field cook so actually wants to be MacGuyver or in her words &quot;beleaguered single mother to an entire army&quot; my system priorities would be very different if someone envisioned their character as a hermit living alone on a mountain who made awesome swords for the people who managed to find them.<br /> <br /> Also basically, this.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meianno Yuurei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1229942#post1229942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have you determined what you don't like about the 3e system?</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-25T06:36:30-07:00","id":1229999,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\"> Why Craft Sucks &amp; How I Fixed It - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> We've had a lot of arguments here recently about the new crafting system. I've been pretty firmly on the &amp;quot;it's bad&amp;quot; side. But I'm tired of unproductive </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/513-gatts","name":"Gatts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar513_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T16:50:22-07:00","id":1230398,"title":"","content":"I'll add my take to the list - as others have said, they're a dime a dozen. To distinguish the purpose of my rewrite from others with different goals:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">This version of the craft system is designed to be small, simple and easy to use. It does not attempt to regulate your play, or insert itself into any part of the game other than actually making things. It does not aim in incentivize certain types of player behavior, only to adjucate what characters are capable of.</div> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://github.com/BlueWinds/Ex3CharmRewrite/blob/master/Charms.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://github.com/BlueWinds/Ex3CharmRewrite/blob/master/Charms.pdf\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://github.com/BlueWinds/Ex3CharmRewrite/blob/master/Charms.pdf\" target=\"_blank\"> https://github.com/BlueWinds/Ex3CharmRewrite/blob/master/Charms.pdf </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> (Craft rules and rewritten charms are pg, 273)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9510-bluewinds","name":"BlueWinds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9510_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"554"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T16:52:39-07:00","id":1230399,"title":"","content":"I will Add Mine to the list as well. Also redid the Solar Teaching charms. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1223396-crafting-and-lore-charm-replacements-for-solars-peach\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...r-solars-peach</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1151-walkingeye","name":"WalkingEye","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1151_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"403"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T17:06:59-07:00","id":1230405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlueWinds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1230398#post1230398\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll add my take to the list - as others have said, they're a dime a dozen. To distinguish the purpose of my rewrite from others with different goals:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">This version of the craft system is designed to be small, simple and easy to use. It does not attempt to regulate your play, or insert itself into any part of the game other than actually making things. It does not aim in incentivize certain types of player behavior, only to adjucate what characters are capable of.</div></div> </div> </div> </div>I'd argue that a game mechanic utterly without incentivized behavior is nearly impossible to write.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T17:22:06-07:00","id":1230416,"title":"","content":"This isn't mine, it's just something I found online, but I've been using this one in my game: <a href=\"http://howsfamily.net/Exalted/wiki/index.php?title=Tasks#Craft\" target=\"_blank\">http://howsfamily.net/Exalted/wiki/i...le=Tasks#Craft</a><br /> <br /> It's built on the popular &quot;reskin the Sorcerous Workings system for Craft&quot; model. I had basically asked my players just not to take Craft at all because it's a headache, but then two insisted, so here we are. It mistakenly conflates &quot;First Age Artifice&quot; with &quot;Artifact N/A,&quot; but aside from that it seems fully functional. We haven't really done much with it so far in the game, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21733-ascension","name":"Ascension","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21733_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"105"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T17:44:14-07:00","id":1230419,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1229959#post1229959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I currently use the system mostly as written with the definition of basic projects <a href=\"https://ericminton.wordpress.com/2015/10/29/exalted-3e-sample-basic-projects/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> save for a few key differences.<br /> - Craft Xp is renamed Inspiration because that's less confusing and gives me an excuse to reward a bunch of it to a player who'd been struggling to get involved.<br /> - The existence of Project Slots is ignored until Superior and Legendary.</div> </div> </div> </div><i>Genius</i><br /> <br /> I will have to put that on the shelf for my next Chronicle as we have already been using the rules as is. But those are nice fixes <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar7_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"7\" data-vbnamecard=\"7\">Lioness</a>!<br /> <br /> <br /> My advice is to use the rules as is, then figure out what it is you dont like and fix it from there. I find that EX3 systems are very, um, well balanced against themselves. So when one seemingly small thing is changed because the system seems clunky and unusual, it breaks something in the overall system you did not expect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>May you live in interesting times...</i><br /> <br /> Storyteller of <b><a href=\"https://sunforgedoath.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Sun Forged Oath</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1998-keichiokami","name":"Keichiokami","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1998_4.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"303"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T04:56:55-07:00","id":1230496,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Keichiokami</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1230419#post1230419\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <i>Genius</i><br /> <br /> I will have to put that on the shelf for my next Chronicle as we have already been using the rules as is. But those are nice fixes <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar7_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"7\" data-vbnamecard=\"7\">Lioness</a>!</div> </div> </div> </div> Thank you. Inspiration was actually something we suggested during the playtest (admittedly in a less developed state) but unfortunately this was the part of the playtest when the leak occurred and after that point it felt like what we were saying wasn't being taken onboard (presumably because by then they were being inundated with feedback from people outside the original test group).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1230405#post1230405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd argue that a game mechanic utterly without incentivized behavior is nearly impossible to write.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's worth aiming for, even if I'd argue that the craft fix in question isn't that.<br /> <br /> <i>Which isn't a bad thing. </i>I really liked exotic ingredients back in 2e, I'd say its the solution for a group that managed to avoid 2nd edition's worst incentives and wants something familiar.<br /> I like it more than Sanctaphrax's which reintroduces Factory Cathedrals and 2e's problematic approach to assistants by turning them into a passive bonus.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T05:00:32-07:00","id":1230497,"title":"","content":"When you said you got rid of all but superior and legendary project slots, that's just major slots that got dumped, right? <br /> <br /> How did you handle the &quot;combine major into superior slots&quot; thing?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T05:29:29-07:00","id":1230505,"title":"","content":"As it's currently handled you create a Superior Project Slot at (artifact rating) Gold and +5 Silver for every dot past 3. This group has only had two sessions thus far so no artifacts yet.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T21:53:10-07:00","id":1230743,"title":"","content":"To add to the pile I guess, I generally use the base system (slots and all) since I actually do like the goals it has of incentivizing a certain approach to crafting and encouraging players to solve problems by making things along the way. I also use the same samples for basic projects.<br /> <br /> That being said, I think a big issue with Craft is more tied with Charms than with the mechanics. I think that there's for instance, more than enough points in normal games to get enough slots that there's just way too mcuh on that,a dn the Craft Charms actually in a way feel like they &quot;skip&quot; the incentives created by the base system. So the take here from other folks I repurposed is to create soemthing that kind of got over &quot;workhorse&quot; stuff earlier, and had more neat wizz-bang effects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/7r0q37m87o7zr9f/Solar%20Craft%20Charms%20Revised%20v5.pdf?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/7r0q37m87o7zr9f/Solar%20Craft%20Charms%20Revised%20v5.pdf?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/7r0q37m87o7zr9f/Solar%20Craft%20Charms%20Revised%20v5.pdf?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.dropbox.com/s/7r0q37m87o7zr9f/Solar%20Craft%20Charms%20Revised%20v5.pdf?dl=0 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Enjoy!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T08:37:11-07:00","id":1230829,"title":"","content":"I did my own take, which you can find in my signature. I didn't like that the system was unlikely to produce unexpected outcomes and didn't handle quality very well. I mostly kept slots and craft points, but I reduced the necessity to constantly make craft your solution to every problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T10:08:06-07:00","id":1230846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1230743#post1230743\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To add to the pile I guess, I generally use the base system (slots and all) since I actually do like the goals it has of incentivizing a certain approach to crafting and encouraging players to solve problems by making things along the way. I also use the same samples for basic projects.<br /> <br /> That being said, I think a big issue with Craft is more tied with Charms than with the mechanics. I think that there's for instance, more than enough points in normal games to get enough slots that there's just way too mcuh on that,a dn the Craft Charms actually in a way feel like they &quot;skip&quot; the incentives created by the base system.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do somewhat agree. I think the Craft system could be a bit simpler (I don't really see the point of silver XP, for example, I understand gating artefacts but why gate swords? And like Lioness I don't find major slots add anything; they've literally never come up), but generally, while at first I thought the Craft system was the issue, I realised it's 90% the charms.<br /> <br /> There's a lot of charms that do things that really aren't useful (mostly about slots), and the Crafting XP charms are mostly pretty poor until you get to the last one, which is ridiculously good.<br /> <br /> We were actually talking about the Craft system the other day at the end of a game, and one player said he hated it. I said that, despite its failings, it did do the one main thing it was designed to do well; that is, stop people churning out artefacts like a production line.<br /> He pointed out that my character has already made a level 2 artefact and a level 3 artefact, only about 4 games after I joined.<br /> This isn't due to the base system, though, it's my 13 craft charms. Which let me churn out artefacts if I have the materials, as well as raising a whole bunch of Craft abilities... but with a lot of book-keeping. <br /> When I was running the game, I eventually just told the Twilight player to not bother counting his Craft XP anymore, since it went up faster than he spent it, due to charms.<br /> <br /> This is not to say that the Craft system is perfect (I remember the first session I ran of 3rd ed, where I had to explain to the player of the 250xp Twilight with 7 craft abilities at 5 that he couldn't make himself a sword until he'd jumped through several hoops), but I suspect that it would work better with more even, better thought-out charms.<br /> <br /> On that note, has anyone tried a Dragonblood Crafter?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 06-28-2018, 01:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T11:14:05-07:00","id":1230869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1230846#post1230846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On that note, has anyone tried a Dragonblood Crafter?</div> </div> </div> </div>The new player I talked about plays a Fire Aspect. I've got a Water Aspect with craft dots but that game hasn't technically started yet and she's still on Basic Projects.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T11:30:28-07:00","id":1230874,"title":"","content":"How has the DB player found it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T12:25:33-07:00","id":1230892,"title":"","content":"What makes it tricky to assess is that she'd never played an RPG before so she's still a bit enamoured with stunting (she's been cooking with fires she makes from her own elemental bolts) and her first major project was to improvise an anti-venom for one of the mortal retainers who'd been bitten by a snake. She's spamming Passion-Inflaming Artistry like its going out of style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T17:50:50-07:00","id":1231273,"title":"","content":"Really, the two biggest issues in the Craft system itself are the objectives and the gating of Major projects behind Basic projects.<br /> <br /> The objective list encourages gaming the system. Crafters are incentivized to all take an Intimacy of &quot;I like making things&quot; because then they automatically score one objective on all projects, with almost no downside. They are incentivized to sell tons of simple things to score a second. This incentive structure pushes Craft heavy characters towards sameness.<br /> <br /> The gating of Major projects behind Basic projects just rubs some people the wrong way, partly because the system is telling people to solve problems with trinkets when they would prefer to use something more substantial and partly because the system is telling you that every problem you solve without using Craft is a missed opportunity to garner craft points.<br /> <br /> The issue with the Solar Craft Charms is that those Charms are balanced in a vacuum. They don't need to provide competitive options because they rarely compete against anything. They can deliver unexciting effects at various points because there isn't anything exciting to take.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T18:41:12-07:00","id":1231279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231273#post1231273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The objective list encourages gaming the system.</div> </div> </div> </div>The fact that we're playing a game in the first place encourages gaming the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231273#post1231273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The objective list encourages gaming the system. Crafters are incentivized to all take an Intimacy of &quot;I like making things&quot; because then they automatically score one objective on all projects, with almost no downside. They are incentivized to sell tons of simple things to score a second.</div> </div> </div> </div>It almost sounds like the system encourages players of crafters to make characters who love their craft and practice a trade.<br /> <br /> (Also, the downside to having a love of making things is pretty obvious; are you missing that having an Intimacy toward crafting gives people a handle by which to manipulate you into crafting stuff for them?)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 06-29-2018, 09:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T18:44:03-07:00","id":1231280,"title":"","content":"I really think the issue is how Basic and Major projects are defined more than the objectives or the gating.<br /> <br /> If Basic, Major, and mundane Superior projects were all defined by their level of impact on the game state (and be more consistent about batching) rather than simulating crafting effort - including a minimum level of impact for Basic projects - those problems would go away.<br /> <br /> A Basic project is one that will only have a temporary effect on the game for a single or small group of characters, but has to be significant enough to matter to the outcome of at least a scene. Your Melee specialist Dawn had their Diaklave stolen from them, and you make them a sword to use until they get it back is Basic rather than Major because it's not about the sword, but the impact.<br /> <br /> Major projects permanently change things for a single character/small group, or temporarily change things for a large group.<br /> <br /> Mundane Superior projects permanently change things for large groups, or temporarily change things that could impact multiple nations.<br /> <br /> This gets rid of &quot;farming&quot; small items with an Intimacy because they still need to impact the scene to matter enough to qualify even as Basic. Sameness is decreased because, again, it has to matter to what's going on, so it can only be as same-y as the scenes allow. It also reduces the &quot;My swords smith can't do anything because swords are always Major.&quot; thing because swords can be Basic as long as the impact is small.<br /> <br /> I think the objectives could use a revisit, but they're not ruining the system.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T19:31:54-07:00","id":1231285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231279#post1231279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The fact that we're playing a game in the first place encourages gaming the system.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's gaming with the system and there's gaming the system. Just because we have a system doesn't mean that what it encourages is what is desired behavior.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It almost sounds like the system encourages players of crafters to make characters who love their craft and practice a trade. </div> </div> </div>What's a word below &quot;forced&quot; and above &quot;encouraged&quot;? The Craft system tries to turn all crafters into a hammer in search of a nail. No other Ability consumes a character concept quite the way that Craft does. If you don't optimize out the busy work, the system either makes you buy Craft Charms to make the busy work go away or else says &quot;No, you can't make what you want. Go make something cheap by the dozen.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (Also, the downside to having a love of making things is pretty obvious; are you missing that having an Intimacy toward crafting gives people a handle by which to manipulate you into crafting stuff for them?) </div> </div> </div>All you need is a Minor Intimacy and nothing more. Besides which, the odds that you'll actually object to making something for someone is low. How often does Thanos roll up and want you to make him the Infinity Gauntlet?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T19:54:55-07:00","id":1231288,"title":"","content":"Thanos might not show up much... but Loki showed up plenty in my game with my Solar shipwright. Or rather more specifically, a loyal but politically ambitious NPC aiming to take over from the NPC we backed certainly liked to use my Intimacies around ship building to make him pleasure yachts instead of military vessels to make our ally look strong.<br /> <br /> These are not hard Intimacies to turn into RP fodder.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T21:48:50-07:00","id":1231298,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231285#post1231285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's gaming with the system and there's gaming the system. Just because we have a system doesn't mean that what it encourages is what is desired behavior.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you seriously believe the devs didn't desire players making crafter characters who engage with the craft system and enjoy their work, that the system just happened to do exactly that by accident?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231285#post1231285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Craft system tries to turn all crafters into a hammer in search of a nail.</div> </div> </div> </div>It only makes sense that a character with dots in an Ability <i>might want to find reason to use that Ability</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231285#post1231285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No other Ability consumes a character concept quite the way that Craft does.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll have you know that Spider is a loremaster first, then a sorcerer, then a swordsman, <i>then</i> a crafter. All he's got in Craft is up to the Durability-Enhancing Technique, but his Lore goes all the way to Truth-Rendering Gaze.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All you need is a Minor Intimacy and nothing more. </div> </div> </div>&quot;A Minor Intimacy and nothing more&quot; is plenty handle enough for a Persuade.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How often does Thanos roll up and want you to make him the Infinity Gauntlet? </div> </div> </div>And after him, Thor. And before him, Loki, and before him, Odin, and before him\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-29T22:42:44-07:00","id":1231303,"title":"","content":"It also never really helps these conversations when how little crafting you actually have to do to get to Major projects even as a mortal.<br /> <br /> If you want to make a Major project once or twice a Story, have one Craft at 5, do one Basic that either hits two objectives and you roll well over the difficulty for 6 sxp, or hit all thre objects for 6 sxp, and you start the next Story with 11 sxp. Enough to do a Major project!<br /> <br /> Craft is only as consuming as you want it to be. Again, mortals, no Charms, it's exceedingly easy to be Crafting at a 2 Basic, to 1 Major, and an occasional Superior something or other with those gxps you're getting from your Majors since you don't have Charms to use it for if you weren't going to. The only reason to be in a constant always-be-crafting mindset where you have to solve everything you can with Craft is if you're playing a character that's consumed by it in the first place. Otherwise you're going to be sitting on a giant pile of cxp that goes unused.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T03:36:35-07:00","id":1231326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231285#post1231285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's a word below &quot;forced&quot; and above &quot;encouraged&quot;? The Craft system tries to turn all crafters into a hammer in search of a nail. No other Ability consumes a character concept quite the way that Craft does. If you don't optimize out the busy work, the system either makes you buy Craft Charms to make the busy work go away or else says &quot;No, you can't make what you want. Go make something cheap by the dozen.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I tend to use &quot;heavily incentivized&quot;. Like, sure, you don't <i>have </i>to follow the specific archetype, just like how you didn't <i>have </i>to be a ragebeast or a pretty butterfly in 2E Lunars. It's just going to be a lot more uphill.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1793-drascin","name":"Drascin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1793_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"400"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T05:21:08-07:00","id":1231340,"title":"","content":"Is anything new or worthwhile going to come out of another go on the Merry-go-round of the craft system argument? <br /> <br /> I don't really think it would be appropriate in this thread in any case, but even if it was spun off into a dedicated one?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T05:51:19-07:00","id":1231343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231280#post1231280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Basic project is one that will only have a temporary effect on the game for a single or small group of characters, but has to be significant enough to matter to the outcome of at least a scene.</div> </div> </div> </div> It's interesting but right now I'm not sure what counts as temporary or not. Is making a meal to ingratiate ones self to a reoccurring NPC temporary because it's food that gets eaten? Is it permanent because it's helping to create an intimacy that will probably be there for the duration?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T07:48:44-07:00","id":1231355,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231298#post1231298\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you seriously believe the devs didn't desire players making crafter characters who engage with the craft system and enjoy their work, that the system just happened to do exactly that by accident?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the Devs tried to make a system that got craft characters to enjoy their work and ended up with a system that produced characters cast from the same mold with an obsession with their work. They got what they wanted and more than they bargained for.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It only makes sense that a character with dots in an Ability <i>might want to find reason to use that Ability</i>. </div> </div> </div>A character with Melee 5 who doesn't kill a rat in each scene is not losing a future opportunity to kill a human. A character with Craft 5 who doesn't make a trinket in each scene is losing a future opportunity to make a non-trinket. Maybe he's already got enough non-trinkets crafts saved up so he doesn't care much, but that doesn't mean that's not what he's letting go by.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'll have you know that Spider is a loremaster first, then a sorcerer, then a swordsman, <i>then</i> a crafter. All he's got in Craft is up to the Durability-Enhancing Technique, but his Lore goes all the way to Truth-Rendering Gaze. </div> </div> </div>It's nice to know that your character with no serious interest in making artifacts managed to avoid getting caught in the incentive structure. *golf clap*<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;A Minor Intimacy and nothing more&quot; is plenty handle enough for a Persuade. </div> </div> </div>and no real trouble to shut down with a Major Intimacy you have towards your goal in the current plot.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231303#post1231303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only reason to be in a constant always-be-crafting mindset where you have to solve everything you can with Craft is if you're playing a character that's consumed by it in the first place. Otherwise you're going to be sitting on a giant pile of cxp that goes unused.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm aware of the ratios involved. The trouble is that it's hard to predict the future. How many times this story are you going to want to make something better than a trinket? You need a buffer. How many artifacts are you going to want to make this story? You probably have a better idea, but how big of a buffer you want there depends on how much you are trying to avoid sinking XP into the Craft Charms for getting more points. Basically, the more the future is uncertain, the more the Skinner box that is the craft system wants you to mash the &quot;solve all problems with Craft&quot; button and that means finding ways to try to do something positive with a trinket because you don't want to tap your buffer of real craft projects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231340#post1231340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is anything new or worthwhile going to come out of another go on the Merry-go-round of the craft system argument?</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends. Is anyone new watching the merry-go-round? Have any of the principle advocates of each side had any new epiphanies in how to explain their perspective to others? Is anyone trying to make their own system to try and resolve the objections of both sides? How many people take a perverse pleasure from the argument? Lots of variables involved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T08:15:19-07:00","id":1231361,"title":"","content":"The concept is a work in progress.<br /> <br /> Ideally, the assignment of &quot;temporary&quot; would be in terms of impact on the story rather than strictly mechanical, but I recognize that there is still a pretty blurry area there.<br /> <br /> My instinct would be to fall back to what the player intends. Putting more decision weight on what level of impact that player wants to have on the story helps reduce the feeling of the base line mechanics as &quot;restrictive.&quot; Is the player making a meal to get on the NPCs good side, but expecting this to be the opening for the circle to convert that NPC to their side, or is the player trying to knock the NPC's socks off so hard that they're forever fond of the PCs?<br /> <br /> If I was writing this up more fully, I'd certainly go more into story and frame of reference. What's a temporary change from the perspective of the story the PCs are in, could be a life changing permanent change if we were telling some random NPC's story instead. I mean, to go back to my own example, if you make a temporary replacement sword for your Dawn, it's not really all that temporary for any guards that get sliced to bits with that sword.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T08:24:03-07:00","id":1231366,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character with Melee 5 who doesn't kill a rat in each scene is not losing a future opportunity to kill a human.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is it news to anyone here that one thing is not another, different thing?<br /> <br /> But we've said that before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character with Melee 5 who doesn't kill a rat in each scene is not losing a future opportunity to kill a human.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, though, a Melee 5 master swordsman who doesn't take the opportunity to beat up mooks may indeed find himself losing the opportunity to kill a larger enemy\u2026 because he hasn't built up enough Initiative.<br /> <br /> But we've said that before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's nice to know that your character with no serious interest in making artifacts managed to avoid getting caught in the incentive structure. *golf clap*</div> </div> </div> </div>Aside from just being irritatingly smarmy, what you seem to be trying to do here is establish a false dichotomy. If I am putting absolutely anything ahead of Craft, I have &quot;no serious interest in making artifacts.&quot; I'm guessing that if I had mentioned my character's enchanted tapestry and magic carpet, then I'd be &quot;caught in the incentive structure,&quot; that it &quot;consumed my character concept,&quot; in a way that <i>only</i> Craft can. <br /> <br /> In spite of Spider being all those other things I mentioned first and foremost.<br /> <br /> But we've said that before. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have any of the principle advocates of each side had any new epiphanies in how to explain their perspective to others?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, like I've said, I'm playing a character who engages with the Craft system now, but you seldom seem to accept anecdotal evidence when it doesn't support your point.<br /> <br /> But we've said that before, too. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lots of variables involved.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nah, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> was right. I don't particularly feel like doing this dance with you again.<br /> <br /> But then again, I've said <i>that</i> before, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 06-30-2018, 11:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T09:17:45-07:00","id":1231381,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231355#post1231355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Devs tried to make a system that got craft characters to enjoy their work and ended up with a system that produced characters cast from the same mold with an obsession with their work.</div> </div> </div> </div>This complaint is always really hard to accept though. The system generally expresses being good with an Ability the same way despite the descriptive differences between characters. Melee characters despite any favored weapon load out, are going to be incentivized towards specific things to be good at Melee since that's measure primarily in combat which incentivizes specific mechanics to engage with. All Melee characters are going to be &quot;obsessed&quot; with their &quot;work&quot; of managing tactical advantage over their opponent(s); aka get as much Initiative as possible.<br /> <br /> I get it. Craft (and not just Craft) feels less seamless, and it isn't interactive with other characters, and all that. But can you really honestly say that beyond subjective feel, any Ability in the game - largely due to the level of abstraction and all - isn't essentially the same in terms of mechanics and incentives regardless of character details? I'm not talking about Charms that add a lot of potential variety until high XP sets in. I mean, mundane Medicine, Survival, Larceny, etc. If you want to get the most out of those... it's pretty much always going to look the same mechanically, which impacts how it'll look in play to avoid a disconnect of mechanics and descriptions.<br /> <br /> The incentives for different Abilities are structured differently based on what (if any) subsystems they interact with. A swordsman doesn't need to stab rats to stab humans and all that dismissive language, but the swordsman needs to follow the incentives of the combat system so that they win fights and don't lose future opportunities to win fights via getting crippled/killed/etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's nice to know that your character with no serious interest in making artifacts managed to avoid getting caught in the incentive structure. </div> </div> </div>But that's the whole point... what you're saying is an &quot;trap&quot; of incentives that forces characters into being samey isn't because if you're not making a character that's aiming for being obsessed with the master of the most difficult expressions of crafting... they don't need to be worried about being obsessed with Crafting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How many times this story are you going to want to make something better than a trinket? </div> </div> </div>Can you appreciate the confusion that comes from an argument that is effectively, &quot;I don't like the system for incentivizing me to try to solve as many problems with Craft as possible, because I want to solve problems with Craft.&quot; If you desire is a character that doesn't get obsessed with solving everything with Craft, why do you need to worry about a buffer? If you can't Craft yourself out of a situation (or want to save the cxp for later) then you get to use whatever other tools you've invested in instead. Isn't that what you want when you say you don't want obsessed MacGuyvers? You only need to worry about buffers if you're worried about crafting your way out of anything that happens. If you don't worry about buffers... you craft as you want to prioritize instead of worrying about &quot;mashing the Skinner box.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T12:29:14-07:00","id":1231413,"title":"","content":"I regret this thread and am currently performing penance at a temple.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229925-hello-i-did-up-a-scavenger-lands-map-based-on-3e\" target=\"_blank\">3e Scavenger Lands map</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/a/NQewCv0\" target=\"_blank\">3e Combat tracker card</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/uGrZk2r\" target=\"_blank\">2e Nexus map</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1079-megapope","name":"Megapope","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-30T19:15:33-07:00","id":1231501,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231340#post1231340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is anything new or worthwhile going to come out of another go on the Merry-go-round of the craft system argument?<br /> <br /> I don't really think it would be appropriate in this thread in any case, but even if it was spun off into a dedicated one?</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. While I think some discussion of the system and its incentives is on-point when we're talking about alternatives its got the potential to spiral off further into the particulars of Solar Craft. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T05:33:56-07:00","id":1231559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231381#post1231381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This complaint is always really hard to accept though. The system generally expresses being good with an Ability the same way despite the descriptive differences between characters. Melee characters despite any favored weapon load out, are going to be incentivized towards specific things to be good at Melee since that's measure primarily in combat which incentivizes specific mechanics to engage with. All Melee characters are going to be &quot;obsessed&quot; with their &quot;work&quot; of managing tactical advantage over their opponent(s); aka get as much Initiative as possible.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is that Initiative is gone at the end of the scene. It doesn't carry over. There is no reason to try to gain as much Initiative as possible in this scene so that you can have lots available for the next time you fight.<br /> <br /> Because of that, the Melee character doesn't need to bring fighting into a social scene or an exploration scene or what have you. He's certainly ready and able to use his sword if that seems like the most fitting option for his personality, but he's not losing anything.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I get it. Craft (and not just Craft) feels less seamless, and it isn't interactive with other characters, and all that. But can you really honestly say that beyond subjective feel, any Ability in the game - largely due to the level of abstraction and all - isn't essentially the same in terms of mechanics and incentives regardless of character details? I'm not talking about Charms that add a lot of potential variety until high XP sets in. I mean, mundane Medicine, Survival, Larceny, etc. If you want to get the most out of those... it's pretty much always going to look the same mechanically, which impacts how it'll look in play to avoid a disconnect of mechanics and descriptions. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure I understand what you are trying to get at here. Other than the five dot rating scale, most Abilities are mechanically different, both in what they can accomplish and how that incentivizes the character to act in play. Medicine isn't an Ability you can just use - you need a sick person to use it on. Larceny you can generally find a use for it wherever, but because money is abstracted, picking pockets for anything trivial isn't something most characters will spend their screen time doing and so what if you walk around in a disguise? Unless you are a wanted man or impersonating someone, the rolls are superfluous.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But that's the whole point... what you're saying is an &quot;trap&quot; of incentives that forces characters into being samey isn't because if you're not making a character that's aiming for being obsessed with the master of the most difficult expressions of crafting... they don't need to be worried about being obsessed with Crafting. </div> </div> </div>If you want your character to make artifacts on a regular basis, you have to play the game the Craft system sets out. If your character doesn't care about artifacts, then you can basically chuck the whole Craft system, as the system exists pretty much exclusively to set the rate at which you can produce artifacts. You don't need to be particularly obsessed with making artifacts to find yourself trying to shoehorn Craft into every situation to score those extra objectives and get the 60 gold points you'll need if you plan to finish an Artifact 3 on just the Craft Excellency.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Can you appreciate the confusion that comes from an argument that is effectively, &quot;I don't like the system for incentivizing me to try to solve as many problems with Craft as possible, because I want to solve problems with Craft.&quot; If you desire is a character that doesn't get obsessed with solving everything with Craft, why do you need to worry about a buffer? If you can't Craft yourself out of a situation (or want to save the cxp for later) then you get to use whatever other tools you've invested in instead. Isn't that what you want when you say you don't want obsessed MacGuyvers? You only need to worry about buffers if you're worried about crafting your way out of anything that happens. If you don't worry about buffers... you craft as you want to prioritize instead of worrying about &quot;mashing the Skinner box.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Are you familiar with the concept of &quot;cost of entry&quot;? Part of the Craft problem is that there's a cost you have to pay before you can solve a problem with Craft if the solution is not a Basic Project. Let me try to demonstrate with an analogy. You have a character who has a gun. The gun is not the sum total of your whole character - you have other things you want to do with your character. However, in order to shoot your gun you need bullets and to get a bullet you have to spend time in a scene making that bullet. How many bullets does your character need to have? Well, that depends on how much shooting you plan to do in the future, but of course you also have to have some spares for when you do unexpected shooting. You can't do the shooting first and then make the bullets - you need the bullets first. So, you have to keep a stockpile, so you'll put in extra scenes where you make bullets so you can have your gun ready to go when you need it. How many scenes do you think this character is going to spend making bullets, especially if he starts play with none?<br /> <br /> This is the Craft problem. If you want to do [significant thing] with Craft when it matters, you have to have done [insignificant thing] previously. How many times do you want to do [significant thing]? Well, make sure you've done at least that many [insignificant thing] or you are SOL. Your Intimacies on paper aren't going to match up with your behavior in play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T07:44:12-07:00","id":1231581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231559#post1231559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing is that Initiative is gone at the end of the scene. It doesn't carry over.</div> </div> </div> </div>Damage, however does. If you don't control Initiative as much as possible, there are consequences that can persist into the next scene (or worse).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because of that, the Melee character doesn't need to bring fighting into a social scene or an exploration scene or what have you. He's certainly ready and able to use his sword if that seems like the most fitting option for his personality, but he's not losing anything. </div> </div> </div>Of course the combat Abilities are inherently limited in that way. Bringing a fight into a non-fight situation is generally disruptive. Crafting can be additive to almost any situation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not sure I understand what you are trying to get at here. </div> </div> </div>I mean the 'samey' argument about how Crafts &quot;pushes&quot; characters into doing things the same way doesn't really work because every Ability does it; even if differently. Every archer has the same subsystems to interact with their Archery that pushes them towards the same kinds of actions. Every magistrate has the same subsystems that push them in specific directions as investigators.<br /> <br /> Craft isn't special in this sense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you want your character to make artifacts on a regular basis, you have to play the game the Craft system sets out. </div> </div> </div>Definite 'regular.' That's part of the issue here. If regular is, &quot;once or twice a story,&quot; getting enough gxp without Charms is probably not going to require any specific &quot;obsession&quot; with Crafting beyond wanting to put your Craft to use instead of it sitting there on the sheet. With Charms you'll have the gxp with ease.<br /> <br /> If you want to be crafting an Artifact every session... well you need to Craft more yeah. But that's why I mean by you're already making a Craft obsessed character at that point, and the system is just regulating how to express that like it does with any other subsystem focused Ability.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If your character doesn't care about artifacts, then you can basically chuck the whole Craft system, as the system exists pretty much exclusively to set the rate at which you can produce artifacts. </div> </div> </div>Or you work on Major projects that tend to take a lot of down time and slots are there to say how many of those you can have going at once, and cxp helps dictate how many slots you can have. Or your Craft includes mundane Superior projects. Or you want to do some Manse building.<br /> <br /> Again, my primary player experience here is as a shipwright... which is a Superior project focused thing (even though there's a rather large number of Basic and Major ones that come up more frequently), despite not being an Artifact focused thing. At a rate of around one to two Basics per scene, and around one Major per session, and the rate of time passing in-game.... it was pretty much directly on point for making navy ships at a reasonable pace without any magic speeding up how fast you could build one, and I ended up with a 'buffer' just because not a lot of orders for Major level ships when there's a war on and all that.<br /> <br /> I'm sure that's not everyone's experience, but what you're saying is the inevitable outcome simply isn't, which is the push back you're getting here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Part of the Craft problem is that there's a cost you have to pay before you can solve a problem with Craft if the solution is not a Basic Project. </div> </div> </div>You say problem, I say incentive doing its job. You can't win a significant fight without making some withering attacks to feed into your decisive attacks, and without some defenses to keep your initiative protected. You can't out sail someone without playing out the momentum game. Etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let me try to demonstrate with an analogy. You have a character who has a gun. </div> </div> </div>Doesn't really work because ammunition just doesn't compare to cxp. .22 wadcutter, 9mm, and .50 anti-vehicle rounds don't really imply different levels of mechanics for how many you bring with you, while cooking yourself breakfast, making a full suit of armor, and crafting a sword of legend with magical powers that it grants to those that wield, all imply things that should have different representations in the mechanics to represent the vastly different assumptions about effort and in-game impact.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is the Craft problem. If you want to do [significant thing] with Craft when it matters, you have to have done [insignificant thing] previously. How many times do you want to do [significant thing]? Well, make sure you've done at least that many [insignificant thing] or you are SOL. Your Intimacies on paper aren't going to match up with your behavior in play. </div> </div> </div>I generally find this to be an Internet argument problem were &quot;Basic&quot; = &quot;Insignificant&quot; when hitting objectives means however little effort some of them might be to do, they had to be significant on some level.<br /> <br /> Again, I already agreed the objectives and project definitions could use work. Making it clear that a &quot;Making Horseshoes - Love&quot; doesn't work for hitting an objective if you make a 100 horseshoes and toss them all out because there's no horses to shoe, would help reinforce the Basic is Still Significant thing. But in these discussions the Basic means Insignificant thing is just unhelpful and borders on dishonest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T08:54:37-07:00","id":1231592,"title":"","content":"Oh, another thing I should mention.<br /> <br /> On my cheat sheets I drew particular attention to the +1 difficulty for using your craft ability for edge cases because I think that's something a lot of people missed. So my Fire Aspect player knew in advance that making medicine wasn't beyond her cooking ability and has openly mused about the possibility of making an earth oven into a kiln.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T09:59:10-07:00","id":1231603,"title":"","content":"Has there been an actual play where they showcase the awsomess of crafting?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12483-ale137","name":"ale137","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T10:50:48-07:00","id":1231609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231581#post1231581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Damage, however does. If you don't control Initiative as much as possible, there are consequences that can persist into the next scene (or worse).</div> </div> </div> </div>But damage is not a good equivalent to craft XP. You get damage after you fail to defend yourself, not because you try to accumulate damage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean the 'samey' argument about how Crafts &quot;pushes&quot; characters into doing things the same way doesn't really work because every Ability does it; even if differently. Every archer has the same subsystems to interact with their Archery that pushes them towards the same kinds of actions. Every magistrate has the same subsystems that push them in specific directions as investigators.<br /> <br /> Craft isn't special in this sense. </div> </div> </div>It's not that every character is samey because they operate using the same mechanics, it's that craft characters end up dropping other Abilities in favor of running with the Craft game. When you choose to use Craft, you are giving up an opportunity to do something else. The Craft system encourages you to try to replace Survival with Craft, Presence with Craft, Medicine with Craft, etc. That's what generates the sameness. It's a bit like the 1st Edition Lunar problem, where there were only so many good Charms and so every Lunar ended up looking mechanically identical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> With Charms you'll have the gxp with ease. </div> </div> </div>This ends up sounding to my ears like &quot;Pay some XP to make the system go away&quot;. Do you see where that would be an indicator that the system is sub-par to me?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Definite 'regular.' That's part of the issue here. If regular is, &quot;once or twice a story,&quot; getting enough gxp without Charms is probably not going to require any specific &quot;obsession&quot; with Crafting beyond wanting to put your Craft to use instead of it sitting there on the sheet.<br /> <br /> If you want to be crafting an Artifact every session... well you need to Craft more yeah. But that's why I mean by you're already making a Craft obsessed character at that point, and the system is just regulating how to express that like it does with any other subsystem focused Ability. </div> </div> </div>Point. Mentally, I think of &quot;regularly&quot; more along the lines of the first instead of the second, but sixty gold points are something like five major projects if you manage to nail every objective, with seven being more likely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or you work on Major projects that tend to take a lot of down time and slots are there to say how many of those you can have going at once, and cxp helps dictate how many slots you can have. Or your Craft includes mundane Superior projects. Or you want to do some Manse building. </div> </div> </div>Craftsman Needs No Tools says &quot;what down time&quot;? to almost every mundane project and even then most major projects are hours to days instead of weeks. Mundane superior projects are limited almost exclusively to architecture and shipbuilding. Manse building is artifact building with a different prerequisite skill.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, my primary player experience here is as a shipwright... which is a Superior project focused thing (even though there's a rather large number of Basic and Major ones that come up more frequently), despite not being an Artifact focused thing. At a rate of around one to two Basics per scene, and around one Major per session, and the rate of time passing in-game.... it was pretty much directly on point for making navy ships at a reasonable pace without any magic speeding up how fast you could build one, and I ended up with a 'buffer' just because not a lot of orders for Major level ships when there's a war on and all that.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm sure that's not everyone's experience, but what you're saying is the inevitable outcome simply isn't, which is the push back you're getting here. </div> </div> </div>This sounds like exactly the sort of behavior I don't want my characters sucked into. Craft stuffed into every scene, possibly multiple times.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You say problem, I say incentive doing its job. You can't win a significant fight without making some withering attacks to feed into your decisive attacks, and without some defenses to keep your initiative protected. You can't out sail someone without playing out the momentum game. Etc. </div> </div> </div>You cite abstractions that serve to facilitate the sort of narrative structure and versimilitude that Exalted games aim for in defense of a versimilitude-breaking abstraction. I've got the metal, I've got the Craft dots. I may even have enough Craft magic that I need a bucket to roll my dice pool. Why can't I make a sword until I've made a horseshoe?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Doesn't really work because ammunition just doesn't compare to cxp. .22 wadcutter, 9mm, and .50 anti-vehicle rounds don't really imply different levels of mechanics for how many you bring with you, while cooking yourself breakfast, making a full suit of armor, and crafting a sword of legend with magical powers that it grants to those that wield, all imply things that should have different representations in the mechanics to represent the vastly different assumptions about effort and in-game impact. </div> </div> </div>You are reading far too much into the analogy. Pick any type of craft XP. If you want to do X task, you need your bullets up front. That means doing other tasks ahead of time, probably when you'd also like to do other things, so you can have the option on tap instead of missing your chance. This is especially true if you aren't taking a generic &quot;I like making things&quot; Intimacy and aren't hawking your trinkets for a couple coppers at every pawn shop you pass i.e. trying to give the objectives meaning instead of making them just a checklist.<br /> <br /> For artifacts, I can see why the grind is necessary to prevent the game from unbalancing. For major projects, whose results can generally be acquired by anyone with Resources 3, that grind feels intrusive.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I generally find this to be an Internet argument problem were &quot;Basic&quot; = &quot;Insignificant&quot; when hitting objectives means however little effort some of them might be to do, they had to be significant on some level.<br /> <br /> Again, I already agreed the objectives and project definitions could use work. Making it clear that a &quot;Making Horseshoes - Love&quot; doesn't work for hitting an objective if you make a 100 horseshoes and toss them all out because there's no horses to shoe, would help reinforce the Basic is Still Significant thing. But in these discussions the Basic means Insignificant thing is just unhelpful and borders on dishonest. </div> </div> </div>What you are complaining about here is a structural problem with the discourse. If I use some specific examples, like making a sword and making horseshoes, then we'll be endlessly quibbling about things like &quot;well, couldn't you have made arrows? How about pot helms?&quot; and get lost in alternate craft projects that all amount to &quot;stuff I didn't want to do with my character's screen time&quot;. So I go to the core descriptions - the parts that matter regardless of the situation. There's the grind and the payoff. I want to avoid the grind when it doesn't matter to game balance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T16:22:53-07:00","id":1231677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231609#post1231609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But damage is not a good equivalent to craft XP. You get damage after you fail to defend yourself, not because you try to accumulate damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Damage isn't the equivalent of cxp, the stuff you spend cxp to make is.<br /> <br /> The comparison is Join Battle/Withering = cxp, and decisive/damage = making something big.<br /> <br /> In both models you have to do stuff besides your end goal before you can accomplish your end goal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not that every character is samey because they operate using the same mechanics, it's that craft characters end up dropping other Abilities in favor of running with the Craft game. </div> </div> </div>I've never seen this actually happen, just asserted it happens by people theory-crafting. The two posters here citing their actual characters, and actual play, are doing so in contrast of your assertion here.<br /> <br /> My experience is &quot;Oh, I'm going to craft a thing to help whatever the core goal of the scene (cook a meal to build up morale of troops, make a an intricately designed knife as a gift to impress someone, sew disguises for characters to make seeking into a gala easier, etc).&quot; with a bit of reactive stuff, &quot;we weren't expecting to capture an enemy, I'll whip up some restraints since they're too strong for the ropes we have.&quot; I've never seen. &quot;I know we're in the middle of a fight, but I'm going to cook. I know we're trying to talk an NPC into siding with our political puppet to control the kingdom, but I'm going to go work on a sword. Etc.&quot;<br /> <br /> Players want to contribute to the scene, not be bored off doing something unrelated. And they want to use their main abilities on a relatively frequent basis. A player that makes a crafting focused characters wants craft stuff, but the system does nothing to cause them to suddenly craft exclusively regardless of what else is going on, because the system lets you have more than enough dots to be good at more than one Ability.<br /> <br /> My shipwright was also our best Sail PC... so guess who didn't drop Sail to craft when we were sailing through dangerous waters?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you choose to use Craft, you are giving up an opportunity to do something else. </div> </div> </div>Not any more than using any other Ability since there's a limit to how much you can be doing at once. But cooking a fancy meal yourself to boost your Socialize and Presence efforts once the party starts are not mutually exclusive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Craft system encourages you to try to replace Survival with Craft, Presence with Craft, Medicine with Craft, etc. </div> </div> </div>Except it doesn't, because it can't. Being able to build tools doesn't confer the skill to use them. Being able to make a harness doesn't mean you have the skill to tame a horse. Being able to design the latest fashion doesn't mean you have the social skills to persuade people. Being able to make medicines doesn't mean you know to diagnose illnesses to know which ones to give a patient.<br /> <br /> The Craft system encourages you to enhance the other Abilities, not replace them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This ends up sounding to my ears like &quot;Pay some XP to make the system go away&quot;. Do you see where that would be an indicator that the system is sub-par to me? </div> </div> </div>Oh, I agree the Solar Craft Charms are sub-par. The &quot;get a ton of cxp for buying this Charm and doing nothing,&quot; Charms are definitely &quot;Pay some XP to make the system go away,&quot; and I dislike those highly. Charms that do things like increase the rewards for successfully finishing projects aren't paying to make the system go away though, but still end up making it much easier to have the cxp you want to do stuff.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Point. Mentally, I think of &quot;regularly&quot; more along the lines of the first instead of the second, but sixty gold points are something like five major projects if you manage to nail every objective, with seven being more likely. </div> </div> </div>Right, so... 5-7 Major projects over 3-5 sessions or so? This does not require the, &quot;Everything I do is Craft!&quot; mentality you're claiming it does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Craftsman Needs No Tools says &quot;what down time&quot;? </div> </div> </div>Don't care, because I'm talking about the base system, not how good or bad the Solar Charms are with this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mundane superior projects are limited almost exclusively to architecture and shipbuilding. </div> </div> </div>No, they aren't. Those are the two examples the book cites. Any large scale mundane project is a Superior one. The examples are just there to try to give STs and players an idea of the line between Major and Superior mundane projects even if it could certainly use more discussion than it got.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Manse building is artifact building with a different prerequisite skill. </div> </div> </div>Mechanically, mostly, but all in all, the point stands that your assertion is inaccurate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This sounds like exactly the sort of behavior I don't want my characters sucked into. Craft stuffed into every scene, possibly multiple times. </div> </div> </div>So by this logic, the combat system is absolutely the worst. If rolling Craft at most five times in one session is a ruinous amount of rolling by just one player... the amount of times you roll your primary combat Ability in a decent fight multiplied by all the players... yikes.<br /> <br /> I just can't understand how rolling one or two Craft rolls in a scene is some crime against play for what was my character's <u>primary</u> Ability. No details on what I was doing or why. You just clearly assume that if a player isn't rolling to make an Artifact, they're just rolling some bullshit action. Your argument is disingenuous at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You cite abstractions that serve to facilitate the sort of narrative structure and versimilitude that Exalted games aim for in defense of a versimilitude-breaking abstraction. </div> </div> </div>I don't agree with the idea that crafting is a verisimilitude breaking abstraction. I think most of the arguments against it are by people that don't actually want it to be verisimilitudinous; because actually making mortal crafting verisimilitudinous to Exalted's setting means dealing with the reality of ancient crafting techniques instead of just being able to shit out a one-in-a-lifetime work every day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've got the metal, I've got the Craft dots. I may even have enough Craft magic that I need a bucket to roll my dice pool. Why can't I make a sword until I've made a horseshoe? </div> </div> </div>Have you actually paid attention to what I've said in this thread, or just asking questions that I've already answered because you need to accumulate &quot;debate xp&quot; for some bigger post?<br /> <br /> Though in terms of versimilutude? Bladesmiths in a setting like Exalts can't afford to spend their whole lives making swords. They start with simpler projects to learn the basics of metallurgy, edge geometries, handle construction, etc. They're make hundreds of knives before they'll have the skills to make a sword just because working knives lets them learn on something they could actually accomplish. There isn't a store to go buy tools, so they're also going to be making lots of their own tools. Then they need to make a living, and most people in Creation can't afford a sword, so they'll keep making knives to sell to afford food, bartering for other services, supplies, etc.<br /> <br /> The system is actually being nice by letting you make a horse shoe or two in the middle of things to push past all that and make a sword, instead of having your roll for a few hundred knives to make a giant stockpile of sxp before you make swords even if it means you'll be able to make a lot of swords without going back to as much knife making if you're careful with your stockpile. But that's how Exalted should roll: you should be able to do things easier than reality, even if the results are sometimes a bit counter intuitive if you think too hard about it, or get wrapped up in a narrative that doesn't work with the system instead of with it.<br /> <br /> This might be some real world experience on my part, but I know a lot of people that do traditional craft work of multiple kinds; including blacksmiths. Doing things they way they do things even with modern tools? That would be mind-numbing RP. It's why most systems push crafting out of play, making it annoying to be a craft-focused character since you can only do stuff out of the spotlight. The only way to keep the verisimilitude of all the small bits of work craftspeople actually have to do when they're not magic wielding demigods, and make Craft something that can be actively used in games instead of a downtime only thing, is to incentivize small quick projects down in the moment rather than doing upfront as part of some apprenticeship system.<br /> <br /> Your character always had to make a horseshoe before they could make a sword. The 3e crafting system just makes it something that matters instead of something handwaved away.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You are reading far too much into the analogy. </div> </div> </div>Or it's not a great analogy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For major projects, whose results can generally be acquired by anyone with Resources 3, that grind feels intrusive. </div> </div> </div>It's not the game's fault if you insist on a clip of 60 sxp before you'll finish a Major project, and thus felt the need to grind to keep your sxp pile that big, out of fear that you might not be able to craft you way out of every problem you face - despite you saying you don't want to craft your way out of every problem - instead of just dealing with either having to see if a Basic or two in the moment would make sense (and it's painfully easy to come up with ones that make sense and add to the story instead of just being dumb grind nonsense), or if you really can't come up with a good Basic just using something else to solve the problem.<br /> <br /> Your stance is so freaking inconsistent on this. You don't want people to craft all the time, every time, for everything.... but you also don't like the system because if you craft too much you might not be able to keep crafting your way out of things.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What you are complaining about here is a structural problem with the discourse. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but not the one you want it to be. My structural problem with the discourse is that you don't allow your opinions and assertions to be treated as anything but unassailable facts even when there's evidence to the contrary. There is a complete imbalance in the structure of benefit of the doubt, and good faith.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I use some specific examples,.... </div> </div> </div>But you don't really use specific examples. You throw out tortured hypothetical situations, and label attempts to present more realistic examples as distractions. And then when that invariably takes its course of actual examples not following your theoretical frame work and thus challenging your &quot;facts&quot; about the system, it turns into quibbling instead of you acknowledge your theory of the problems with the system needing refinement.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's the grind and the payoff. I want to avoid the grind when it doesn't matter to game balance. </div> </div> </div>See, it's not about verisimilitude at all, crafting shit is grindy as hell to actually do. You do that whole &quot;we just endlessly quibble about different projects you can do,&quot; (which is really, &quot;you could do a project that makes sense instead of something you find stupid,&quot;), but you're shifting the goalpost around far more.<br /> <br /> Then there's the going back to the circular, &quot;calling having to do a Basic or two a grind is hyperbolic,&quot; &quot;It doesn't matter anything other than I can craft what I want when I want or it's grinding,&quot; stuff.<br /> <br /> Plus, of course, it's not all about &quot;game balance.&quot; It's also about enabling people to have fun with the rules. Some people want to play MacGuyver, not Artificer, and they should have a system that not just lets them do it, but rewards them for it. Verisimilitude matters, so trying to make a sword that's meant to last for generations is something that has a lot of time where you put the metal in heat for hours and can do something else, but you can only manage so much of that at once, so it's good to have some pacing to control that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 07-01-2018, 07:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-01T21:05:35-07:00","id":1231720,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231677#post1231677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Damage isn't the equivalent of cxp, the stuff you spend cxp to make is.<br /> <br /> The comparison is Join Battle/Withering = cxp, and decisive/damage = making something big.<br /> <br /> In both models you have to do stuff besides your end goal before you can accomplish your end goal.</div> </div> </div> </div>That makes somewhat more sense, but it's still not a good comparison. The vast majority of things you are going to fight are unnamed characters, animals, automata, etc. that you will never see again, whether you kill them in the scene, drive them off, or leave them unconscious. The rest are things you aren't likely to see again in combat until enough time has passed that they'll be back to full health. You don't get to keep the damage you deal for a later fight most of the time. You most certainly can't insert a fight against whatever big bad you are up against into any scene you want.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've never seen this actually happen, just asserted it happens by people theory-crafting. The two posters here citing their actual characters, and actual play, are doing so in contrast of your assertion here.<br /> <br /> My experience is &quot;Oh, I'm going to craft a thing to help whatever the core goal of the scene (cook a meal to build up morale of troops, make a an intricately designed knife as a gift to impress someone, sew disguises for characters to make seeking into a gala easier, etc).&quot; with a bit of reactive stuff, &quot;we weren't expecting to capture an enemy, I'll whip up some restraints since they're too strong for the ropes we have.&quot; I've never seen. &quot;I know we're in the middle of a fight, but I'm going to cook. I know we're trying to talk an NPC into siding with our political puppet to control the kingdom, but I'm going to go work on a sword. Etc.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Some of those first things you list are the very things I'm talking about. The morale of the troops? Mechanically unrepresented, so cooking them a meal probably does nothing but add color to your character. If that's a thing your character would do, then great. You have established that your character is the kind of person that cooks all the time and for any reason. But you shouldn't feel the need to inject cooking a meal into the scene if that's not relevant to your character. But you need your craft XP, so hey look, your character is doing it anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not any more than using any other Ability since there's a limit to how much you can be doing at once. </div> </div> </div>Other Abilities don't have the gating problem. Craft has a reason to get intrusive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh, I agree the Solar Craft Charms are sub-par. The &quot;get a ton of cxp for buying this Charm and doing nothing,&quot; Charms are definitely &quot;Pay some XP to make the system go away,&quot; and I dislike those highly. Charms that do things like increase the rewards for successfully finishing projects aren't paying to make the system go away though, but still end up making it much easier to have the cxp you want to do stuff. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I mainly took that route with my own Craft rewrite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Right, so... 5-7 Major projects over 3-5 sessions or so? This does not require the, &quot;Everything I do is Craft!&quot; mentality you're claiming it does. </div> </div> </div>Each of those major projects takes 1-2 Basic projects. That's somewhere in the 10 - 21 scenes of crafting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, they aren't. Those are the two examples the book cites. Any large scale mundane project is a Superior one. The examples are just there to try to give STs and players an idea of the line between Major and Superior mundane projects even if it could certainly use more discussion than it got. </div> </div> </div>What other types of mundane superior projects is your character going to be doing in a Bronze Age world on anything resembling a regular basis?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So by this logic, the combat system is absolutely the worst. If rolling Craft at most five times in one session is a ruinous amount of rolling by just one player... the amount of times you roll your primary combat Ability in a decent fight multiplied by all the players... yikes. </div> </div> </div>It's not the rolling. It's the screen time. It's the way it reflects on the character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You just clearly assume that if a player isn't rolling to make an Artifact, they're just rolling some bullshit action. Your argument is disingenuous at best. </div> </div> </div>That argument is disingenuous at best. Again, it's not the rolling. It's the screen time. If you have to have your character find ways to insert Craft into a scene so that you can stay on track with your other Craft goals, it feels wrong. Your character is acting out of character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't agree with the idea that crafting is a verisimilitude breaking abstraction. I think most of the arguments against it are by people that don't actually want it to be verisimilitudinous; because actually making mortal crafting verisimilitudinous to Exalted's setting means dealing with the reality of ancient crafting techniques instead of just being able to shit out a one-in-a-lifetime work every day. </div> </div> </div>There's a huge difference between &quot;realistic&quot; and &quot;versimilitudinous&quot;. The first means trying to recreate reality, the second means trying to recreate the story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Have you actually paid attention to what I've said in this thread, or just asking questions that I've already answered because you need to accumulate &quot;debate xp&quot; for some bigger post? </div> </div> </div>Points for style.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Though in terms of versimilutude? Bladesmiths in a setting like Exalts can't afford to spend their whole lives making swords. &lt;Examples of realism cut for length&gt;<br /> <br /> Your character always had to make a horseshoe before they could make a sword. The 3e crafting system just makes it something that matters instead of something handwaved away. </div> </div> </div>You do a good job demonstrating why the system should avoid realism, but a terrible job of understanding versimilitude. You point out that once you've made hundreds of knives, you totally should be able to make a sword whenever you have a reason, the time, and the materials. That's the part that catches. My character already has the Craft dots. It's assumed that my character has done those hundreds of small tasks off-screen somewhere before reaching Craft 2 or 3. Now my character has an in-character reason why he wants to make a sword. It breaks versimilitude to tell me &quot;you can't because you don't have the craft xp&quot; and it's a mundane sword. There's no game balance reason that I shouldn't be able to do it, so why stand in my way? My character's story is that of a character able to make swords!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not the game's fault if you insist on a clip of 60 sxp before you'll finish a Major project, and thus felt the need to grind to keep your sxp pile that big, out of fear that you might not be able to craft you way out of every problem you face - despite you saying you don't want to craft your way out of every problem - instead of just dealing with either having to see if a Basic or two in the moment would make sense (and it's painfully easy to come up with ones that make sense and add to the story instead of just being dumb grind nonsense), or if you really can't come up with a good Basic just using something else to solve the problem.<br /> <br /> Your stance is so freaking inconsistent on this. You don't want people to craft all the time, every time, for everything.... but you also don't like the system because if you craft too much you might not be able to keep crafting your way out of things.<br /> \u200b </div> </div> </div>It feels like I'm talking past you. Let me try again to get the point across. You create a character that you want to be able to do something the game defines as a Major project. The game gives you two options: either be entirely inconsistent in your ability to do those Major projects when you want or else stockpile some craft points so you'll always have that Major project option on tap. There's no middle option, just different sizes of craft point stockpiles.<br /> <br /> If your character gets in a situation where the thing they would do is blocked off without good reason, then your character has to act out of character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, but not the one you want it to be. My structural problem with the discourse is that you don't allow your opinions and assertions to be treated as anything but unassailable facts even when there's evidence to the contrary. There is a complete imbalance in the structure of benefit of the doubt, and good faith. </div> </div> </div>That's not a structural problem. A structural problem is one where the dynamics of the argument present you with no good options, not when the people involved present you with no good options.<br /> <br /> You say you've presented evidence to the contrary, but at least as far as this thread has gone, you've mostly presented evidence for my position. You've said your shipwright character does something Craft-related in every single scene, sometimes multiple Craft-related things. You claim your character isn't Craft-obsessed, but it sounds like the most defining thing about the character is that they try to use Craft to solve every problem possible. If I'm not supposed to believe that makes my point a point of fact and not just an opinion, how am I supposed to take it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But you don't really use specific examples. You throw out tortured hypothetical situations, and label attempts to present more realistic examples as distractions. And then when that invariably takes its course of actual examples not following your theoretical frame work and thus challenging your &quot;facts&quot; about the system, it turns into quibbling instead of you acknowledge your theory of the problems with the system needing refinement. </div> </div> </div>What &quot;tortured hypothetical situations&quot;? There's nothing particularly tortured about &quot;My character wants to make a sword and the system says no, go do something else first&quot;. It's pretty much explicitly a potential result of the system.<br /> <br /> When did I label a realistic example as a distraction? Honest question. I don't recall doing that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> See, it's not about verisimilitude at all, crafting shit is grindy as hell to actually do. You do that whole &quot;we just endlessly quibble about different projects you can do,&quot; (which is really, &quot;you could do a project that makes sense instead of something you find stupid,&quot;), but you're shifting the goalpost around far more. </div> </div> </div>Again, versimilitude is not realism. Versimilitude is about the story, not the reality.<br /> <br /> This is, again, why I used [significant thing] and [insignificant thing] before. My character wants to do [significant thing] at this moment in time, [significant thing] being a mundane project. The system says do [insignificant thing] first. Why is it insignificant? Because it doesn't mean the same thing to the character. I don't want to get bogged down in the minutia of &quot;Why aren't pot helms significant enough? Why isn't sharpening swords significant enough? Why isn't ... etcetera etcetera&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then there's the going back to the circular, &quot;calling having to do a Basic or two a grind is hyperbolic,&quot; &quot;It doesn't matter anything other than I can craft what I want when I want or it's grinding,&quot; stuff. </div> </div> </div>So &quot;grind&quot; wasn't the best choice of word. I tried to give a simple analogy to demonstrate the problem. I'm not sure why that analogy didn't demonstrate one of the core issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Plus, of course, it's not all about &quot;game balance.&quot; It's also about enabling people to have fun with the rules. Some people want to play MacGuyver, not Artificer, and they should have a system that not just lets them do it, but rewards them for it. Verisimilitude matters, so trying to make a sword that's meant to last for generations is something that has a lot of time where you put the metal in heat for hours and can do something else, but you can only manage so much of that at once, so it's good to have some pacing to control that. </div> </div> </div>We have a system that rewards McGuyver over Artificer. Heck, we have a system that rewards &quot;Short Order Cook&quot; over &quot;Swordsmith&quot;.<br /> <br /> Modulating the rate of parallel production is a function of slots, not craft xp. I have no quarrel with the idea of &quot;You just can't juggle that many projects at once&quot;. That part makes sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T03:44:29-07:00","id":1231749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ale137</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231603#post1231603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Has there been an actual play where they showcase the awsomess of crafting?</div> </div> </div> </div>I considered doing an actual play write-up but both my players are new to Exalted and weren't sure how they felt about their actions coming under the scrutiny of more experienced players.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T03:54:30-07:00","id":1231752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231720#post1231720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That makes somewhat more sense, but it's still not a good comparison.</div> </div> </div> </div>It'd s s fine comparison. The point is that the game makes you do one set of actions before you can do a second set of actions which are your intended goal. Even your whole critic about damage not mattering after the fight doesn't really counter that because (1) yes you absolutely can try to continue to initiate combat against an already injured character if you like, or more likely in play the evil NPCs will try to do this to you, and (2) lots of craft projects aren't things your assumed to hold onto, so just like damage if the NPC you crafted something for goes away after that, then the end result is gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The morale of the troops? Mechanically unrepresented,.... </div> </div> </div>Get a +1 exceptional equipment modifier to rally actions or inspire actions. There, mechanical representation of the efforts achieved. The troops, well fed instead of sufficiently fed to stay combat ready, are now easier to rally in the face of routs, and easier to increase their resolve (and Resolve) to stay in the fight against attempts to intimidate them off the field.<br /> <br /> As for the other two, appropriate gifts/bribes/etc. are necessary for bargain actions, and disguises can certainly get equipment bonuses to your Larceny checks as well.<br /> <br /> So yeah, this isn't just &quot;flavor&quot; but stuff that has mechanical impact.<br /> <br /> And even if it was just flavor, the idea that a character cooking a meal to raise the spirits of their allies in a time of crisis just defines them as just, &quot;the kind of person that cooks all the time and for any reason,&quot; is ridiculous. It's like saying, &quot;your character has found the man that killed their family, and struck them down with the bow their grandfather's bow, you have established your character is just the kind of person that kills people with arrows all the time, and for any reason.&quot; It's ridiculous. That flavor and color is what makes characters interesting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Other Abilities don't have the gating problem. </div> </div> </div>You can't Lore something without an appropriate specialty, so you better intrude reasons to study any new relevant topic so you can Lore it up ASAP.<br /> <br /> You can't use Ride without something to Ride, so you better constantly intrude reasons to have a mount around.<br /> <br /> Etc.<br /> <br /> I know, I know crafting gating is different. But not different enough that your logic works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Each of those major projects takes 1-2 Basic projects. That's somewhere in the 10 - 21 scenes of crafting. </div> </div> </div>No, it's that many scenes where some crafting happened. My character built a stretcher once at the end of a scene because we got separated from our Medicine specialist and wanted to carry an injured NPC safely. That wasn't a &quot;scene of crafting,&quot; it was an obvious thing to do in the situation that was spawn by stopping a jail break in a large complex dungeon where the escapees purposefully split us up in hopes of not getting creamed facing us all at once. Calling it a &quot;scene of crafting,&quot; would be like calling the leap to get over to a key on a different ledge made it a &quot;scene of jumping.&quot; It was one action that took up a tiny bit of spotlight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What other types of mundane superior projects is your character going to be doing in a Bronze Age world on anything resembling a regular basis? </div> </div> </div>Depends on your character and the story? I mean, single characters tilling wild lands into acres of fresh farmlands isn't exactly a rare thing in Exalted's source material, and it seems to me to be a Superior farming project, and something that could easily come up frequently in some stories. Though I'm not sure why &quot;regular basis&quot; is a qualifier here. Seems like it's mostly there to take away the plethora of non-regular examples of mundane Superior projects that could fall under other crafts than shipwright and architect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's the screen time. It's the way it reflects on the character. </div> </div> </div>You're seriously comparing Basic and Major projects negatively in terms of how much screen time they take up compared to combat? This is a joke right?<br /> <br /> And it doesn't &quot;reflect&quot; on a character in any bad way that you've asserted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you have to have your character find ways to insert Craft into a scene so that you can stay on track with your other Craft goals, it feels wrong. </div> </div> </div>I've never had to find ways to insert Craft into a scene. My ST pays attention to characters and tries to make sure they all get changes to do cool things appropriate to their characters as much as possible in scenes so the spotlight gets shared, and Craft is useful when you're doing it to advance the scene or add to the story first, and getting the rewards second.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your character is acting out of character. </div> </div> </div>Wow. That's just absurd. &quot;My chef wants to cook a meal.&quot; &quot;That's out of character! Chefs never want to cook meals for people!&quot;<br /> <br /> What, exactly, is out of character about a craftsman taking an opportunity to apply their trade when it presents itself?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a huge difference between &quot;realistic&quot; and &quot;versimilitudinous&quot;. The first means trying to recreate reality, the second means trying to recreate the story. </div> </div> </div>There is a difference, it isn't that big, and you're definition of verisimilitude is incorrect. Verisimilitude is the trait of appearing to be realistic even if it isn't (it literally comes from the roots for 'truth' and 'simulate'), whether applied to our reality, or the fictional reality of Creation. Verisimilitude is the process of reducing the strain of suspension of disbelief despite unreal things being present. You seem to be conflating it with emulation.<br /> <br /> Since Creation's mortal blacksmiths operate under the same basic considerations that ancient real world blacksmiths did, being verisimilitudinous to the setting means sticking fairly close to realistic depictions of blacksmiths. And a truly verisimilitudinous crafting system would reflect that.<br /> <br /> A place where being verisimilitudinous to the setting means being unrealistic is something like throwing out how optics work by allowing Creation to both be a flat earth, and have normal horizons.<br /> <br /> So, yeah, you don't actually want a lot of verisimilitude in mortal crafting in Exalted's crafting system, because that would be tedious as hell.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It breaks versimilitude to tell me &quot;you can't because you don't have the craft xp&quot; and it's a mundane sword. </div> </div> </div>No, this is a break in immersion. Also, again, have you been reading my posts? Because this whole back and forth started when I contested your assertion that gating and the basic objectives where the problem, where I disagreed and said that the definitions of the projects was the core problem, and should be moved to a more narrative base than an in-character effort base precisely because it avoids this kind of immersion breaking.<br /> <br /> It's not a break in verisimilitude because it is not a strain on suspension of disbelief that sometimes craftspeople don't have the mental energy despite having the time and materials around to do an intensive project. &quot;I've been crafting masterwork swords for a month straight, and now I just don't want to look at a sword again for awhile,&quot; is totally something that happens, even if you don't like when the system mandates that it happens.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's no game balance reason that I shouldn't be able to do it, so why stand in my way? </div> </div> </div>Because, however much I disagree with the specific approach the original devs took, it is about verisimilitude towards the setting. Creation is a place where crafting swords is a Big Deal for mortal crafting, and the game wants to keep it that way by making it difficult to simply only crafts swords, and craft them whenever you want. It's a pacing issue, not a balance one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game gives you two options: either be entirely inconsistent in your ability to do those Major projects when you want or else stockpile some craft points so you'll always have that Major project option on tap. There's no middle option, just different sizes of craft point stockpiles. </div> </div> </div>There is a third option: generate sxp regularly enough that your ability to craft Major projects is consistent even as you always spend on Majors instead of stockpiling. The system does not mandate significantly inconsistent sxp generation rates.<br /> <br /> Also, you really seem to be proving my point that the issue is how projects are defined here rather than the gating aspect. By having some Crafts that are Basic heavy, and some that are Major heavy, and some that are Superior heavy, and others that are balanced, it messes with enjoying the system. Redefining the projects on a narrative impact basis eliminates this because no Craft is inherently more focused on one type of project over the others. Instead of, &quot;I'm frustrated because the books say swords are Major,&quot; you get, &quot;I can craft all the swords I want, but the bigger a deal the sword is to the story, the more effort the game asks of me.&quot; Problem solved.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If your character gets in a situation where the thing they would do is blocked off without good reason, then your character has to act out of character. </div> </div> </div>The game doesn't really dictate the in-game reason, so there's no acting out-of-character unless you create a narrative on why they're blocked off that does so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A structural problem is one where the dynamics of the argument present you with no good options, not when the people involved present you with no good options. </div> </div> </div>But not mutually exclusive. I effectively accused you of creating the dynamics of the argument to present me with no good options. Dynamics of arguments are people constructed and all that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You claim your character isn't Craft-obsessed, but it sounds like the most defining thing about the character is that they try to use Craft to solve every problem possible. </div> </div> </div>Because that's what you want to hear.<br /> <br /> You don't care that a Basic crafting project in a scene is a fairly minor thing. You want to blow it out of proportion, and feel free to because I'm not posting full write-ups of four hour long play sessions here. Like the jail break scene earlier. I did one Craft roll that took all of a minute to do, where I spent at least 10 minutes on Athletics stuff, and a good 45 minutes with Brawl in combat. But sure. I built a stretch for an injured character after the fight. Totally Crafting obsessed, totally stole all the spotlight with that one crafting, and not the big ass fight that was part fo the scene.<br /> <br /> Most scenes have more than one thing to accomplish.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I'm not supposed to believe that makes my point a point of fact and not just an opinion, how am I supposed to take it? </div> </div> </div>You're supposed to take it with charity and assume that when I say my character does one or two craftings in a scene it's a minor part of the scene to believe that to be the case instead of assuming that when I say may character crafted something that it magically took up the whole scene and solved every problem in it despite me not saying that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What &quot;tortured hypothetical situations&quot;? </div> </div> </div>The tired refrain of horseshoes and swords, or whatever.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When did I label a realistic example as a distraction? </div> </div> </div>I'm referencing your treatment of attempts to come up with realistic examples of how to have a narrative where the gating doesn't break immersion is this:, &quot;,,,then we'll be endlessly quibbling about things,,,&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The system says do [insignificant thing] first. Why is it insignificant? Because it doesn't mean the same thing to the character. </div> </div> </div>And it's still bullshit. Significance isn't binary, and the other thing(s) you have to go do need to hit the basic objectives which is the metric the game uses for being significant.<br /> <br /> The other thing might be less significant to your character, but it can't be insignificant or it doesn't count towards getting you cxp anway.<br /> <br /> And, of course, the whole phrasing denotes approaching the system with, &quot;this is busted, I want to play it in a fashion that demonstrates it,&quot; instead of, &quot;I'm going to do my best to have fun playing the system and see what happens.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't want to get bogged down in the minutia of &quot;Why aren't pot helms significant enough? Why isn't sharpening swords significant enough? Why isn't ... etcetera etcetera&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Again though, those things aren't brought up because of the reason you're dismissing them. They're brought up because it's not hard to find a less significant but still significant thing for your crafter to craft if you need to up your cxp, instead of just going, &quot;it's not this one thing, and despite the fact that I know the system doesn't like mono-crafters, I refuse to allow them to find anything else significant so I can say it's the game's fault.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not sure why that analogy didn't demonstrate one of the core issues. </div> </div> </div>Because it doesn't? I'm not sure why you don't see that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We have a system that rewards McGuyver over Artificer. Heck, we have a system that rewards &quot;Short Order Cook&quot; over &quot;Swordsmith&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Yep. The system unevenly rewards different crafts. That's not generally a good thing. But at the same time it's not a bad thing to discourage crafts that are too narrow. It's a lot more fun for the game to have a blacksmith instead of a bladesmith, because going broader fits the level of abstraction of the game better, and it's easier on incorporating your concept into different situations.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T04:05:36-07:00","id":1231755,"title":"","content":"A bog standard sword you churned out in a few hours is absolutely a basic project. I'd reserve major for fancy swords you'd expect a noble to have. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T09:05:55-07:00","id":1231828,"title":"","content":"By default, they're major as they represent some pretty notable material benefit (a weapon) and the fact that even swords we think of as bog standard took a lot of labor back in the day. You could equip armies, but it took near armies of smiths to do so becuase before industrialization making shit at scale as hard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T09:26:16-07:00","id":1231838,"title":"","content":"Yeah but this is heroic fantasy so fuck that noise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T10:57:47-07:00","id":1231875,"title":"","content":"Forging is the time consuming part when making a sword.<br /> If you just want a sharp piece of metal with a handle? Two of the girls I went to school with got in a lot of trouble for sharpening a pair of metal rulers during a Design and Technology class and getting one of them embedded in a wooden desk during a sword fight.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T11:18:49-07:00","id":1231879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231755#post1231755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A bog standard sword you churned out in a few hours is absolutely a basic project. I'd reserve major for fancy swords you'd expect a noble to have.</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's not have this particular fragment of the argument. It goes nowhere. Even if you convince everyone that <i>this</i> particular project belongs in a lower bracket, there's still going to be some project that's a Major project and the gating mechanic doesn't make sense when applied to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231752#post1231752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a fine comparison..</div> </div> </div> </div>We'll have to agree to disagree on that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Get a +1 exceptional equipment modifier to rally actions or inspire actions. There, mechanical representation of the efforts achieved. The troops, well fed instead of sufficiently fed to stay combat ready, are now easier to rally in the face of routs, and easier to increase their resolve (and Resolve) to stay in the fight against attempts to intimidate them off the field. </div> </div> </div>I was not suggesting that the lack of mechanical representation was the part that was wrong in the situation. I was pointing out that this is not necessarily something in character for your character. If you make it a mechanically represented, it now will mean <i>everyone</i> will need to make sure there's a good cook in the army.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the other two, appropriate gifts/bribes/etc. are necessary for bargain actions, and disguises can certainly get equipment bonuses to your Larceny checks as well. </div> </div> </div>Gifts/bribes are situational, so I didn't quibble. I thought the disguises bit was a nice touch, provided your character is a tailor and that contributing in that way is something your character would do. Certainly something you could probably acquire with Resources, but then there's also an argument against leaving a paper trail.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And even if it was just flavor, the idea that a character cooking a meal to raise the spirits of their allies in a time of crisis just defines them as just, &quot;the kind of person that cooks all the time and for any reason,&quot; is ridiculous. It's like saying, &quot;your character has found the man that killed their family, and struck them down with the bow their grandfather's bow, you have established your character is just the kind of person that kills people with arrows all the time, and for any reason.&quot; It's ridiculous. That flavor and color is what makes characters interesting. </div> </div> </div>You forget that this is just one note of a larger pattern. This isn't just this one time you do this sort of thing, but also that you keep looking for reasons to do this sort of thing. You cook for the troops. You make sure to mention that you made dinner around the campfire. You bake pies for the new neighbors. etc. Your character keeps using their chunk of screen time for craft instead of other things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't Lore something without an appropriate specialty, so you better intrude reasons to study any new relevant topic so you can Lore it up ASAP. </div> </div> </div>Bad analogy. Specialties are paid out of XP, not actions on screen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't use Ride without something to Ride, so you better constantly intrude reasons to have a mount around. </div> </div> </div>Point, but Ride has a natural maximum cap in place. You can only ride one thing at a time. If you've got your horse outside, you've basically got all the mounts you need.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I know, I know crafting gating is different. But not different enough that your logic works. </div> </div> </div>Why not? In acknowledging that the gating is different in Craft, you have created a gap in your argument, but by not elaborating you haven't filled that gap.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It was one action that took up a tiny bit of spotlight. </div> </div> </div>I feel like this sort of thing is like trying to argue with an Impressionist painter about a particular dot of paint. I don't object to any particular dot. I object to what all the dots together start to say about the character. Just because your character has a craft skill doesn't mean your character should look at the world through the lens of &quot;can I solve this with my craft?&quot; but that's the type of world view the craft system tries to impose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Depends on your character and the story? I mean, single characters tilling wild lands into acres of fresh farmlands isn't exactly a rare thing in Exalted's source material, and it seems to me to be a Superior farming project, and something that could easily come up frequently in some stories. Though I'm not sure why &quot;regular basis&quot; is a qualifier here. Seems like it's mostly there to take away the plethora of non-regular examples of mundane Superior projects that could fall under other crafts than shipwright and architect. </div> </div> </div>1) Farming doesn't typically fall under the Craft Ability.<br /> 2) Even if it did, would that be a Superior project or just a Basic project repeated over and over?<br /> <br /> More importantly and more to the point, this particular strand of the argument has been about your assertion that craft points are still important to track even if you aren't making artifacts. I will concede this is true when in the realm of architecture and shipbuilding, but if superior projects are something that only happens under unusual circumstances there's no need to meter how much of such things you can do. They're freak occurrences, so the issue can just be handwaved without causing any larger issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're seriously comparing Basic and Major projects negatively in terms of how much screen time they take up compared to combat? This is a joke right?<br /> <br /> And it doesn't &quot;reflect&quot; on a character in any bad way that you've asserted. </div> </div> </div>You definitely missed my point there. Allow me to try to state it clearly: The Craft system attempts to impose on every character interested in doing major things with their craft the world view of &quot;How can I solve this problem using my craft?&quot;. This is not appropriate for all characters that have dots in Craft and want to use them. Some people want to play characters with craft skills who have other problem-solving paradigms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've never had to find ways to insert Craft into a scene. My ST pays attention to characters and tries to make sure they all get changes to do cool things appropriate to their characters as much as possible in scenes so the spotlight gets shared, and Craft is useful when you're doing it to advance the scene or add to the story first, and getting the rewards second. </div> </div> </div>I find this interesting. How much work does your Storyteller have to do to keep shipbuilding relevant to the story?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Wow. That's just absurd. &quot;My chef wants to cook a meal.&quot; &quot;That's out of character! Chefs never want to cook meals for people!&quot; </div> </div> </div>Of course a sentence ripped from context sounds ridiculous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is a difference, it isn't that big, and you're definition of verisimilitude is incorrect. Verisimilitude is the trait of appearing to be realistic even if it isn't (it literally comes from the roots for 'truth' and 'simulate'), whether applied to our reality, or the fictional reality of Creation. Verisimilitude is the process of reducing the strain of suspension of disbelief despite unreal things being present. You seem to be conflating it with emulation. </div> </div> </div>You're probably correct about the word's origins and uses in other contexts, but I've been having discussions about RPG settings and mechanics for a long time. &quot;Versimilitude&quot; has been co-opted in at least the past few forums I've been on as a short hand for &quot;all the stuff that makes the world look right to the story, even if background details are being ignored or glossed over&quot;.I had thought this was a piece of shared context.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not a break in verisimilitude because it is not a strain on suspension of disbelief that sometimes craftspeople don't have the mental energy despite having the time and materials around to do an intensive project. &quot;I've been crafting masterwork swords for a month straight, and now I just don't want to look at a sword again for awhile,&quot; is totally something that happens, even if you don't like when the system mandates that it happens. </div> </div> </div>First point: I'm generally the arbiter of how my character is feeling. I'm the one who thinks it would be appropriate for my character to make a sword right now.<br /> Second point: There are plenty of times when you just have to power through regardless of creative burnout. One of the earliest anecdotes that I recall in the history of the &quot;Crafting debate&quot; was a Twilight trying to help defend a town during a siege. It was literally their first time looking at the Craft rules. The Twilight wanted to help build some catapults but the system told him &quot;No, you aren't inspired enough&quot;.<br /> Third point: That sounds a lot like trying to justify the restriction after the fact rather than an actual motivator for the restriction.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is a third option: generate sxp regularly enough that your ability to craft Major projects is consistent even as you always spend on Majors instead of stockpiling. </div> </div> </div>That's a stockpile of one major project's worth.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, you really seem to be proving my point that the issue is how projects are defined here rather than the gating aspect. By having some Crafts that are Basic heavy, and some that are Major heavy, and some that are Superior heavy, and others that are balanced, it messes with enjoying the system. Redefining the projects on a narrative impact basis eliminates this because no Craft is inherently more focused on one type of project over the others. Instead of, &quot;I'm frustrated because the books say swords are Major,&quot; you get, &quot;I can craft all the swords I want, but the bigger a deal the sword is to the story, the more effort the game asks of me.&quot; Problem solved. </div> </div> </div>The downside to that approach is that now all the work that was offloaded from the Storyteller is now back. Before you know what you are going to pay, you have to ask the Storyteller how important to the story he thinks the project is. It's certainly a valid approach, but it's not one that I would like. I prefer to try to address the burs on the current system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game doesn't really dictate the in-game reason, so there's no acting out-of-character unless you create a narrative on why they're blocked off that does so. </div> </div> </div>The problem is that there's no in-game reason. The reason the character is blocked exists out of game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're supposed to take it with charity and assume that when I say my character does one or two craftings in a scene it's a minor part of the scene to believe that to be the case instead of assuming that when I say may character crafted something that it magically took up the whole scene and solved every problem in it despite me not saying that. </div> </div> </div>You might want to ratchet the hyperbole back a notch or two. I'm not saying it takes up all the screen time or that it solves all of the problems. You did, however, bring your Craft on-screen and make a point that this is how your character looks at the world because you bring it on-screen often. Do you want to say that same thing about every character with a craft skill?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The tired refrain of horseshoes and swords, or whatever. </div> </div> </div>Tired is different from tortured.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm referencing your treatment of attempts to come up with realistic examples of how to have a narrative where the gating doesn't break immersion is this:, &quot;,,,then we'll be endlessly quibbling about things,,,&quot; </div> </div> </div>I thought you were meaning that you had brought up a realistic example demonstrating some point and I had dismissed it. I clearly laid out exactly why I was avoiding concretizing the argument by using [significant thing] and [insignificant thing] and I stand by that reasoning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And it's still bullshit. Significance isn't binary, and the other thing(s) you have to go do need to hit the basic objectives which is the metric the game uses for being significant.<br /> <br /> The other thing might be less significant to your character, but it can't be insignificant or it doesn't count towards getting you cxp anway. </div> </div> </div>The objectives define a very low standard of significance. Intimacy of &quot;I like making things&quot; and then hawk it for a bent nickel and you've gotten two out of three. I think we agree that the objectives define significance poorly.<br /> <br /> Further, I'm not talking about mechanical significance. We've established that as a low bar to clear. I'm talking about character and story significance. It means something different when I make you a sword than when I make you a knife.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, of course, the whole phrasing denotes approaching the system with, &quot;this is busted, I want to play it in a fashion that demonstrates it,&quot; instead of, &quot;I'm going to do my best to have fun playing the system and see what happens.&quot; </div> </div> </div>You seem to be complaining that I am attempting to make the strongest, clearest argument that I can. Yes, I used a framing technique. There's a reason that framing gets used a lot in debates. If you control the shape of the box, you have the home court advantage.<br /> <br /> You, of course, are coming back with an attempt at reframing. You are attempting to put me in the box of &quot;intentionally sabotaging the system&quot;. I refuse to get in that box. I am acknowledging where the incentive structures of the system are. That they easily bend towards &quot;this is busted&quot; is the problem I am pointing out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again though, those things aren't brought up because of the reason you're dismissing them. They're brought up because it's not hard to find a less significant but still significant thing for your crafter to craft if you need to up your cxp, instead of just going, &quot;it's not this one thing, and despite the fact that I know the system doesn't like mono-crafters, I refuse to allow them to find anything else significant so I can say it's the game's fault.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Basic and Major cover huge swaths of projects. My character doesn't have to be a guy who only makes swords for this gating problem to show up. Maybe my character is a well-rounded blacksmith and does everything from cooking pots to decorative ironwork. Right now, it is narratively appropriate for him to make a sword. The system says no. I think this is jarring and out of place. If I use that example, though, you come back with &quot;Why not make a spear?&quot; It's probably not duplicitous on your part - it's a natural part of the human mind to hear a problem and try to solve it in a practical way. &quot;Change the laws of physics&quot; is generally not an answer that even deserves the time of day in a normal context. In this debate, though, the response &quot;Why not make a spear?&quot; is a red herring. I'm trying to discuss that jarring disconnect between what my character is capable of doing and wishes to do and the result that he can't do it. Talking about &quot;Why not a make a spear?&quot; is irrelevant to that overarching problem of getting slapped by the rules for not crafting in the pattern that the rules say I should be using.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because it doesn't? I'm not sure why you don't see that. </div> </div> </div>I don't see the failure because I don't know at what point your mind left the track that mine followed. This is the fundamental issue in trying to reconcile world views. I don't read minds. That's why these posts are turning into behemoths. We're throwing words at each other trying to figure out how the other person is thinking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":74,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","user":"Kyeudo","date":". 07-02-2018, 09:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T11:51:08-07:00","id":1231890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231752#post1231752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;it's not this one thing, and despite the fact that I know the system doesn't like mono-crafters, I refuse to allow them to find anything else significant so I can say it's the game's fault.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>If I want to play a &quot;mono-crafter&quot; and the game punishes me for it even though not punishing mono-crafters would not hurt game balance, it definitely is the games fault.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231752#post1231752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">going broader fits the level of abstraction of the game better, and it's easier on incorporating your concept into different situations</div> </div> </div> </div>But what if I don't want to incoporate it into different situations, and don't want a broad concept. The game actively punishes non-broad concepts by forcing them to either go broad or pay xp for charms that let the skip the parts of the broader concept they're not interested in.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231752#post1231752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, of course, the whole phrasing denotes approaching the system with, &quot;this is busted, I want to play it in a fashion that demonstrates it,&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a ridiculously uncharitable reading. Why people have problems with craft is &quot;I know how I envision my character and it doesn't include crafting random small stuff all the time, why is the system actively trying to make me change my character concept for no good reason?&quot; This shouldn't be news given that people have said as much over and over again since the craft system was released.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2579-limaxophobiac","user":"limaxophobiac","date":". 07-02-2018, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2579-limaxophobiac","name":"limaxophobiac","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"294"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T12:50:21-07:00","id":1231904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>limaxophobiac</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231890#post1231890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I want to play a &quot;mono-crafter&quot; and the game punishes me for it even though not punishing mono-crafters would not hurt game balance, it definitely is the games fault.</div> </div> </div> </div>You might want to tack down what you mean by &quot;mono-crafter&quot; there. There's a world of difference between a guy who only makes swords and a guy with all the skills necessary to make swords.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is a ridiculously uncharitable reading. Why people have problems with craft is &quot;I know how I envision my character and it doesn't include crafting random small stuff all the time, why is the system actively trying to make me change my character concept for no good reason?&quot; This shouldn't be news given that people have said as much over and over again since the craft system was released. </div> </div> </div>Deep breath. I suspect it's a world-view level thing, where the problem is framed differently inside his head but communicating that frame is difficult because most of the time you don't even realize you have a frame. It also could be that he's used to thinking inside the system and we're looking at the system from the outside. You don't think about worlds where objects fall up because you are so used to having gravity around.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T13:25:38-07:00","id":1231910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231879#post1231879\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Let's not have this particular fragment of the argument. It goes nowhere. Even if you convince everyone that <i>this</i> particular project belongs in a lower bracket, there's still going to be some project that's a Major project and the gating mechanic doesn't make sense when applied to it.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there will with <i>that</i> attitude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T14:29:05-07:00","id":1231940,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231879#post1231879\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was not suggesting that the lack of mechanical representation was the part that was wrong in the situation. I was pointing out that this is not necessarily something in character for your character. If you make it a mechanically represented, it now will mean <i>everyone</i> will need to make sure there's a good cook in the army.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again the lack of consistency here is stagger. &quot;I brought up a lack of mechanical representation, but I didn't mean that this mattered when you pointed out that it was mechanically represented, and instead that the mechanical presentation being there is really a bad thing!&quot; You can cook and boost morale... so every one will want good cooks that can boost morale? And? Every army wants multiple Exalts fighting for them too. Everyone wants the most advantages in something high stakes like war.<br /> <br /> Oh and then is about &quot;it might not be in character!&quot; Well, no, it was in character, that's why it happened. Your entire argument is basically, if you do something that has a mechanical advantage for your character, than it inherently undermines the ability for the action to be in-character. Which basically is an argument to toss out all mechanics because any mechanic can have an incentive to do something out of character.<br /> <br /> And I believe the famous saying is, &quot;an army marches on it's stomach.&quot; An army without a quartermaster, and appropriate subordinates (like cooks), is an army that folds under stress. So, yeah, every army should be making sure they have cooks, either regular members of a supply corp, or bringing in outsiders (generally locals) if you somehow don't have your own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Certainly something you could probably acquire with Resources, but then there's also an argument against leaving a paper trail. </div> </div> </div>Or, you know, it's Creation, not reality, and not everything is for sale all the time just because you have Resources. There's no Amazon with overnight shipping. There isn't necessarily a skilled tailor and/or seamstress around that will even want to make suspicious things for you where you are. The &quot;Resources can just buy it,&quot; thing is a bit specious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You forget that this is just one note of a larger pattern. </div> </div> </div>Not really? I'm just not assuming a pattern. You're approaching this with a set pattern in mind, so filling in the details how you want. Which is fine for something like appreciating an Impressionist painting, but not great for assumptions about how things will play out in an interactive, collaborative, highly structured creative thought exercise.<br /> <br /> To me, the pattern as it forms over time is what makes the character. The actions that bring the stuff on their sheet to life as something fun and interesting emerge from each of those actions. Maybe your character is mercenary, maybe a bleeding heart, or a dedicated loyalist. Who they do these things for, and why, matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your character keeps using their chunk of screen time for craft instead of other things. </div> </div> </div>Why not? If I made a chef, I want that to be part of their screen time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Bad analogy. Specialties are paid out of XP, not actions on screen. </div> </div> </div>Gating is gating is gating. Lore is actually worse because it uses XP which actively denies you other things vastly more drastically than crafting actions do.<br /> <br /> But the essential problem is you, again, aren't consistent. &quot;This form of gating is OK, but this form isn't, and the reasons are always shifting.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In acknowledging that the gating is different in Craft, you have created a gap in your argument, but by not elaborating you haven't filled that gap. </div> </div> </div>The gap doesn't effect my point, so it doesn't matter. You haven't actually established why Craft's gating is bad, but all the other forms of gating are good, in a consistent manner.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just because your character has a craft skill doesn't mean your character should look at the world through the lens of &quot;can I solve this with my craft?&quot; but that's the type of world view the craft system tries to impose. </div> </div> </div>Why shouldn't a character look at solving problems with their best skills first?<br /> <br /> If a character is a skilled doctor, and a shitty driver, why shouldn't they say, &quot;I'm going to call 911 and stabilize this person that's bleeding out because I know what I'm doing,&quot; instead of trying to save time and drive the patient to the nearest ER without an ambulance to safely speed through lights and all that. Or flipped, the crack driver rushes the person to the ER because they can weave through traffic like a madman without worry, but they'll probably make things worse trying to do first aid?<br /> <br /> It's one thing to say, &quot;this situation calls for something that isn't your best skill, and trying to shoehorn it in is a problem,&quot; it's a different thing to say, &quot;considering your best skills first if they can apply is bad.&quot;<br /> <br /> If I make a Dawn combat monster, damn straight I'm going to think, &quot;can I violence my way through this problem?&quot; as a consideration. That's what I made my character to do. The answer might be, &quot;Yes, lets cut some people up,&quot; &quot;No, this is a bad time for violence, let the the smart one, social one, or whatever handle it,&quot; or, &quot;Maybe, so talk to the others and see which approach is best.&quot; And more. Just because something is something I consider because it's my character's go to skill, doesn't mean I'm always going to use it to the detriment of the story, or the fun of the game. Because that's not the point of playing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1) Farming doesn't typically fall under the Craft Ability. </div> </div> </div>All of farming? No. Lots of things farmers do? Yes. Building a barn is craft. Tilling fresh fields is certainly craft. Building fences, enclosures, and pens are definitely craft. Plucking crops? Not craft. Riding a horse to herd livestock? Definitely not craft.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Even if it did, would that be a Superior project or just a Basic project repeated over and over? </div> </div> </div>Explicitly not how Craft projects work. You can't turn a higher level project into lots of lower level projects. Otherwise everything would just be a series of Basic projects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I will concede this is true when in the realm of architecture and shipbuilding, but if superior projects are something that only happens under unusual circumstances there's no need to meter how much of such things you can do. They're freak occurrences, so the issue can just be handwaved without causing any larger issues. </div> </div> </div>The problem is that what is a &quot;freak occurrence&quot; to most people in Creation could easily be a big deal for the main characters in an Exalted campaign. Exigents, for example, are the kind of characters that can end up specialized about this sort of thing. The system is meant to be future-proofed for such things.<br /> <br /> I mean, the whole setup is kind of weird. How often are people building palaces in your games? It's never happened mundanely in one of mine... but you're fine with that being metered. Why is architecture OK even if it's not really something adventuring heroes are likely to do much of in most iterations of the game? Why can't it be handwaved? The book brings it up?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Craft system attempts to impose on every character interested in doing major things with their craft the world view of &quot;How can I solve this problem using my craft?&quot;. </div> </div> </div>What's the difference between &quot;interested in doing major things with their craft,&quot; and, &quot;solving a problem with [their] craft?&quot;<br /> <br /> Because that's where I think all of this falls down. It just seems a weird semantic game (actually a lot of this is a weird semantic game), rather than en expression of a real problem.<br /> <br /> What is an interesting thing with their craft, that doesn't some some narrative problem? Why are such things even important to consider?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is not appropriate for all characters that have dots in Craft and want to use them. </div> </div> </div>Why is a dot on your sheet there if you don't intend to use it? Or if you don't want to use it and it's just there for flavor, why do you care if the system doesn't let you use it a specific way later?<br /> <br /> This is another point of incoherence. &quot;I don't want to use these dots on my sheet, but it's a problem that the system doesn't let me use them?&quot; Huh? Even in a &quot;big picture&quot; view of not crafting all the time thing, it doesn't make sense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some people want to play characters with craft skills who have other problem-solving paradigms. </div> </div> </div>And they can! My craft focused character problem solved other ways all the time, because I could Craft plenty and still do other things! The crafting system didn't (and as far as I can tell never actually has) mind control me into always crafting everything all the time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I find this interesting. How much work does your Storyteller have to do to keep shipbuilding relevant to the story? </div> </div> </div>Almost none. I made the character because they wanted to run something on the Dreaming Sea for our first 3e game...our primary city being a military harbor during the lead up to open war between naval powers made it kind of... always relevant. There were a few scenes were it don't go smoothly (but we all chalked that up to adjusting to the new edition, such as the +1 for similar by different projects thing getting missed), but it wasn't a problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course a sentence ripped from context sounds ridiculous. </div> </div> </div>It still sounds ridiculous in context. &quot;My chef makes a meal and gets cxp for it, thus is acting out of character inherently,&quot; is an absurd statement, and that's the context. If you acknowledge that it could be in character, then your whole point here falls apart, because there's nothing wrong with characters spotlight time on in-character actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First point: I'm generally the arbiter of how my character is feeling. I'm the one who thinks it would be appropriate for my character to make a sword right now. </div> </div> </div>Fair, but doesn't change anything (as is acknowledge already).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Second point: There are plenty of times when you just have to power through regardless of creative burnout. </div> </div> </div>OK, but any mechanic to determine if you do or don't have the mental energy to power through is going to be the same thing. If you could innately trade WP for cxp, then it would just happen when you run out of WP instead. If you have it be fiat based, then you're neglecting the importance of the the rules to solve the &quot;I shot you first, no I shot you first,&quot; classic dilemma.<br /> <br /> Player, &quot;I power through!&quot;<br /> ST, &quot;No, you've crafting too much, you're clearly exhausted,&quot;<br /> <br /> The system is there to keep that from happening.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Third point: That sounds a lot like trying to justify the restriction after the fact rather than an actual motivator for the restriction. </div> </div> </div>I didn't design the system, you can use this against anything I say no matter how valid it is, making this a meaningless point. It's also not really a vital distinction. Whether I cam up with a justification after the fact that works, or I've managed to accurately interpreted the actual motivator and present a justification for it? The justification stands. The nature of getting to it doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's a stockpile of one major project's worth. </div> </div> </div>That's not what stockpile means (again the whole semantics thing). A stockpile is explicitly an excess of what you need in the moment, a generally for the assumption that you'll need more at a later time when you can't acquire what you need.<br /> <br /> Having a well on your property where you get your water isn't stockpiling water, nor is drawing out a bucket of water to use for a drink, but it provides you a consistent source of fresh water. <br /> <br /> Building cisterns and filing them up so you have water even if there's a bad dry spell is stockpiling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The downside to that approach is that now all the work that was offloaded from the Storyteller is now back. </div> </div> </div>You seemed to have missed the post where I said I'd actually offload it onto the player when stating their intentions for the project. The ST still has their job of arbiter as for any other action, but the player sets the terms first in this context. &quot;I want to make a sword to impress the guards of this small village we're leaving in a week, so I want to make a Basic project sword.&quot; would be how things go by default.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is that there's no in-game reason. The reason the character is blocked exists out of game. </div> </div> </div>But this doesn't address the point. As it exists out of game, you're free to explain it in game how you want so it makes sense and doesn't cause out of character actions.<br /> <br /> There's no in game reason I need 7+ Initiative before I can land a killing blow on a target. But we narrate combat such the withering attacks fit the narrative of my character trying to kill their enemy, but fails and builds until they have the base Initiative to try. I could try to constantly narrate things differently and then make it not make sense and cause out of character actions, but why would I want to do that?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You might want to ratchet the hyperbole back a notch or two. </div> </div> </div>I don't think I'm being hyperbolic there... so maybe you should consider your language choices instead?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not saying it takes up all the screen time or that it solves all of the problems. </div> </div> </div>Actually, that is, as far as I can tell 100% of your objection.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You did, however, bring your Craft on-screen and make a point that this is how your character looks at the world because you bring it on-screen often. Do you want to say that same thing about every character with a craft skill? </div> </div> </div>The only point I made about the way my character looks at the world, is that they know they're awesome at making stuff, and when there's a problem that can be solved with building stuff, they'll consider doing just that. And yes, I think every character with dots in a Skill should have, &quot;I'd consider using this Skill when appropriate,&quot; said about them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Tired is different from tortured. </div> </div> </div>Like most synonyms, yes. But they overlap in context. Maybe you should stop with the definition policing here?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It means something different when I make you a sword than when I make you a knife. </div> </div> </div>But still, that doesn't mean one is insignificant.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You seem to be complaining that I am attempting to make the strongest, clearest argument that I can. </div> </div> </div>No, I'm complaining that you're using dishonest debating tactics, lack of charity in reading, and such things which don't make your arguments strong or clear. Just annoying.<br /> <br /> When you acknowledge that you have to &quot;claim home field advantage&quot; by framing the debate in your favor, you're acknowledging that you don't have as strong or clear argument, you need to manipulate the terms because you points aren't good enough on their own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You are attempting to put me in the box of &quot;intentionally sabotaging the system&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Except all of your arguments come down to playing the game under the assumption that it will be done in bad faith. Exalted is a game that explicitly has a lot of places that could be abused, but stopped caring about policing that and trusts the players and ST to be there to have fun instead of trying to combat bad actors with the rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Right now, it is narratively appropriate for him to make a sword. </div> </div> </div>Is it though? It might be appropriate for a sword to be made. Or for him to want to make a sword. But there are narratively approrpiate reasons why it might not be approrpiate in the total of the narrative as well. So it's only jarring if the total narrative has no reason for him to not end up making the sword included.<br /> <br /> That's the whole problem with this &quot;problem.&quot; It only happens if you let it happen. If the group has set up no narrative reasons for this situation, how did it even come about? How come they don't have enough sxp and why doesn't that narrative explain things? Why aren't some Basics appropriate to the narrative?<br /> <br /> And, back to this thing you ignore time and time and time again: what's wrong if a character can't solve this narrative issue with Craft this time if you don't want things to always be solved with Craft? Because you've never, ever, addressed this disconnect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I use that example, though, you come back with... </div> </div> </div>No, but you do end up sounding condescending and like you know you're actually wrong but can't admit it with underhanded bullshit where you play psychic (but you don't read minds!)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't see the failure because I don't know at what point your mind left the track that mine followed. </div> </div> </div>I followed the whole thing. It's just illogical nonsense in my estimation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We're throwing words at each other trying to figure out how the other person is thinking. </div> </div> </div>I don't feel from this thread, or past ones, that you give a single shit about what I'm thinking; especially since you keep assuming what I'm thinking in fairly annoying ways.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>limaxophobiac</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231890#post1231890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I want to play a &quot;mono-crafter&quot; and the game punishes me for it even though not punishing mono-crafters would not hurt game balance, it definitely is the games fault.</div> </div> </div> </div>The game cares about more than balance. So, no.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But what if I don't want to incoporate it into different situations, and don't want a broad concept. </div> </div> </div>Then maybe Exalted isn't your game? Broad concepts are kind of how it rolls.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is a ridiculously uncharitable reading. </div> </div> </div>If you feel that way. Come back to me when you feel like calling out everyone instead of one person when you jump in the middle of stuff. But this isn't the first Craft debate rodeo, and some of us are very tired of the theory-craft based assertions about how things work only backed up by ancedotes of either people new to the edition not adjusting to it, or people white-rooming things specifically to break them rather than approximate what actually happens in play.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why people have problems with craft is &quot;I know how I envision my character and it doesn't include crafting random small stuff all the time, why is the system actively trying to make me change my character concept for no good reason?&quot; This shouldn't be news given that people have said as much over and over again since the craft system was released. </div> </div> </div>And... as I've already gone into detail, the game doesn't actually do this. &quot;Small&quot; is the same as &quot;insignificant&quot; in this sense. It's just a word that tries to paint Basic projects as &quot;bad&quot; in some fashion, despite the fact that all these concepts would actually do Basic projects all the damned time if played honestly. The only way the system is actually making you change your concept is by letting it be less realistic/etc. and focus on narrative goals, instead of making you focus on what such a concept would actually have to deal with in the technological level of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T15:49:36-07:00","id":1231963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231838#post1231838\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah but this is heroic fantasy so fuck that noise.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't think of a genre that overplays the significance of forging swords more than heroic fantasy does. Even when they're not exactly magical swords.<br /> <br /> Besides, it's still a setting in which people regularly die of cholera.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T15:51:09-07:00","id":1231964,"title":"","content":"Anyway, here's something that is technically a response to something in the Devs thread, but isn't exactly appropriate there, and this is the closest topic to it...<br /> <br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I just can't help but want to register my amusement with the fact that &quot;blueprint&quot; has become a colloquialism for any kind of drawn plan, when in fact the blueprint itself is actually a photographic reproduction produced after the fact, and that method of copying plans has been obsolete for decades.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Especially when you have things like Tom and Jerry cartoons enshrining the image of somebody having an actual sheet of blue paper on which they draw in plans with white ink.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T18:22:40-07:00","id":1232009,"title":"","content":"Why did I even say anything I've never even been all that interested in playing a crafter in any edition of exalted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231940#post1231940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then maybe Exalted isn't your game? Broad concepts are kind of how it rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't usually actively punish you for not rolling with it though. You can f.ex. make a character that's a great musician, take 5 ranks in Performance and simply never do any oratory, acting, or dancing and the system doesn't go out of its way to screw with you. OTOH if you want to make a character that's a great architect/civil-engineer that raises city walls, manses, etc. and isn't into home repairs you're out of luck unless you want to burn 8 charms purchases on getting Supreme Perfection of Craft.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231940#post1231940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And... as I've already gone into detail, the game doesn't actually do this.&quot;Small&quot; is the same as &quot;insignificant&quot; in this sense. It's just a word that tries to paint Basic projects as &quot;bad&quot; in some fashion, despite the fact that all these concepts would actually do Basic projects all the damned time if played honestly.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't see anywhere you've shown that the game doesn't require you to complete basic projects to do major ones. Small mean small, which basic projects are defined as being.<br /> <br /> 'If played honestly' is absurd given that a character concept can 'honestly' be literally anything and includes plenty of concepts that wouldn't do basic projects regardless of what you think makes sense in the setting or the kind of narrative the people who wrote the system want to promote and is basically just saying 'character concepts that don't fit this system are bad and shouldn't be supported'. Given that character concepts that don't do much crafting beside major+ of-screen downtime projects are represented (probably the norm) in previous editions of exalted and most other fantasy RPGs and have worked fine and been enjoyed by lots of people, acting like people that have a problem with a system thats built to be hostile to them just don't understand the system seems disingenuous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2579-limaxophobiac","name":"limaxophobiac","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"294"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T19:26:32-07:00","id":1232020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231940#post1231940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again the lack of consistency here is stagger. &quot;I brought up a lack of mechanical representation, but I didn't mean that this mattered when you pointed out that it was mechanically represented, and instead that the mechanical presentation being there is really a bad thing!&quot; You can cook and boost morale... so every one will want good cooks that can boost morale? And? Every army wants multiple Exalts fighting for them too. Everyone wants the most advantages in something high stakes like war.</div> </div> </div> </div>You aren't following, I guess. I mentioned that it wasn't mechanically represented as a way of noting that you were doing it as a form of color for the character and a source of craft points. Keeping the small army of bureaucrats and quartermasters and such below the level of abstraction is generally desirable to avoid turning your game of Exalted from heroic fantasy into Bronze Age spreadsheet keeping.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not really? I'm just not assuming a pattern. You're approaching this with a set pattern in mind, so filling in the details how you want. Which is fine for something like appreciating an Impressionist painting, but not great for assumptions about how things will play out in an interactive, collaborative, highly structured creative thought exercise.<br /> <br /> To me, the pattern as it forms over time is what makes the character. The actions that bring the stuff on their sheet to life as something fun and interesting emerge from each of those actions. Maybe your character is mercenary, maybe a bleeding heart, or a dedicated loyalist. Who they do these things for, and why, matter. </div> </div> </div>When the pattern that emerges, that people like you tell me is the experience in play, is that crafter characters all try to inject craft into most scenes, I must acknowledge the incentive structure as effective and I must address the question of whether or not that incentive structure is too effective. It appears to be too effective, funneling characters with at least a certain investment in craft into a mindset that doesn't seem to present in other Abilities.<br /> <br /> In ways, collaborative, interactive endeavors are the ones most susceptible to subtle incentives. You've got &quot;Emperor's New Clothes&quot; scenarios, flaws of social signaling, stereotypes, type-casting, etc. Human social behavior is fascinating to study.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why not? If I made a chef, I want that to be part of their screen time. </div> </div> </div>I think you're missing part of the nuance here. When you play your chef character, is he spending more time crafting than he would if there were no craft points? Is your character a bigger craft-nut in practice than you intended him in concept? The evidence seems to point to yes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Gating is gating is gating. Lore is actually worse because it uses XP which actively denies you other things vastly more drastically than crafting actions do. </div> </div> </div>No. If you don't pay attention to the nuances of how the gating is in place, you'll miss things about whether it needs to be in place and also how it can be addressed. Lore gating is resolved off-screen, not on-screen. It also does not provide an incentive to look for ways to inject Lore into the game. Lore gating annoys me, but solving that is a pretty quick and obvious house rule. The craft system, on the other hand, has much to recommend it, but making changes to it results in a cascade of invalidated assumptions because it is much more involved.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the essential problem is you, again, aren't consistent. &quot;This form of gating is OK, but this form isn't, and the reasons are always shifting.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Where have I shifted my reasoning? Are you sure you haven't just read me uncharitably? You do seem to be arguing from a position of trying to defeat me more than a position of trying to reach understanding or to persuade.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The gap doesn't effect my point, so it doesn't matter. You haven't actually established why Craft's gating is bad, but all the other forms of gating are good, in a consistent manner. </div> </div> </div>That invalidates most of this branch of argument. If the gating isn't bad, then why are we comparing gating in other Abilities? Why aren't you making arguments for why the gating is good instead?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why shouldn't a character look at solving problems with their best skills first? </div> </div> </div>You, again, seem to be missing the point. There's nothing wrong with having a character who looks at the world through solving problems with Craft. There's a problem when all characters with more than a casual investment in Craft look at the world through the lens of Craft. It's like the problem Lunars had with Deadly Beastman Transformation, but it's a behavioral optimization instead of a mechanical optimization.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Explicitly not how Craft projects work. You can't turn a higher level project into lots of lower level projects. Otherwise everything would just be a series of Basic projects. </div> </div> </div>You can't break a higher level project down into lower level projects because the higher level projects take skills and require considerations that aren't present in the lower level projects. You might build a doghouse as a Basic project, but a thousand doghouses stacked atop each other isn't a skyscraper, it's a pile of rubble. I can't count the number of times I've watched <i>Forged In Fire </i>and one or both smiths simply aren't used to forging something as long as a sword or as wide as a kopesh.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, the whole setup is kind of weird. How often are people building palaces in your games? It's never happened mundanely in one of mine... but you're fine with that being metered. Why is architecture OK even if it's not really something adventuring heroes are likely to do much of in most iterations of the game? Why can't it be handwaved? The book brings it up? </div> </div> </div>You seem unfamiliar with the concept of infrastructure. When you build an aqueduct, pave a road, dam a river, or erect a fort, you are making future efforts easier. Infrastructure is a compounding factor - the more you have, the easier it is to build more and the more effective it becomes. A road between two cities is only two possible routes improved, but a road connecting three cities is six possible routes improved and connecting four cities improves twelve routes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What's the difference between &quot;interested in doing major things with their craft,&quot; and, &quot;solving a problem with [their] craft?&quot; </div> </div> </div>Allow me to correct your question: &quot;What's the difference between a character interested in doing major things with their craft and a character with a world view of 'How can I solve this problem using my craft?'&quot;. If you leave out the &quot;world view&quot; part, you miss the heart of the matter. Let me give you an example to try to illustrate the difference. We have two blacksmiths, mechanically identical on paper. Their players both want to eventually craft some artifacts. The first adopts a world view of &quot;I solve problems with my craft&quot;. The second adopts the world view of &quot;I solve problems by hitting things with a hammer.&quot; Given a social situation, the first makes a decorated knife as a bribe or a gift, the second berates those standing in his way, bluntly driving his points home. Does this demonstrate the difference?<br /> <br /> You'll note that if both achieve their aim in the social situation, the first is closer to making an artifact than the second.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why is a dot on your sheet there if you don't intend to use it? Or if you don't want to use it and it's just there for flavor, why do you care if the system doesn't let you use it a specific way later?<br /> <br /> This is another point of incoherence. &quot;I don't want to use these dots on my sheet, but it's a problem that the system doesn't let me use them?&quot; Huh? Even in a &quot;big picture&quot; view of not crafting all the time thing, it doesn't make sense. </div> </div> </div>You are asking the wrong question again. I said the dots are on their sheet and they want to use them. They may not want to adopt the world view of &quot;I solve problems with my craft&quot; as their ethos.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And they can! My craft focused character problem solved other ways all the time, because I could Craft plenty and still do other things! The crafting system didn't (and as far as I can tell never actually has) mind control me into always crafting everything all the time. </div> </div> </div>Everything you've told me is that your character's world view is &quot;I solve problems with my craft&quot;. Your character, as far as you have told me, thinks about making something first and foremost when considering solutions to the problem. Incentive structure isn't mind control. It's a slope. It makes it easy to go one way and harder to go another. You keep telling me you go with the flow, so to speak. Because of that, it's going to be hard to pick out which parts are because you consciously decided that would be your character's world view and which parts are because the system made it easy to do things like that and hard to do it in other ways.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It still sounds ridiculous in context. &quot;My chef makes a meal and gets cxp for it, thus is acting out of character inherently,&quot; is an absurd statement, and that's the context. If you acknowledge that it could be in character, then your whole point here falls apart, because there's nothing wrong with characters spotlight time on in-character actions. </div> </div> </div>Look at it back in context:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231720#post1231720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That argument is disingenuous at best. Again, it's not the rolling. It's the screen time. If you have to have your character find ways to insert Craft into a scene so that you can stay on track with your other Craft goals, it feels wrong. Your character is acting out of character.</div> </div> </div> </div>You have a character that wouldn't have done something, but because they need the silver points they inject a craft project into the scene. They are breaking with the character they wanted for mechanical benefit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> OK, but any mechanic to determine if you do or don't have the mental energy to power through is going to be the same thing. If you could innately trade WP for cxp, then it would just happen when you run out of WP instead. If you have it be fiat based, then you're neglecting the importance of the the rules to solve the &quot;I shot you first, no I shot you first,&quot; classic dilemma.<br /> <br /> Player, &quot;I power through!&quot;<br /> ST, &quot;No, you've crafting too much, you're clearly exhausted,&quot;<br /> <br /> The system is there to keep that from happening. </div> </div> </div>Or you skip the problem when it doesn't matter for game balance and say &quot;Go ahead and make that catapult. Yes, you can make a sword. No, you don't have to make a bunch of arrows first.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But this doesn't address the point. As it exists out of game, you're free to explain it in game how you want so it makes sense and doesn't cause out of character actions.<br /> <br /> There's no in game reason I need 7+ Initiative before I can land a killing blow on a target. But we narrate combat such the withering attacks fit the narrative of my character trying to kill their enemy, but fails and builds until they have the base Initiative to try. I could try to constantly narrate things differently and then make it not make sense and cause out of character actions, but why would I want to do that? </div> </div> </div>The Initiative system acknowledges that it is an unrealistic abstraction. It explicitly states that it is there to allow for narratively interesting fights and also prevent the sorts of things that lead to 2e's Paranoia Combat paradigm. It has clear reasoning behind it and even provides its own explanation of how to make it fit smoothly into the story.<br /> <br /> The gating on Major projects doesn't have any of that and it also runs into real game situations where it clearly doesn't make sense - remember the story of the player wanting to build catapults to help defend the besieged town?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Actually, that is, as far as I can tell 100% of your objection. </div> </div> </div>Then either I suck far more at writing than I thought or you should go and reread this argument again. I suspect the later.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only point I made about the way my character looks at the world, is that they know they're awesome at making stuff, and when there's a problem that can be solved with building stuff, they'll consider doing just that. And yes, I think every character with dots in a Skill should have, &quot;I'd consider using this Skill when appropriate,&quot; said about them. </div> </div> </div>Not a point in this argument, a point in the game you were in. Every action your character takes means something. Repeated actions reinforce those meanings. If I were to ask someone in your group how your character would solve a problem, how likely would they be to answer &quot;Probably make something with Craft&quot;?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like most synonyms, yes. But they overlap in context. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Tortured&quot; implies that the logic backing it doesn't work. &quot;Tired&quot; implies the argument has seen lots of use. I'm fine with being accused of using tired arguments - I'm a conservative and it goes with the territory - but being accused of using tortured arguments makes me take a second look at my points to see what I missed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, I'm complaining that you're using dishonest debating tactics, lack of charity in reading, and such things which don't make your arguments strong or clear. Just annoying. </div> </div> </div>Where have I been dishonest?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you acknowledge that you have to &quot;claim home field advantage&quot; by framing the debate in your favor, you're acknowledging that you don't have as strong or clear argument, you need to manipulate the terms because you points aren't good enough on their own. </div> </div> </div>Not really. Everyone wants to frame the debate, even if they have a strong argument. The &quot;LGBT rights&quot; debate was won the moment they managed to frame the debate as the &quot;LGBT rights&quot; debate. The pro-choice/pro-life debate continues to rage partly because both sides presented strong framing to the issue. When you can work inside the frame of reference you choose, you can more easily demonstrate your points because you don't need to rebuild certain values using a different group's premises. You can spend all your time on how your argument descends from the nearer axioms rather than trying to manage a longer chain of logic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except all of your arguments come down to playing the game under the assumption that it will be done in bad faith. Exalted is a game that explicitly has a lot of places that could be abused, but stopped caring about policing that and trusts the players and ST to be there to have fun instead of trying to combat bad actors with the rules. </div> </div> </div>We're discussing one place where the rules are encouraging behavior to go in one direction over another and another place where the rules are explicitly stopping a behavior that doesn't need policed that strongly. Neither of those problems require me to assume bad faith. In fact, the first is more an issue when playing naively, without any understanding that following a carrot can lead you to go too far with a behavior than you intended.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is it though? It might be appropriate for a sword to be made. Or for him to want to make a sword. But there are narratively approrpiate reasons why it might not be approrpiate in the total of the narrative as well. So it's only jarring if the total narrative has no reason for him to not end up making the sword included.<br /> <br /> That's the whole problem with this &quot;problem.&quot; It only happens if you let it happen. If the group has set up no narrative reasons for this situation, how did it even come about? How come they don't have enough sxp and why doesn't that narrative explain things? Why aren't some Basics appropriate to the narrative? </div> </div> </div>Through the magic of hypothetical situations, I can absolutely guarantee that it is narratively appropriate for my character to make a sword here. That is<i> one of the foundational assumptions of the proposed hypothetical. </i>How did this situation happen? Some logical chain of events that I could lay out in way more words than I want to type, probably involving a brand new character in the first scene of play or an experienced character in an unexpected situation just after finishing a planned project.<br /> <br /> You are confirming one of the things that I suspected - you take the system as the given state of affairs. You aren't thinking &quot;What would be appropriate for my character to make here?&quot;. Instead, you are thinking &quot;What Basic Project would be appropriate for my character to make here?&quot;. You don't even think &quot;Should I make something that is considered a Major Project here?&quot; unless you have the craft points for a Major project.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, back to this thing you ignore time and time and time again: what's wrong if a character can't solve this narrative issue with Craft this time if you don't want things to always be solved with Craft? Because you've never, ever, addressed this disconnect. </div> </div> </div>I'll address it again and I'll try to be clearer. You are conflating two separate problems - there's the incentive problem and there's the gating problem. They are inter-related but they aren't the same thing. I object to every character with a solid dose of Craft looking to solve every problem with Craft. It makes the stories of craft characters more homogenous than I think is healthy for the game. At the same time, I also object to a mundane character action being arbitrarily barred from my character's repertoire without a compelling reason. Part of the reason the incentive problem is so pernicious is because the behaviors you need to adopt to avoid the gating problem are reinforced by the incentive problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, but you do end up sounding condescending and like you know you're actually wrong but can't admit it with underhanded bullshit where you play psychic (but you don't read minds!) </div> </div> </div>You just barely did just what I predicted not one quote block previous in your own post. Here, look at it again:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231940#post1231940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it though? It might be appropriate for a sword to be made. Or for him to want to make a sword. But there are narratively approrpiate reasons why it might not be approrpiate in the total of the narrative as well. So it's only jarring if the total narrative has no reason for him to not end up making the sword included.<br /> <br /> That's the whole problem with this &quot;problem.&quot; It only happens if you let it happen. If the group has set up no narrative reasons for this situation, how did it even come about? How come they don't have enough sxp and why doesn't that narrative explain things? Why aren't some Basics appropriate to the narrative?</div> </div> </div> </div>To paraphrase: &quot;Why is it most appropriate for your character to make a sword? Can't you just make something else? Why will only a sword do?&quot;<br /> Before you tell me my predictions are wrong and condescending, make sure you haven't just proved me correct right before doing so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I followed the whole thing. It's just illogical nonsense in my estimation. </div> </div> </div>If you find it illogical, then obviously there's a disconnect. If you can't be more specific than &quot;the whole thing is illogical&quot;, then I can't refine my model. If I can't refine my model, I certainly can't see things your way and you certainly can't see things mine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't feel from this thread, or past ones, that you give a single shit about what I'm thinking; especially since you keep assuming what I'm thinking in fairly annoying ways. </div> </div> </div>If I didn't care what you thought, I'd just mute you and move on with my life. I wouldn't put this much effort into arguing. You've been quite helpful so far, actually. You've allowed me to refine a few of my arguments a little bit and you've provided some additional insight into the disjunct between people who think like me and people who think like you.<br /> <br /> As for assuming what you are thinking, of course I have to do that. I can't read your thoughts and you certainly don't have the time to tell me all of what you are thinking, so I have to build a model on assumptions. If my assumptions are annoying, then that's just a fault of imperfect data and flawed human cognition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game cares about more than balance. So, no. </div> </div> </div>Assertions are not arguments. What aspect of mono-crafters runs counter to a value the game cares about?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then maybe Exalted isn't your game? Broad concepts are kind of how it rolls. </div> </div> </div>Since when has &quot;bureaucratic fate ninja troubleshooter&quot; been a broad concept? Yet we have Sidereals. Exalted rolls with concepts at multiple levels of abstraction. We even have two (or more) different Abilities for punching people in the head!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you feel that way. Come back to me when you feel like calling out everyone instead of one person when you jump in the middle of stuff. But this isn't the first Craft debate rodeo, and some of us are very tired of the theory-craft based assertions about how things work only backed up by ancedotes of either people new to the edition not adjusting to it, or people white-rooming things specifically to break them rather than approximate what actually happens in play. </div> </div> </div>That's a level of bitter that I haven't been in a long time. It explains a lot of how this thread has gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231964#post1231964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, here's something that is technically a response to something in the Devs thread, but isn't exactly appropriate there, and this is the closest topic to it...<br /> <br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I just can't help but want to register my amusement with the fact that &quot;blueprint&quot; has become a colloquialism for any kind of drawn plan, when in fact the blueprint itself is actually a photographic reproduction produced after the fact, and that method of copying plans has been obsolete for decades.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Especially when you have things like Tom and Jerry cartoons enshrining the image of somebody having an actual sheet of blue paper on which they draw in plans with white ink.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div>That is something that I didn't know. I always had wondered why architectural drawings were done in white ink on blue paper.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T19:36:04-07:00","id":1232022,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>limaxophobiac</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232009#post1232009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't usually actively punish you for not rolling with it though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it does, just not evenly. The more systems associated with a concept, the more the game works poorly if you don't allow for an amount of broadness to your concept.<br /> <br /> So things like Performance and Bureaucracy, which have exceedingly little mechanical systems to deal with, are fairly free of much pressure because they lack systems to pressure them with.<br /> <br /> Combat focused concepts require quite of bit of broadness to be successful. If you try to do everything in combat with just Melee... you're going to get &quot;punished&quot; for neglecting Awareness, Athletics, Dodge, etc. as you face any fight that isn't just people standing next to each other swinging weapons until someone falls down.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> OTOH if you want to make a character that's a great architect/civil-engineer that raises city walls, manses, etc. and isn't into home repairs you're out of luck unless you want to burn 8 charms purchases on getting Supreme Perfection of Craft. </div> </div> </div>If you haven't noticed we're avoiding getting into the &quot;Solar Craft Charms sucks&quot; thing as much as possible? Because that's one of the few consensus points in all this. While not everyone agrees with how to address it, people that love the 3e crafting rules as is, to people that want it to die in a fire and erase it from memory, all tend to agree that the Solar Craft Charms make the situation worse, and are sub-par in a number of ways.<br /> <br /> Also, a civil engineer is basically not something that falls into the crafting system (civil engineers are planners, not builders), but rather the leadership rules (not that I feel that's a good thing... but it's where the rules fall). <br /> <br /> And, of course, Bronze Age technological craftspeople that build big architectural structures don't just build palaces/city walls/etc. Not everyone seeking to commission their work is after something so large and so elaborate. &quot;I built walls for my city that will last a generation! Oh, well, I guess that puts my city wall building business in jeopardy because my ruler isn't going to want me building other cities' walls, and they don't need me to build them a new wall again for a few decades... I'm going to have to find other stuff to do....&quot; Or your character is constantly doing repairs (and repairs are allowed to be lower ranked projects) to maintain their giant edifices for their patrons; also being a steady flow of cxp in the process.<br /> <br /> One of the most famous architects in the inspirational sources for Exalted is someone most people have no idea was an architect: Daedalus. Because Daedalus' most associated work are the wings he made for Icarus, not as the architect of the Labyrinth (aka how he and his son ended up in the situation needing those wings in the first place).<br /> <br /> So, no, I'm not terribly sympathetic to, &quot;I want to make a character that's not really in line with the setting or the inspirational source material of the game, and I refuse to adapt the concept to make them more so.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Small mean small, which basic projects are defined as being. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Small&quot; is never used in the text to define a Basic project. There are Basic project examples that are bigger than Major project examples in every sense of the words. Spears are physically larger than swords. The process of annealing, shaping, thermocycling, quenching, and tempering a knife with hand tools is going to be a bigger time commitment than cooking a banquet for a forty people (largely by necessity since cooking without a fridge has to be done quickly to avoid food spoiling, especially with things like presentation; you don't want your decorative greens wilting before the plates are even served). Etc.<br /> <br /> But my stance is that the definitions of projects is the most fundamental problem with the basic crafting rules, and the part that fixing does the most good doing, since it can reduce/mitigate/eliminate the issues with the objectives and gating if handled. So tearing into my arguments on why that's the case instead of the gating issue with the argument about how the projects are defined as your base point isn't really going to go far.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 'If played honestly' is absurd... </div> </div> </div>Strongly stated? Yes. Absurd. No. The whole &quot;preparation&quot; section informs concepts as part of a group agreement on what an individual instance of the game will be about, and what concepts will fit, and the standards that concepts will be held to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...is basically just saying 'character concepts that don't fit this system are bad and shouldn't be supported'. </div> </div> </div>No, it's saying that making a concept at odds with the intentions of the game isn't a problem with the game.<br /> <br /> If a player comes to your Exalted game and insists on playing a helicopter pilot. They refuse to pilot some fantasy flying ornithopter. They refuse to accept armaments that aren't gunpowder based.<br /> <br /> What do you do? You tell them if they can't adapt the concept, or pick a new one, they can't play in your Exalted game. Because there's not bad about the concept, it's just wrong for the game, and an unwillingness to adapt it is a clear sign to not continue further. You can play with that person when you're running something set where helicopters exist.<br /> <br /> Exalted shouldn't support every concept. It's not that they're bad, just that they're wrong for the game. Save them for games they work in, or adjust them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Given that character concepts that don't do much crafting beside major+ of-screen downtime projects are represented (probably the norm) in previous editions of exalted and most other fantasy RPGs and have worked fine and been enjoyed by lots of people, acting like people that have a problem with a system thats built to be hostile to them just don't understand the system seems disingenuous. </div> </div> </div>Other editions/systems have generally just shafted crafting focused characters by making crafting either just handwaved away and therefore annoying to want to play, or given exceedingly crappy systems (Exalted 2e, charging XP in certain editions of D&amp;D, etc...) that just make crafting mind numbingly boring where the only reason you didn't see the equivalent of Basic projects made much in-play is because you could make an Artifact mechanical horse with barely any more effort than making a horseshoe.<br /> <br /> Of course, I've played crafters in all three editions of Exalted, so having one that's actually says, &quot;playing a Craft focused character is something we want you to do!&quot; instead of, &quot;Craft is something you do between sessions because only people that want to bore the rest of the group do crafting in-game&quot; is something I highly enjoy as a change.<br /> <br /> And, the strawmaning aside there, my issue is not that people don't understand the system, but that they understand it and choose that they want to fight the system for what I consider an incredibly selfish reason in a group activity to be entirely unappealing.<br /> <br /> If people actually care about playing RPGs, they're used to adapting their concepts to the game, and their group. So I do not buy, &quot;I have a concept that will only ever craft [Major/Superior project] and refuses to ever do lower ranked projects,&quot; as something to abide in my fellow players. It's not fun for any of us to deal with such stubbornness for something that's solved so simply.<br /> <br /> I kicked someone out of an Exalted 1e game because he threw a hissy fit over his diaklaive getting stolen and refused to play without his favored weapon because &quot;it is his concept.&quot; It wasn't that it was gone for good, it was to introduce an NPC that was testing the PCs who would have given it back (or gotten killed and they'd have found it then, whatever). I'm not an ass that just takes toys away at a whim. But if you were dealing with that, would you tolerate it? Because that's what &quot;I only craft city walls&quot; is to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T21:39:01-07:00","id":1232047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232020#post1232020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mentioned that it wasn't mechanically represented as a way of noting that you were doing it as a form of color for the character and a source of craft points.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which I also addressed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When the pattern that emerges, that people like you tell me is the experience in play, is that crafter characters all try to inject craft into most scenes... </div> </div> </div>I have said no such thing. This is a lie, pure and simple.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think you're missing part of the nuance here. </div> </div> </div>Nuance doesn't mean, &quot;you're not including the falsehoods I'm injecting into the conversations as if they were true.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you play your chef character, is he spending more time crafting than he would if there were no craft points? </div> </div> </div>Nope.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is your character a bigger craft-nut in practice than you intended him in concept? </div> </div> </div><br /> Nope<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The evidence seems to point to yes. </div> </div> </div>Bullshit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lore gating is resolved off-screen, not on-screen. </div> </div> </div>Resolving the gate is. The gate is present on-screen until then. There is no on-screen difference between wanting to use Lore but not having an appropriate specialty, and wanting to craft a Major project and not having the sxp. In that moment, both are blocked from doing the thing they want to do. The nuances don't change that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also does not provide an incentive to look for ways to inject Lore into the game. </div> </div> </div>3e turned Lore from an Ability the people in my groups only took because of prerequistes that demanded it to do other things, to one of the most used Abilities in non-combat situations in the game (and even used in combat at least three times to help take down nasty critters by Loring in weaknesses when brute force wasn't working). The utility of the new Lore system absolutely increased the on-screen time spent by our two Lore heavy characters seeking out ways of justifying new Lore specialties. Pretty much any time we got into a library/similar source of information, they immediately wanted to read up and see if a new specialty was in their future or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where have I shifted my reasoning? </div> </div> </div>Constantly? You've repeatedly jumped from verisimilitude, to game balance, to narratives, to player desires, to &quot;big picture&quot; to whatever. There's no expressed consistent stance, just whatever you feel undermines counterpoints the best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You do seem to be arguing from a position of trying to defeat me more than a position of trying to reach understanding or to persuade. </div> </div> </div>At this point? I'm mostly just defending my stance because I think other people value it and it should be. I don't think you want to understand me, so there's no point in me trying to reach and understanding with you. If you want to change my mind on that... you need to seriously reconsider some of your posting choices.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why aren't you making arguments for why the gating is good instead? </div> </div> </div>Because, if you were trying to understand me and all, you'd know that my position is that gating isn't the issue I have with the system, so I don't need to defend it to keep my point consistent. I don't need to argue the virtues of gating to make the point that the level of gating isn't bad, and isn't the fundamental problem to address.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's nothing wrong with having a character who looks at the world through solving problems with Craft. </div> </div> </div>But you keep saying this exact thing is bad! That's like.... your most common statement about this. That the game is wrong for incentivizing this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a problem when all characters with more than a casual investment in Craft look at the world through the lens of Craft. It's like the problem Lunars had with Deadly Beastman Transformation, but it's a behavioral optimization instead of a mechanical optimization. </div> </div> </div>Comparing 3e craft to DBT makes no sense. DBT (which DBT anyway?)'s problems are numerous and stemmed from the lack of vision for what the heck Lunars are supposed to be. Crafting, regardless of likes or dislikes, is not born of slapping together WtA with Exalted and not thinking it through hard enough. Your whole argument rests on crafting being designed too strongly for a purpose, not being haphazard.<br /> <br /> And this doesn't change the fundamental issue of your argument. All characters are going to consider interacting with the world on the grounds of their best skills. Craft isn't unique, and the incentives don't change that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't break a higher level project down into lower level projects because the higher level projects take skills and require considerations that aren't present in the lower level projects. </div> </div> </div>Citation needed.<br /> <br /> And this logic doesn't really work. The definitions of the projects don't include this. If you're a <i>Forged in Fire</i> fan, why can't creating a billet, shaping it into a sword, hardening and tempering, fit and finish, be five Basic projects for a skilled bladesmith instead of one Major project? All those skills are needed for a Basic project knife, so that's not the problem. All those considerations are there for a knife too, even if things get harder with a sword because of the size.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You seem unfamiliar with the concept of infrastructure. </div> </div> </div>Not really? You're not addressing the question (why is architecture OK, but these other things not, when the frequency in play seems similar) by talking about the importance of infrastructure.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, infrastructure is exactly why these things would stay Superior projects in my proposed house rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Allow me to correct your question: </div> </div> </div>Stop doing this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;What's the difference between a character interested in doing major things with their craft and a character with a world view of 'How can I solve this problem using my craft?'&quot;. </div> </div> </div>That's not my question. The question I asked is my question, and the one I think needs to be answered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you leave out the &quot;world view&quot; part, you miss the heart of the matter. </div> </div> </div>Or, rather, if you'd acknowledge that the &quot;world view&quot; thing is something you've inserted for the sole purposes of controlling the frame of the discussion instead of honestly engaging with it, you'd have to acknowledge your argument is much, much weaker than you think. The world view thing is a false dichotomy where characters have to exist in one of two states: either they don't look at the world in terms of crafting at all, or they're obsessed and it's all they see. My shipwright, was definitely a craft-nut (Craft Supernal and all), but when it came to defending his crew (can you guess what one of his Intimacies was and which Charm he might have used it with a lot?), Brawl tended to take over his &quot;world view&quot; because beating the shit out of people that threatened his crew mattered more than building a clever contraption. Characters are complicated like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You'll note that if both achieve their aim in the social situation, the first is closer to making an artifact than the second. </div> </div> </div>The first is practicing their crafting skills, and the second isn't. Why shouldn't they be closer to making an artifact? Improving your smithing is something that comes with lots of practice and effort. One character is doing that as abstracted by them using crafts to move things forward, the other isn't.<br /> <br /> If that's all the difference is, I'm not seeing the problem. Both are doing something, one related it to a secondary goal and advanced both goals, one didn't, and thus only advanced one goal.<br /> <br /> Two mechanically identical characters are in a social situation to get people to recognize their academic skills. One uses a soft approach and not only impresses people, but gets access to a library, and is now also closer to a new Lore specialty. The other berates people with their superior knowledge and does impress people with their mastery of the subject of the symposium, but doesn't endear themselves to the community. They don't get any closer to access to a special library and justification for a new Lore specialty.<br /> <br /> That's just how shit works. Characters that take advantage to advance multiple goals when the opportunity presents itself get farther than those that insist on avoiding the chance to do so. This is far from an issue unique to Craft, and certainly not one the Craft system alone incentivizes.<br /> <br /> An assassin that kills a king, and one that kills a guard both successfully killed someone, to vastly different results. That's what <b>should</b> happen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I said the dots are on their sheet and they want to use them. </div> </div> </div>OK, I misread that one a bit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They may not want to adopt the world view of &quot;I solve problems with my craft&quot; as their ethos. </div> </div> </div>Again though, there's no effective difference between, &quot;I want to do major things with my craft,&quot; and, &quot;I solve problems with my craft,&quot; besides your framing attempts. The &quot;ethos&quot; is just how frequently they want to do it. This goes right back to, &quot;if you want a character that doesn't craft a lot, why is it a problem that the game makes it so that characters that don't craft a lot, can't craft a lot?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Everything you've told me is that your character's world view is &quot;I solve problems with my craft&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Again, this is a lie. I've said repeated things to the contrary of this before this post. You're just being dishonest about this because the idea that my character can be more complicated isn't something your frame can withstand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You have a character that wouldn't have done something, but because they need the silver points they inject a craft project into the scene. </div> </div> </div>Your hypothetical strawman isn't my character, and doesn't reflect my views, experiences, or any of that. Don't try to shove that dishonest crap on me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or you skip the problem when it doesn't matter for game balance and say &quot;Go ahead and make that catapult. Yes, you can make a sword. No, you don't have to make a bunch of arrows first.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Again, why is game balance the only consideration here? This is what I mean about consistency. You're happy to bring up other design issues... right up until they would be an impediment to your argument. Then things get tossed aside. I frankly do not want to go back to arguing with my ST about exactly how much effort it takes to build a catapult when they say &quot;no&quot; instead of yes. Those arguments suck. Having a system in place gets rid of those arguments. Throwing it back to ST fiat doesn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The gating on Major projects doesn't have any of that and it also runs into real game situations where it clearly doesn't make sense - remember the story of the player wanting to build catapults to help defend the besieged town? </div> </div> </div>The book not having enough essays on Craft doesn't prove anything besides the book not doing more explaining when it should have.<br /> <br /> And one early complaint from when people were still getting used to the system which gets repeatedly endlessly shouldn't trump people with years of experience actually playing the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I suspect the later. </div> </div> </div>Shocking, but indicative of your dishonesty regarding actually wanting to understand my position. If you want to understand me, assuming I'm at fault for unclear communications doesn't really help, does it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I were to ask someone in your group how your character would solve a problem, how likely would they be to answer &quot;Probably make something with Craft&quot;? </div> </div> </div>Nope. They'd most likely say something like, &quot;Depends on the situation.&quot; because that's how my character solved problems: with his best skill for the task at hand. That and my crafting did exceedingly little &quot;problem solving,&quot; and a lot more, &quot;facilitating the group solving the problem together.&quot; Making a small river boat to avoid open sail on the sea or riding a cloud up in the sky where everyone could see us, but moving faster than we could as a group didn't really solve a problem (we could have walked too) just made things easier to get to the bigger problem (getting behind enemy lines to capture a spy).<br /> <br /> Because again, the whole &quot;world view/ethos&quot; thing is crap. Did that character craft a bunch? Yes! Why do you think I made a Supernal Craft character? Do I think my ST or players would have ever felt I was artificially shoehorning in projects, derailing stuff to craft, or only consider crafting to handle things? N.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Tortured&quot; implies that the logic backing it doesn't work. </div> </div> </div>No, it implies that you've bent and twisted the facts to fit your logic (hence you've tortured things), in this case I mean you've done so by repetition and reinforcing biases (hence overlapping with tired). Tortured arguments tend to problems because the assumptions are forced, not that the base logic is wouldn't work with better assumptions. Again, can you stop with the definition police thing? You keep mucking it up.<br /> <br /> [quote]Where have I been dishonest?[/quote<br /> <br /> I've tried to be more direct about it to be more clear.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not really. Everyone wants to frame the debate, even if they have a strong argument. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Wants to&quot; and &quot;does to win,&quot; aren't the same thing. I might want to, but I'm fighting the urge as much as possible and just trying to push back to neutral, rather than going with it despite the negative impact on the conversation. And please, lets keep real world politics out of this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you can work inside the frame of reference you choose, you can more easily demonstrate your points because you don't need to rebuild certain values using a different group's premises. </div> </div> </div>If you don't care about a different group's premises, you don't care about understanding them do you?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Neither of those problems require me to assume bad faith. </div> </div> </div>Continuing to assert a problem that arouse during &quot;naive&quot; play will come up in play with more understanding does, indeed, require you to assume in bad faith. When you repeatedly say things like my examples show my characters to be &quot;crafting everything world viewers&quot; you're assuming in bad faith, because you're not actually taking what I say at face value, or applying deeper clarification when provided. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Through the magic of hypothetical situations,... </div> </div> </div>Exactly. Hypothetical situations don't prove shit in the face of experience. They're the tools of illusionists in this context, creating what they want the audience to see, not about relating truth about the topic. You're not describing a problem in the game, you're describing a problem if your perfectly construct an absurd situation and wand wave away the absurdity of it to call it logical. That does not lead to good house rules. That doesn't indicate a good grounding in what would improve the system. It just prolongs arguments because it lets you assert whatever you want in a white-room of context you construct that has no direct correlation to playing the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ,,,an experienced character in an unexpected situation just after finishing a planned project. </div> </div> </div>Sounds like a character that might need to recharge before crafting another sword to me!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You aren't thinking &quot;What would be appropriate for my character to make here?&quot;. Instead, you are thinking &quot;What Basic Project would be appropriate for my character to make here?&quot;. You don't even think &quot;Should I make something that is considered a Major Project here?&quot; unless you have the craft points for a Major project. </div> </div> </div>Nope. Stop lying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'll address it again and I'll try to be clearer. You are conflating two separate problems - there's the incentive problem and there's the gating problem. They are inter-related but they aren't the same thing. I object to every character with a solid dose of Craft looking to solve every problem with Craft. It makes the stories of craft characters more homogenous than I think is healthy for the game. At the same time, I also object to a mundane character action being arbitrarily barred from my character's repertoire without a compelling reason. Part of the reason the incentive problem is so pernicious is because the behaviors you need to adopt to avoid the gating problem are reinforced by the incentive problem. </div> </div> </div>And this is all completely lacking in reasons to consider it accurate to the play of the game. The &quot;every problem with Craft&quot; only exists in white-room hypothetical theory-crafting, not actual play, meaning there isn't an actual incentive problem that reinforces and issues with the gating issue. The gating issue is, of course, completely overstated. Yes, I appreciate the complaint, but because of the inter-relating issues, the gating complaint is tainted by the falsehood of the incentive complaint. Though, again, I don't think your phrasing of the gating complaint is actually sufficient. &quot;Mundane&quot; isn't enough of a qualifier to state that gating/pacing is inappropriate. Nor is, &quot;but I want to make the thing.&quot; <br /> <br /> This is where not having consistency comes into play. It ignores things like verisimilitude (people in Creation without magic don't pump out swords, building a city's walls could be a mortals single greatest project of their entire lives) that are helped with pacing controls. It ignores things like using the system to arbitrate to cut down on ST/player arguments. Etc. If you consistently applied one metric, this falls apart. Because you're citing two here alone. If you craft a bigger picture model, you actually have to stick to it, and can't cherry pick which considerations matter when.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You just barely did just what I predicted not one quote block previous in your own post. </div> </div> </div>Nope.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To paraphrase: </div> </div> </div>You're continuing to be very bad at this... I'm hoping it's on purpose considering the other option, however frustrating that is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Why is it most appropriate for your character to make a sword? Can't you just make something else? Why will only a sword do?&quot; </div> </div> </div>Not accurate to what I said. My point - and continuing form the &quot;fuck hypotheticals&quot; thing - with those questions is that without the context of how you got to a situation, the limitations being appropriate or not appropriate are impossible to judge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Before you tell me my predictions are wrong and condescending, make sure you haven't just proved me correct right before doing so. </div> </div> </div>Sure thing, since we haven't gotten to this yet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I didn't care what you thought, I'd just mute you and move on with my life. </div> </div> </div>If you say so. I don't find this a convincing statement that your intentions are what you say they are. Life experience says people will keep arguing and debating stuff, often in long form, even if they don't care about what the other side things.<br /> <br /> So, while you're refining things, you can also work on refining your approach too?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for assuming what you are thinking, of course I have to do that. </div> </div> </div>On some level, yes, but you're well beyond the base-line level of assumptions based on the imperfections of human communication.<br /> <br /> You can do more asking, and more paying attention to the responses... because you do things like the &quot;well I only hear your character is the world view of craft all problems&quot; and you're assuming more than necessary to communicate, and hampering communication as addressed above.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What aspect of mono-crafters runs counter to a value the game cares about? </div> </div> </div>Making Craft an Ability that is actively used in-play instead of being regulated to downtime. Crafting in-play requires flexibility in what you'll craft rather than being dead set on only crafting one thing, or it doesn't really get the desired result.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since when has &quot;bureaucratic fate ninja troubleshooter&quot; been a broad concept? </div> </div> </div>It's a pretty broad concept to me... Sidereals demonstrates exactly how broad it really is, not the opposite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We even have two (or more) different Abilities for punching people in the head! </div> </div> </div>Do you really want to get into the Brawl vs. MA debate here?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's a level of bitter that I haven't been in a long time. </div> </div> </div>Frustration, not bitterness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T01:45:48-07:00","id":1232069,"title":"","content":"Hey, can anyone here point me to a good homebrew social system? I hate how the one in the book makes you shoehorn social actions into every scene just to make sure you can do other ones later, regardless of whether it\u2019s in-character for you to be doing social actions. Every time you don\u2019t instill someone or read their intentions you\u2019re missing a chance to persuade them later, which makes every social character look the same; they approach every scene through the worldview of \u201cHow can I use social influence here?\u201d There\u2019s a problem when all characters with more than a casual investment in social abilities look at the world through the lens of social abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T08:02:57-07:00","id":1232122,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> I just want you to know that your side of this bitter and lengthy argument has made me reconsider my stance on the craft system and actually consider playing a character with dots in craft. I was very much stuck in the hypothetical mindset previously but your description of craft in play has made me quite hopeful. Just figured you'd want to know you're accomplishing <i>something</i> from the time you've sunk into this thread.<br /> <br /> On another note, I'm also homebrewing up some Alchemicals. Does anyone have some suggestions on how to write craft charms that aren't just boring cxp generators and/or slot charms? Or point to a good thread for re-written solar craft charms?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T08:11:48-07:00","id":1232127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232047#post1232047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nuance doesn't mean, &quot;you're not including the falsehoods I'm injecting into the conversations as if they were true.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>What you think you say and what I think I hear aren't necessarily the same thing. That doesn't mean anyone is lying. Accusing someone of arguing in bad faith is a great way to make sure the debate is unproductive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3e turned Lore from an Ability the people in my groups only took because of prerequistes that demanded it to do other things, to one of the most used Abilities in non-combat situations in the game (and even used in combat at least three times to help take down nasty critters by Loring in weaknesses when brute force wasn't working). The utility of the new Lore system absolutely increased the on-screen time spent by our two Lore heavy characters seeking out ways of justifying new Lore specialties. Pretty much any time we got into a library/similar source of information, they immediately wanted to read up and see if a new specialty was in their future or not. </div> </div> </div>That's because 3e added new things you can do with Lore, not because it added a gating mechanic to Lore.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Constantly? You've repeatedly jumped from verisimilitude, to game balance, to narratives, to player desires, to &quot;big picture&quot; to whatever. There's no expressed consistent stance, just whatever you feel undermines counterpoints the best. </div> </div> </div>Narrow it down to something you can point to. If I'm doing that unconsciously, I'd like to know about it. If it's just a case of &quot;two things can be true at the same time&quot;, I'd like to explain it better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think you want to understand me, so there's no point in me trying to reach and understanding with you. If you want to change my mind on that... you need to seriously reconsider some of your posting choices. </div> </div> </div>How so?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because, if you were trying to understand me and all, you'd know that my position is that gating isn't the issue I have with the system, so I don't need to defend it to keep my point consistent. I don't need to argue the virtues of gating to make the point that the level of gating isn't bad, and isn't the fundamental problem to address. </div> </div> </div>You keep making arguments that there is nothing wrong with the gating. That has made me think you are defending the gating mechanic and would like to keep it. If that is incorrect, I apologize for the confusion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But you keep saying this exact thing is bad! That's like.... your most common statement about this. That the game is wrong for incentivizing this. </div> </div> </div>The game is wrong for incentivizing that world view in every character. Having it in the occasional character is fine. Having it consume all characters is bad.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Comparing 3e craft to DBT makes no sense. DBT (which DBT anyway?)'s problems are numerous and stemmed from the lack of vision for what the heck Lunars are supposed to be. Crafting, regardless of likes or dislikes, is not born of slapping together WtA with Exalted and not thinking it through hard enough. Your whole argument rests on crafting being designed too strongly for a purpose, not being haphazard. </div> </div> </div>The problem with DBT (either edition, but I've been told it was way worse in 1st ed.) was that it was an option that was too far above other options. You always wanted to take it if combat was your character's thing. Why did every character in 2e have a perfect defense? Same reason. The option was just too good not to have at your disposal. It doesn't matter which flawed philosophy lead to One True Build syndrome, the problem exists regardless. Crafters all approaching the wold like McGuyver is a One True Build issue, but it's one that shows up in play behavior rather than on a character sheet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And this doesn't change the fundamental issue of your argument. All characters are going to consider interacting with the world on the grounds of their best skills. Craft isn't unique, and the incentives don't change that. </div> </div> </div>If you think an incentive doesn't shift behavior, you need to go read some psychology or economics. Characters have multiple &quot;best skills&quot;, but Craft pays you to put it on the screen. If you want to hit all three objectives, it requires you to put it on the screen.<br /> <br /> Back in 2e, you could technically do the same sort of stuff with Craft that you can do in 3e. Did you play Craft-heavy characters the same way? Were you rolling Craft in every scene? Were you looking for little things you could do with Craft? Were you building gifts and bribes? Or did the change in editions change how you played a crafter?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Citation needed. </div> </div> </div>I provided examples. Those are citations. Or have you managed to build a skyscraper by piling up a thousand dog houses?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And this logic doesn't really work. The definitions of the projects don't include this. If you're a <i>Forged in Fire</i> fan, why can't creating a billet, shaping it into a sword, hardening and tempering, fit and finish, be five Basic projects for a skilled bladesmith instead of one Major project? All those skills are needed for a Basic project knife, so that's not the problem. All those considerations are there for a knife too, even if things get harder with a sword because of the size. </div> </div> </div>Because those things are different for a knife and a sword? You are having to maintain an edge profile along a much longer surface, long enough that you need to reheat every six to eight inches, which interrupts your rhythm and requires you to find and continue from your last position without getting off track. Hardening and tempering present much greater problems because of the greater number of places where the blade can warp and also because it is harder to maintain an even temperature along the longer length of metal. If you hilt a sword like you do a knife, you are going to have the balance weighted towards the blade, making it more suited to chopping when that might not be what you want. You have all the problems that a knife does and more. It's not that each element simply takes more skill to pull off.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not really? You're not addressing the question (why is architecture OK, but these other things not, when the frequency in play seems similar) by talking about the importance of infrastructure.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, infrastructure is exactly why these things would stay Superior projects in my proposed house rules. </div> </div> </div>The vast majority of architectural projects are infrastructure of some kind. That's why I think gating the frequency of mundane Superior projects is important.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's not my question. The question I asked is my question, and the one I think needs to be answered. </div> </div> </div>I was being charitable. If the question you asked is the question you wanted me to answer, then your question is either the start of a straw man or else the start of a red herring. I don't see much difference between doing significant things with Craft and solving problems with Craft.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or, rather, if you'd acknowledge that the &quot;world view&quot; thing is something you've inserted for the sole purposes of controlling the frame of the discussion instead of honestly engaging with it, you'd have to acknowledge your argument is much, much weaker than you think. The world view thing is a false dichotomy where characters have to exist in one of two states: either they don't look at the world in terms of crafting at all, or they're obsessed and it's all they see. My shipwright, was definitely a craft-nut (Craft Supernal and all), but when it came to defending his crew (can you guess what one of his Intimacies was and which Charm he might have used it with a lot?), Brawl tended to take over his &quot;world view&quot; because beating the shit out of people that threatened his crew mattered more than building a clever contraption. Characters are complicated like that. </div> </div> </div>You are engaging in the fallacy of the excluded middle here and projecting it onto me. I don't appreciate it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The first is practicing their crafting skills, and the second isn't. Why shouldn't they be closer to making an artifact? Improving your smithing is something that comes with lots of practice and effort. One character is doing that as abstracted by them using crafts to move things forward, the other isn't.<br /> <br /> If that's all the difference is, I'm not seeing the problem. Both are doing something, one related it to a secondary goal and advanced both goals, one didn't, and thus only advanced one goal. </div> </div> </div>You don't see the problem caused by dangling a carrot in front of one method of problem-solving? How it will lead to more of the one and less of the other?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This goes right back to, &quot;if you want a character that doesn't craft a lot, why is it a problem that the game makes it so that characters that don't craft a lot, can't craft a lot?&quot; </div> </div> </div>Because it locks the things they want to craft behind a wall until they craft a lot?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your hypothetical strawman isn't my character, and doesn't reflect my views, experiences, or any of that. Don't try to shove that dishonest crap on me. </div> </div> </div>Your failure at reading comprehension does not make my statement a straw man. The character I was referring to is a hypothetical character, not your actual character. You've made this mistake twice now over the same quote. I do realize I did switch from the generic &quot;you&quot; to the generic plural &quot;they&quot;, but that really shouldn't have tripped you up this badly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, why is game balance the only consideration here? This is what I mean about consistency. You're happy to bring up other design issues... right up until they would be an impediment to your argument. Then things get tossed aside. I frankly do not want to go back to arguing with my ST about exactly how much effort it takes to build a catapult when they say &quot;no&quot; instead of yes. Those arguments suck. Having a system in place gets rid of those arguments. Throwing it back to ST fiat doesn't. </div> </div> </div>We're operating in a heroic fantasy game. Much of the slogging is abstracted out of the game for nearly everything. Weapons don't need oiled or cleaned to prevent rust. Armor doesn't need repaired after battle. You don't have to track your characters income down to the last dinar, nor their expenses. You don't have to explicitly state you went to get some food in-between scenes. You certainly don't inform the group about using the latrine. So on and so forth. Abstraction lets you get to the FUN of the game rather than bog down in tedium like making nails or carving buttons. That's why a gating mechanic without recourse to a legitimate game balance concern is a bad thing. It limits character options arbitrarily.<br /> <br /> You are getting the strands of this argument confused. I made the remark about why even have the craft system if you don't intend to make artifacts in reference to other things. Even then, I doubt you ever had to argue about how much effort it takes to build a catapult in past editions. The Storyteller probably said &quot;It's Craft[Wood], difficulty 3, it will take an hour&quot; and you rolled for it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And one early complaint from when people were still getting used to the system which gets repeatedly endlessly shouldn't trump people with years of experience actually playing the system. </div> </div> </div>All of the &quot;years of experience&quot; still don't demonstrate why that character shouldn't be able to build catapults when lives are literally on the line. That's why it gets to be a trump card.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Shocking, but indicative of your dishonesty regarding actually wanting to understand my position. If you want to understand me, assuming I'm at fault for unclear communications doesn't really help, does it? </div> </div> </div>Well, when I go back and read the statements that you misunderstood and reflect back on previous times when I have made similar arguments and had them understood, I am left with the weight of evidence pointing at the failure to communicate being on your end. It's possible that I was talking to people who were on my same wavelength before and so they were more able to understand me than the general population, but I have no evidence to suggest they were unique in such a way. Reality is not always kind.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, it implies that you've bent and twisted the facts to fit your logic (hence you've tortured things), </div> </div> </div>You say &quot;No&quot;, then restate the definition I gave. I like your restatement a bit better. More evocative language.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you don't care about a different group's premises, you don't care about understanding them do you? </div> </div> </div>There's a difference between understanding their premises and wanting to use them as a baseline. I can understand why people like to use coconut milk in their cooking, but that doesn't mean I want to use it in mine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Continuing to assert a problem that arouse during &quot;naive&quot; play will come up in play with more understanding does, indeed, require you to assume in bad faith. </div> </div> </div>If the problem stops arising in &quot;experienced&quot; play, then behaviors have changed because of the system. I look at the changes and say &quot;Why are you routing around this issue? Why are you making what the system tells you to make instead of making what you want to make?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Exactly. Hypothetical situations don't prove shit in the face of experience. They're the tools of illusionists in this context, creating what they want the audience to see, not about relating truth about the topic. You're not describing a problem in the game, you're describing a problem if your perfectly construct an absurd situation and wand wave away the absurdity of it to call it logical. That does not lead to good house rules. That doesn't indicate a good grounding in what would improve the system. It just prolongs arguments because it lets you assert whatever you want in a white-room of context you construct that has no direct correlation to playing the game. </div> </div> </div>A hypothetical is used to demonstrate a problem without the irrelevant details obscuring the issue. Irrelevant details being things like why the crafter wants to make a particular thing, how the crafter ended up with no craft points, or what the particular thing being made is. Just because a situation is most clearly seen in a white room doesn't make the problem any less real. All of your experience in play doesn't disprove the scenario, only state that you yourself have never been in that scenario. Some of the reason why you have never been in that scenario seem to be behavioral changes in response to an arbitrary restriction imposed by the system, which is the problem I am seeking to illustrate.<br /> <br /> You may not even be aware that you've changed your behavior. Incentive structures can be insidious like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sounds like a character that might need to recharge before crafting another sword to me! </div> </div> </div>Nope. Heroic fantasy game, not Crafting Simulator 2018.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nope. Stop lying. </div> </div> </div>Just because you don't like my assessment of the behavior you tell me you perform doesn't make me a liar. Accusing me of lying is disingenuous at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And this is all completely lacking in reasons to consider it accurate to the play of the game. The &quot;every problem with Craft&quot; only exists in white-room hypothetical theory-crafting, not actual play, meaning there isn't an actual incentive problem that reinforces and issues with the gating issue. The gating issue is, of course, completely overstated. Yes, I appreciate the complaint, but because of the inter-relating issues, the gating complaint is tainted by the falsehood of the incentive complaint. Though, again, I don't think your phrasing of the gating complaint is actually sufficient. &quot;Mundane&quot; isn't enough of a qualifier to state that gating/pacing is inappropriate. Nor is, &quot;but I want to make the thing.&quot; </div> </div> </div>So, despite the things you tell me about your experience in play lining up with the behaviors predicted by the incentive structure and the existence of the gating problem, I am supposed to believe that your experiences in play prove that there are no such problems?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is where not having consistency comes into play. It ignores things like verisimilitude (people in Creation without magic don't pump out swords, building a city's walls could be a mortals single greatest project of their entire lives) that are helped with pacing controls. It ignores things like using the system to arbitrate to cut down on ST/player arguments. Etc. If you consistently applied one metric, this falls apart. Because you're citing two here alone. If you craft a bigger picture model, you actually have to stick to it, and can't cherry pick which considerations matter when. </div> </div> </div>The game is not a simulator. The rules are explicitly meant to only handle on-screen behavior of player characters. NPCs run on handwaves and shortcuts. Versimilitude in the system is &quot;Is it appropriate for my heroic fantasy character to be able to do this thing?&quot;<br /> <br /> Which Storyteller/PC arguments are avoided by gating Majors behind Basics? Did your Storyteller use to tell you that you couldn't make a sword right now because you were creatively burned out and need to go make some horseshoes for a while?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nope. </div> </div> </div>I literally quoted where you did that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not accurate to what I said. My point - and continuing form the &quot;fuck hypotheticals&quot; thing - with those questions is that without the context of how you got to a situation, the limitations being appropriate or not appropriate are impossible to judge. </div> </div> </div>Heroic fantasy game. My character can dump a bucket of dice on the problem, there is no game balance concern. Impassable waist-high fences and invisible walls are bad game design.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Making Craft an Ability that is actively used in-play instead of being regulated to downtime. Crafting in-play requires flexibility in what you'll craft rather than being dead set on only crafting one thing, or it doesn't really get the desired result. </div> </div> </div>Is there a problem with handling the things your characters make during downtime? It's actually an option in the existing system if you don't mind losing out on the third objective most of the time, but then that's the only objective that's hard to hit. You'd, of course, need a Storyteller who is okay with using the RAW rather than taking umbrage with &quot;Alright, so I make three dozen knives and sell them cheaply, here's the rolls, and that nets me six silver points a piece&quot;.<br /> <br /> I happen to be a Storyteller who finds that statement unsettling on a deep level, as it implies you are wasting my time with stuff that should be below the level of abstraction. That really should just be summed up as &quot;I spend downtime practicing my trade as a knife maker&quot; with no dice rolling involved, but the system makes it important to care about arbitrary small details happening off screen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do you really want to get into the Brawl vs. MA debate here? </div> </div> </div>It's a good example of the system occasionally being absurdly specific instead of broad.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":74,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","user":"Kyeudo","date":". 07-03-2018, 11:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T08:30:54-07:00","id":1232137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232127#post1232127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you think you say and what I think I hear aren't necessarily the same thing. That doesn't mean anyone is lying. Accusing someone of arguing in bad faith is a great way to make sure the debate is unproductive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Arguing with someone who's arguing in bad faith is <i>also </i>pretty solidly unproductive, though. Is it more effective to call someone out when they're debating dishonestly, or play into their hand?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T10:29:44-07:00","id":1232166,"title":"","content":"Sometimes I look into these arguments (and others I've been involved with), and look at how much thought and time people invest in it, and just wonder why can't I devote this effort to something productive?<br /> <br /> But I guess it is fun, and then the question is more why can't I so easily devote hours into not fun things that will bring more things that I want instead of fun things that are little but instant gratification. Simpler.<br /> <br /> Procrastination and compound interest, immovable object and unstoppable force.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T15:50:45-07:00","id":1232244,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232122#post1232122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[Just figured you'd want to know you're accomplishing <i>something</i> from the time you've sunk into this thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad to hear it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On another note, I'm also homebrewing up some Alchemicals. Does anyone have some suggestions on how to write craft charms that aren't just boring cxp generators and/or slot charms? Or point to a good thread for re-written solar craft charms? </div> </div> </div>Something I've been considering but haven't worked out yet in terms of Charm design when considering how I'd improve Solar Crafts is something that I could see Alchemicals taking instead to give them a more distinct flavor on the bread and butter Craft Charms:<br /> <br /> Craft slots as Charm currency. This would come in two forms, mostly aligning to the current Momentum and Power trees. First would be bonuses to Charms based on how many Slots have projects in them. The more stuff you're doing at once, the cheaper and/or stronger Charms that do things like increase rewards for completing projects, do tricks like producing an extra item, etc. are. An Alchemical in this model would likely embody a workshop a bit more literally than the Solar Charm names evoke. While you need to be careful around tonal issues with mass production, the &quot;do awesome things&quot; here would all stem conceptually from producing more.<br /> <br /> Second would be bonuses to Charms based on dedicating unused slots too them. This is for when you're seriously crafting something and putting all your energy into it. Incidentally making whatever slot generating Charms remain after the culling very valuable for synergy here making them less boring as they boost the efficiency of your dice-trick Charms, freeing up room for &quot;do awesome things&quot; Charms at the top of the tree instead of ever more potent/complicated dice tricks. And of course here we're producing &quot;better.&quot; Though replicating that for anything but Artifacts is difficult with 3e's abstraction of equipment. Though nothing technically stops there from being Charms that let an Exalt add a Tag to a weapon/armor (though it's dangerous territory to go overboard with), which would be cool for demonstrating the higher tech level of the citizens of Autocthon as something that's not super high up the tree but an example of &quot;better&quot; that's not just more cxp/slots.<br /> <br /> --------------<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232127#post1232127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That doesn't mean anyone is lying.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't mean you're not lying either. You asked me to highlight where I felt you were doing bad things. I did. Complaining about me doing what you asked me to do isn't productive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's because 3e added new things you can do with Lore, not because it added a gating mechanic to Lore. </div> </div> </div>Except, you know, the incentive to get around the Lore gating mechanic mean they were invested in finding in-game justifications for new specialties that goes beyond the increase in Lore's utility.<br /> <br /> I actually don't have a problem with that. It fits for my (though it should seem obvious that I don't have a problem with a lot of the stuff you think are problems) estimation of what Lore heavy characters would be doing anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How so? </div> </div> </div>I think I've expressed directly quite a few things I feel are detrimental to my feelings that you honestly want to understand what I think and why, but sure, lets go over this directly:<br /> <br /> 1) A lack of inquisitiveness without immediate contradiction. If every time I try to explain something I get, &quot;well you're wrong,&quot; it doesn't communicate that you care, and it doesn't communicate that you're putting effort into seeing my point. You have never replied to my first post in dissent of your statement about what's a better priority to fix. You only replied to me when I sided with Alucard in opposition to your counters towards their posts. I have repeatedly highlight that unaddressed post as something you seem to have ignored, as it informs my posts after it, and the most I've gotten is neat idea, don't care.<br /> <br /> 2) Unproductive debating tactics oriented towards frame controlling over making strong points that demonstrate an understanding of my own points. This includes things like the constant attempts at definition policing, insting on the primacy of your personally constructed hypothetical scenarios, bad faith assumptions on how the system has impacted my playstyle (hint: it hasn't, it just rewards me for what I was already trying to do instead of fighting me over it), bad faith gestures like rewriting my questions/statements to change their meaning to support your stance instead of dealing with the statement as stands, frequent question dodging, and so on.<br /> <br /> 3) Lying about what I said, even if a mistake or unintentional, with no admission of such.<br /> <br /> 4) Using compliance with your own requests as rhetorical weapons against me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You keep making arguments that there is nothing wrong with the gating. </div> </div> </div>No, I keep making arguments that the current level of gating is acceptable in the context of how the game operates, and isn't the primary detriment to the base-line crafting system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game is wrong for incentivizing that world view in every character. </div> </div> </div>Facts not in evidence. Repeating it doesn't make it true. The one real-play example you even cite in your arguments doesn't touch on this, and then two people citing play experience contradict it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem with DBT (either edition, but I've been told it was way worse in 1st ed.) was that it was an option that was too far above other options. </div> </div> </div>Right. Because WtA (with Crinos/DBT as a baseline best thing for combat form, and an addition natural form that's better than other kinds of shapeshifting) + Exalted got messy and was never properly corrected. DBT came out of WtA's Crinos form battle supremacy, as the inspiration from werewcritters overrode the Lunars being total shapeshifters not just werewcreatures.<br /> <br /> That's not the issue you're stating is the problem with Craft.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It doesn't matter which flawed philosophy lead to One True Build syndrome, the problem exists regardless. Crafters all approaching the wold like McGuyver is a One True Build issue, but it's one that shows up in play behavior rather than on a character sheet. </div> </div> </div>&quot;One True Build behavior&quot; is an nonsensical concept. OTBs exist the relative vacuum of number crunching, where behaviors are informed by more than just mechanics.<br /> <br /> This is like saying, &quot;combat forces you to make decisive attacks to win fights,&quot; so there is a &quot;OTB behavior&quot; issue where characters are all oriented towards how to get in effective decisive attacks in their action choices.<br /> <br /> Your objection can be applied to <b>any</b> mechanic and lacks any specificity on why Craft's way is significantly worse for play. As was parodied below my last post, the social mechanics (something 3e is largely lauded for getting right) is wrong by this standard, because being an effective social character requires a mindset of Intimacy instilling and manipulation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Characters have multiple &quot;best skills&quot;, but Craft pays you to put it on the screen. If you want to hit all three objectives, it requires you to put it on the screen. </div> </div> </div>Craft in past editions had problems being put on screen, hence the choice to increase incentives to do so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did you play Craft-heavy characters the same way? Were you rolling Craft in every scene? Were you looking for little things you could do with Craft? Were you building gifts and bribes? </div> </div> </div>Yes, yes, no because that's a lie about how I've stated I approach crafting in play and I don't do it in 3e either, yes.<br /> <br /> The difference is that 3e is actually fun for things where 1e and 2e were not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I provided examples. Those are citations. </div> </div> </div>No, they aren't. That's not what citation means.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because those things are different for a knife and a sword? </div> </div> </div>You haven't described anything different besides the sword being harder to apply the techniques to due to its size. Basic projects have variable difficulties to account for this.<br /> <br /> This completely ignores the question (again) of why the sword can't be broken down into multiple discrete steps. Why isn't shaping a billet into a sword blade with proper geometry a Basic project with a higher difficulty than a knife? Esp. for a mortal blade smith, wouldn't multiple projects (any of which failing could ruing the total piece) also represent increased difficulty by increasing the odds of failure?<br /> <br /> You're not demonstrating that &quot;skill&quot; is a sufficient basis for why projects can't be broken apart into multiple lower ranked projects. The far simpler reason of, &quot;because it stops a slippery slope until everything is Basic,&quot; is sufficient for why the rules don't allow it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The vast majority of architectural projects are infrastructure of some kind. That's why I think gating the frequency of mundane Superior projects is important. </div> </div> </div>And another dodge. That's not the question at hand. Frequency of these sorts of projects was the question, because you rejected Superior project examples that would not be &quot;regular&quot; enough as examples for ones from other crafts besides the two referenced in the book (as examples, not as an exhaustive list). How regularly does one person build a piece of infrastructure that would qualify as a Superior project? That's the question posed because it seems you're being inconsistent by putting a &quot;regular&quot; qualifier on non-book options that doesn't consider the frequency of the book's examples.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I was being charitable. </div> </div> </div>No. You might believe that about yourself, but you're just being dishonest; apparently with yourself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the question you asked is the question you wanted me to answer, then your question is either the start of a straw man or else the start of a red herring. I don't see much difference between doing significant things with Craft and solving problems with Craft. </div> </div> </div>Actually, it's not a strawman or a red herring. It's a solid hole in your case. If there is no meaningful difference between &quot;doing significant things with Craft,&quot; and &quot;solving problems with Craft&quot; then a huge amount of your argumentation has faulty logic because you've been using some unspecified &quot;difference&quot; as the dividing line between what's good and what's bad crafting function. Wanting to do significant things with Craft was the &quot;good thing,&quot; and &quot;solving problems with Craft&quot; is the start to the &quot;world view.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's a sign your argumentation is majorly flawed, not a fallacy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You are engaging in the fallacy of the excluded middle here and projecting it onto me. I don't appreciate it. </div> </div> </div>I've offered you repeated chances to accept the middle, and you've repeatedly rejected them to enforce things as a false dichotomy. I'm not projecting it on you. It is, as you've communicated it to me, the keystone to your entire view of the subject. If you don't appreciate that? You only have yourself to blame.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't see the problem caused by dangling a carrot in front of one method of problem-solving? How it will lead to more of the one and less of the other? </div> </div> </div>I don't have a problem with this specific form of it, no. The problem with your example is that it isn't actually even. Two characters are putting out two different levels of effort and getting commensurate results. This is not a situation where both characters did the same amount of work, and one will consistently get less results for it. This is a situation where one character does more work, and gets more rewards. <b>I absolutely want the game to incentivize this</b>.<br /> <br /> Games that reward everyone equally despite the level of effort put in, are highly problematic for most gaming groups, because it questions the ill-defined human concept of fairness in games. Most people struggle to have fun if the game rewards everyone equally if there isn't equal effort. Overdoing this is also bad - especially for limited resources like XP - but ultimately people tend to have more fun if a character that does two things (even if to the same goal) gets two results, and a character that does one thing gets one result.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because it locks the things they want to craft behind a wall until they craft a lot? </div> </div> </div>And... we're back to &quot;a lot&quot; again... wow. I've already thoroughly broken down why this is hyperbole is crap. I thought we were past it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We're operating in a heroic fantasy game. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Heroic Fantasy&quot; is not a panacea to fill in all your arguments with. The fact that you're down to citing this repeatedly only goes further to suggest your intentions are not what you state they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's why a gating mechanic without recourse to a legitimate game balance concern is a bad thing. It limits character options arbitrarily. </div> </div> </div>This logic is still faulty:<br /> <br /> 1) Balance is not the only consideration to value. Initiative gating in combat isn't about game balance, it's about structuring the feel of combat to make it more what Exalted tries to be.<br /> <br /> 2) Gating doesn't inherently limit character options unless the gates are on character options (such as mortals not being able to craft things Exalts can). It inherently limits pacing. Facing a gate like cxp doesn't say, &quot;you can't do X,&quot; is says, &quot;you have to put in more effort to do X.&quot; That might make X unappealing for a given situation, but so can lots of things that aren't mechanical getting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even then, I doubt you ever had to argue about how much effort it takes to build a catapult in past editions. </div> </div> </div>I rage quit a 1e game over an argument over something similar actually... which fell apart soon after because, well, heavy handed STs getting into 3 hour debates with a player wanted to make something with Crafts (and actually something far simpler than a catapult) during effectively a downtime scenario... it's just a bad idea for keeping players around regardless of who they agree with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Storyteller probably said &quot;It's Craft[Wood], difficulty 3, it will take an hour&quot; and you rolled for it. </div> </div> </div>I was certainly expecting that to be the case vs. what happened, yes. But there wasn't really much more to the 1e system if you think you're asking for a minor project for your character (also Craft [Wood] would be 2e, 1e did multiple Crafts like 3e does).<br /> <br /> I also don't think you remember the 1e Craft rules all that well. The game had two tiers of mundane crafting. The first was almost the same as Basic projects, if more loosely defined. The second was a time consuming three step process that would have meant building a catapult would have probably taken too long to matter anyway. The planning extended roll has a minimum interval of a day, and you keep rolling until the ST decides you've planned enough (which has no further mechanical impact, it's just a hoop you jump through). Material gathering is a simple roll, so that's nice, but again, another step with no impact beyond the ST fiat on it mattering. Then another extended roll with a completely ST fiat interval, with no set number of successes besides how many your ST thinks you should need (but a cap on rolling once you hit too many, so it sucks if hit that cap on a meh roll and come up a few successes short).<br /> <br /> The only thing 1e did better here, really, is that it has set rules for leading a crafting project instead of doing it yourself.<br /> <br /> The big point though, is that a siege engineer in 1e couldn't just come across a battle, and whip out a catapult. The mechanical gating required them to work for days on such a project, rolling out busy work to jump through hoops.<br /> <br /> So, tell me, which would you rather have to deal with:<br /> <br /> 1) &quot;I want to craft the thing because it would be helpful to do ASAP!&quot; &quot;OK, here's a three step project, you'll need 7 successes total with 1 day intervals to plan it out, an hour and a roll of picking out the best materials, and 10 successes with an interval of 2 hours to actually build it.&quot;<br /> <br /> vs.<br /> <br /> 2) &quot;I want to craft the thing because it would be helpful to do ASAP!&quot; &quot;OK, it would be a difficulty 4 Major project that will take about 10 hours to finish, but you don't have 10 sxp yet, so lets see if there's some Basic projects your character can do to help out first and then we'll do the big thing.&quot;<br /> <br /> Because the second's a lot nicer to, &quot;I want to make a catapult right now,&quot; in practice even if the feel doesn't work for everyone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All of the &quot;years of experience&quot; still don't demonstrate why that character shouldn't be able to build catapults when lives are literally on the line. That's why it gets to be a trump card. </div> </div> </div>Because catapults aren't quarterstaves. Making them is a huge deal, and enough of a time commitment that lives being on the line easily means you don't have the time to make catapults.<br /> <br /> Even if you took sxp out, 10 hours to make one (and that's fucking generous as hell to build a catapult from scratch with the tech level of the game) would make it difficult to accomplish in a meaningful amount of time.<br /> <br /> So, no, it's still not a good trump card. It's also still not a situation with a &quot;trump card,&quot; mister &quot;I object to being labeled guilty of excluding the middle!&quot; one situations nor working well doesn't demonstrate the whole thing is bad, especially if more data points say otherwise.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, when I go back and read the statements that you misunderstood and reflect back on previous times when I have made similar arguments and had them understood, I am left with the weight of evidence pointing at the failure to communicate being on your end. </div> </div> </div>So, you reread your own work, and you felt that you expressed yourself well the first time. I think I just felt thousands of editors cringe. If you cared, you'd ask fresh eyes to look things over before doubling down on this. Reality isn't kind, but our brains love to tell us we're the special ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You say &quot;No&quot;, then restate the definition I gave. </div> </div> </div>Nope Lie.<br /> <br /> Your definition is that the logic is fault. My definition is the the facts are faulty. The difference seems fairly obvious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a difference between understanding their premises and wanting to use them as a baseline. </div> </div> </div>If you aren't going to engage with other people's premises, you're not trying to understand them, because you're forcing all of their statements into your baseline instead of analyzing them in their intended context. Fighting over the frame shouldn't be the goal of discussing mechanics in a RPG. Acknowledging them should be about moving past them, which you have repeatedly expressed no interest in.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can understand why people like to use coconut milk in their cooking, but that doesn't mean I want to use it in mine. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but to expand the analogy, you're insisting that using coconut milk in my cooking means my recipes are inherently not to considered since I'm not operating in your anti-coconut milk frame. Which is the problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the problem stops arising in &quot;experienced&quot; play, then behaviors have changed because of the system. </div> </div> </div>Games having learning curves doesn't mean there's a problem with the system. And then there's people like me were my behaviors only changed in the sense of motivation to engage with a system that rewarded my playstyle instead of punishing it/ignoring it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I look at the changes and say &quot;Why are you routing around this issue? </div> </div> </div>Because you're not listening to us when we say that this is how we want things to work. We're not &quot;routing around an issue,&quot; we're playing and finding that what you say is an issue isn't actually there, and playing the system is actually fun.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why are you making what the system tells you to make instead of making what you want to make?&quot; </div> </div> </div>I'm not. Insisting I am is lying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A hypothetical is used to demonstrate a problem without the irrelevant details obscuring the issue. </div> </div> </div>It could be used in that fashion. You're using it to obscure relevant details that undermine your points and make irrelevant details look legitimate. That's the problem with hypothetical scenarios, we can construct them however we want to do hide or highlight what we want, and aren't forced by anything other than conversation pushback (aka this) to stop doing so in a nonconstructive fashion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You may not even be aware that you've changed your behavior. </div> </div> </div>Oooooo armchair psych time!<br /> <br /> Did you know that you might not even be aware that you've responded to a logical argument that out debated your own with an irrational defensive set of posts designed solely to allow you to think you're &quot;winning&quot; so you don't have to face the discomfort of your preconceptions being challenged significantly?<br /> <br /> Come on, lets keep at this. This game is totally constructive, intellectual honest, and in good faith!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nope. Heroic fantasy game, not Crafting Simulator 2018. </div> </div> </div>Sorry, but you're just proving my point. You clearly don't seem to realize I'm the universe's arbiter of Heroic fantasy, so appealing to genre is just admitting I'm right. Sucks for you to not have that advantage though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just because you don't like my assessment of the behavior you tell me you perform doesn't make me a liar. Accusing me of lying is disingenuous at best. </div> </div> </div>My feelings are what makes it a lie. When I say &quot;my behavior was X,&quot; and you say, &quot;your behavior was Y.&quot; is when you've moved into lying. You're claiming something that isn't true, as true. You're rationalizations don't change that.<br /> <br /> And I don't think you know what disingenuous means... are we going to have to go over another word?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, despite the things you tell me about your experience in play lining up with the behaviors predicted by the incentive structure and the existence of the gating problem, I am supposed to believe that your experiences in play prove that there are no such problems? </div> </div> </div>If you accept my statements in good faith, you have to at least accept that your position on any problems in the system are not accurate, and require significant refinement if not outright revision, because you have data that doesn't fit your model.<br /> <br /> You might still find the gating too strong after that, but right now you're just doubling down and continuing to assert your &quot;assessments&quot; as more valid than my own statements of my personal experiences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game is not a simulator. </div> </div> </div>Strawman. I cited verisimilitude, pacing, and ST-player interactions. I never stated that the game needs to simulate reality (and verisimilitude is simulating genre conceits anyway).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The rules are explicitly meant to only handle on-screen behavior of player characters. NPCs run on handwaves and shortcuts. </div> </div> </div>Obviously not true. NPCs can be run on handwaving and shortcuts, or full rules, as the ST feels appropriate. And even then, plenty of the rules still fully apply to NPCs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Versimilitude in the system is &quot;Is it appropriate for my heroic fantasy character to be able to do this thing?&quot; </div> </div> </div>No, this is Exalted, not generic heroic fantasy (remember, that's my ordained domain! Appeal to genre denied!). Exalted explicitly establishes tiers of appropriateness. What is appropriate for a mortal smith heroic fantasy character in Exalted is not the same as what is appropriate for Solar smith heroic fantasy character in Exalted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which Storyteller/PC arguments are avoided by gating Majors behind Basics? </div> </div> </div>How much work it takes to do things. Again, you might not like the specific form. You're obnoxious dedication to excluding the middle constantly leads you to these stupid needles and barbs of, &quot;oh Heavy Arms, you think the system is perfect as written you're so precious,&quot; nonsense. Gating in crafting isn't perfect, but it's better. See above about 1e crafting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did your Storyteller use to tell you that you couldn't make a sword right now because you were creatively burned out and need to go make some horseshoes for a while? </div> </div> </div>Specifically no, in spirit yes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I literally quoted where you did that. </div> </div> </div>I believe you believe that, but you're wrong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Heroic fantasy game. </div> </div> </div>There we go again... denied!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Impassable waist-high fences and invisible walls are bad game design. </div> </div> </div>But they keep happening...<br /> <br /> Actually they're fine game design that's flawed by the visibility. In videogames, locking off sections of the game until the player makes enough progress is frequently a good thing. The only problem with wait-high fences are that you can see the locking off, not that it happens. The visibility breaks immersion as you know the game has a reason to stop you, but the visual representation doesn't communicate a sufficient one.<br /> <br /> If cxp is a waist-high fence issue... then really we're talking about all the wrong things, because then the problem isn't that gating happens, or that the gating is even improperly calibrated, it's just visible in a way that's off-putting to people. The question then is what can be done to reduce/mitigate it's visibility, not if the base mechanics are really the problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is there a problem with handling the things your characters make during downtime? </div> </div> </div>No, there's a problem if I can't bring it on-screen though, which 3e fixed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I happen to be a Storyteller who finds that statement unsettling on a deep level, as it implies you are wasting my time with stuff that should be below the level of abstraction. </div> </div> </div>So.... you're saying you can write expressions of player actions you personally find off-putting? So? Isn't that like... assumed something we can all do?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That really should just be summed up as &quot;I spend downtime practicing my trade as a knife maker&quot; with no dice rolling involved,... </div> </div> </div>That's what the automatic cxp if you have done at least one project during the story is for. Maybe you should consider increasing the frequency those rewards trigger? I mean, I house rule that to also apply at creation with the assumption that start of play represents the end of the &quot;prelude story&quot; and start of a new story. It's sensible and means there's some cxp on the sheet at the start of play without bum rushing for certain Charms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's a good example of the system occasionally being absurdly specific instead of broad. </div> </div> </div>Right, but it's also a clear outlier rather than a solid comment on the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-03T22:05:41-07:00","id":1232311,"title":"","content":"I'm going to do something a little odd here. I'm going to move the later part of your responses and my reply to the front. I like the tone the discussion takes there a lot more than in the chronologically earlier sections. Maybe that will help keep this argument from getting too much more negative. It might make things seem a little disjointed, though, since I replied in chronological order originally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232244#post1232244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But they keep happening...<br /> <br /> Actually they're fine game design that's flawed by the visibility. In videogames, locking off sections of the game until the player makes enough progress is frequently a good thing. The only problem with wait-high fences are that you can see the locking off, not that it happens. The visibility breaks immersion as you know the game has a reason to stop you, but the visual representation doesn't communicate a sufficient one.<br /> <br /> If cxp is a waist-high fence issue... then really we're talking about all the wrong things, because then the problem isn't that gating happens, or that the gating is even improperly calibrated, it's just visible in a way that's off-putting to people. The question then is what can be done to reduce/mitigate it's visibility, not if the base mechanics are really the problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, here's one of those statements that makes all of the argumentation worth doing.<br /> <br /> I do believe the gating is inappropriate, but if it wasn't apparent that the gating was even happening it certainly would be less grating. There still needs to be gating on the Superior projects and that, while well motivated, is still off-putting, as you put it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So.... you're saying you can write expressions of player actions you personally find off-putting? So? Isn't that like... assumed something we can all do? </div> </div> </div>The game makes him mention the exact number of projects, their classification, how they check off objectives, and even roll for them. Do I seriously want to know that he made three dozen knives off screen? No. Does he seriously want me to know he made three dozen knives off-screen? No. He wants to have the craft points so he can later tell me &quot;I forge a fine sword and decorate it with gold filligree and a ruby pommel as a gift to the Sultan&quot;. However, we just rolled three dozen Craft rolls to make sure he got the exceptional successes so we can make sure to do the bookkeeping on his silver points.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's what the automatic cxp if you have done at least one project during the story is for. Maybe you should consider increasing the frequency those rewards trigger? I mean, I house rule that to also apply at creation with the assumption that start of play represents the end of the &quot;prelude story&quot; and start of a new story. It's sensible and means there's some cxp on the sheet at the start of play without bum rushing for certain Charms. </div> </div> </div>And if the player wants more than five silver points for the single Craft Ability he took? You need ten before you can do much with it unless you get Charms involved.<br /> Three dozen knives is 216 silver points. That's a lot more than 5.<br /> <br /> I don't have a real craft player currently. This is another one of those hypotheticals you love so much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Doesn't mean you're not lying either. You asked me to highlight where I felt you were doing bad things. I did. Complaining about me doing what you asked me to do isn't productive. </div> </div> </div>I was expecting that you'd point out places in the past where you thought I had contradicted myself, shifted position, or whatever, not that you would just start calling me a liar every third sentence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except, you know, the incentive to get around the Lore gating mechanic mean they were invested in finding in-game justifications for new specialties that goes beyond the increase in Lore's utility.<br /> <br /> I actually don't have a problem with that. It fits for my (though it should seem obvious that I don't have a problem with a lot of the stuff you think are problems) estimation of what Lore heavy characters would be doing anyway. </div> </div> </div>Tried to find where in-game justification for new specialties was required in the rules, but I couldn't. Are you certain this isn't an &quot;At our table&quot; sort of thing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1) A lack of inquisitiveness without immediate contradiction. If every time I try to explain something I get, &quot;well you're wrong,&quot; it doesn't communicate that you care, and it doesn't communicate that you're putting effort into seeing my point. You have never replied to my first post in dissent of your statement about what's a better priority to fix. You only replied to me when I sided with Alucard in opposition to your counters towards their posts. I have repeatedly highlight that unaddressed post as something you seem to have ignored, as it informs my posts after it, and the most I've gotten is neat idea, don't care. </div> </div> </div>You mean your proposal that the different categories of project should be defined by the project's impact on the story rather than the project's complexity? It doesn't provoke any passion in me. I think it runs afoul of my preferences for clean categorization. I don't think it is necessarily a bad idea if developed, but I am unsure of what ramifications it has in game design terms. There doesn't seem to be anything there that needs addressed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Unproductive debating tactics oriented towards frame controlling over making strong points that demonstrate an understanding of my own points. This includes things like the constant attempts at definition policing, insting on the primacy of your personally constructed hypothetical scenarios, bad faith assumptions on how the system has impacted my playstyle (hint: it hasn't, it just rewards me for what I was already trying to do instead of fighting me over it), bad faith gestures like rewriting my questions/statements to change their meaning to support your stance instead of dealing with the statement as stands, frequent question dodging, and so on. </div> </div> </div>Every debate has a frame. Either you set it, I set it, or our efforts to frame the debate jointly set it. Calling attempts to frame the debate &quot;unproductive&quot; or &quot;dishonest&quot; is unproductive and dishonest. Arguing about how to think about a problem is sometimes more productive than arguing about the problem itself. Part of the issue here is that apparently the problem only exists inside certain frames of reference, so debate framing is going to be a necessary part of the discussion.<br /> <br /> If you use a word one way and I use a word another, we are communicating past each other. I need to tell you what I think the word means when I become aware that we are using words differently.<br /> <br /> I rewrote those questions/statements because I was giving you the benefit of the doubt. If you would like to assert that you were trying to create a straw man of my arguments, be my guest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) Lying about what I said, even if a mistake or unintentional, with no admission of such. </div> </div> </div>I haven't lied to my knowledge, so there is nothing to admit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4) Using compliance with your own requests as rhetorical weapons against me. </div> </div> </div>How so?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Facts not in evidence. Repeating it doesn't make it true. The one real-play example you even cite in your arguments doesn't touch on this, and then two people citing play experience contradict it. </div> </div> </div>The incentive structure is right there in the rules, on the page clear as crystal. Your testimony of real play experience conforms to that incentive structure. Who is the second person? Alucard? He seemed overtly hostile to even having the discussion, so I have been ignoring him.<br /> <br /> A problem with an incentive structure can't be demonstrated by single examples. It is a pattern that crops up across multiple anecdotes about in-play experiences that conform to the incentive structure of the rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;One True Build behavior&quot; is an nonsensical concept. OTBs exist the relative vacuum of number crunching, where behaviors are informed by more than just mechanics. </div> </div> </div>Not really. We've seen examples of it, such as the paranoia combat paradigm's assertion that attempts at social combat should be met with actual combat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is like saying, &quot;combat forces you to make decisive attacks to win fights,&quot; so there is a &quot;OTB behavior&quot; issue where characters are all oriented towards how to get in effective decisive attacks in their action choices. </div> </div> </div>No, it would be like saying &quot;Archery attacks plus withdraw actions are objectively the best way to win fights&quot; (if that were true). 3e is still too young yet for overall optimal combat strategies to have emerged yet - there's too much unknown about other splats options.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your objection can be applied to <b>any</b> mechanic and lacks any specificity on why Craft's way is significantly worse for play. As was parodied below my last post, the social mechanics (something 3e is largely lauded for getting right) is wrong by this standard, because being an effective social character requires a mindset of Intimacy instilling and manipulation. </div> </div> </div>I dispute that it can be applied to any mechanic, though there are some that it can be applied to. Making some behavior sub-optimal can be of benefit to the game - anything that works to discourage murder-hobos for example.<br /> <br /> That parody was incoherent, mainly because it failed to understand the disconnect between Craft actions. A Craft action to make a horseshoe somehow enables you to later make a sword. A social action to persuade a Guild prince does make you more able to take social actions targeting the Mask of Winters. It was a good attempt, but it only played to preconceived notions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, yes, no because that's a lie about how I've stated I approach crafting in play and I don't do it in 3e either, yes. </div> </div> </div>Then you have actually provided some counter evidence that you have not been influenced by the new incentive structure. Congratulations. Now, is there any way to show this to be true for the general case? I know I've heard from plenty of people that 3e has changed how their Craft-heavy characters act.<br /> <br /> A question cannot be a lie.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, they aren't. That's not what citation means. </div> </div> </div>Am I to understand that you want actual academic citations in an internet debate about the classification of craft projects in an RPG system?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're not demonstrating that &quot;skill&quot; is a sufficient basis for why projects can't be broken apart into multiple lower ranked projects. The far simpler reason of, &quot;because it stops a slippery slope until everything is Basic,&quot; is sufficient for why the rules don't allow it. </div> </div> </div>That logic doesn't work in the system or making a thousand arrows would be a Superior project instead of a thousand Basic projects.<br /> <br /> Blacksmithing is probably not the best field to demonstrate why you can't break a project down. I don't know enough about it to know what considerations I don't know about. I don't understand just how techniques change as you go from working with six inches of metal to six feet of metal because I don't know how to work with even six inches of metal. I do, however, know a reasonable amount about construction from working with my father. My father, from his time spent working construction, knows enough to design and build a two story house. Between modern materials and a general sense of what floor plans work, the structure is unlikely to collapse. However, I would not trust him to design a four-story building. As the structure gets taller and broader, considerations like where load-bearing walls are placed, how plumbing needs to be routed to function properly, how the structure needs to be ventilated in order to be habitable, the effects of wind on structural stability, etc. all become more important. It's not just needing to craft the walls, it's needing to craft the walls in the right place at the right time or the whole thing fails. Thus the doghouse analogy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And another dodge. That's not the question at hand. Frequency of these sorts of projects was the question, because you rejected Superior project examples that would not be &quot;regular&quot; enough as examples for ones from other crafts besides the two referenced in the book (as examples, not as an exhaustive list). How regularly does one person build a piece of infrastructure that would qualify as a Superior project? That's the question posed because it seems you're being inconsistent by putting a &quot;regular&quot; qualifier on non-book options that doesn't consider the frequency of the book's examples. </div> </div> </div>Not a dodge. Here's your original question:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231940#post1231940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, the whole setup is kind of weird. How often are people building palaces in your games? It's never happened mundanely in one of mine... but you're fine with that being metered. Why is architecture OK even if it's not really something adventuring heroes are likely to do much of in most iterations of the game? Why can't it be handwaved? The book brings it up?</div> </div> </div> </div>I answered that question. Twice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Actually, it's not a strawman or a red herring. It's a solid hole in your case. If there is no meaningful difference between &quot;doing significant things with Craft,&quot; and &quot;solving problems with Craft&quot; then a huge amount of your argumentation has faulty logic because you've been using some unspecified &quot;difference&quot; as the dividing line between what's good and what's bad crafting function. Wanting to do significant things with Craft was the &quot;good thing,&quot; and &quot;solving problems with Craft&quot; is the start to the &quot;world view.&quot; </div> </div> </div>It is not a hole in my case and you are compounding your fallacious argument with a fallacious categorical syllogism. Just because a character wants to accomplish significant things with Craft doesn't mean they want to solve <i>this specific problem</i> with Craft. &quot;Solving all problems with Craft&quot; isn't the start to the world view I'm opposed to, it's the whole of that world view. I don't want that mindset imposed on every character involved in Craft. Not every crafter should be McGuyver.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And... we're back to &quot;a lot&quot; again... wow. I've already thoroughly broken down why this is hyperbole is crap. I thought we were past it. </div> </div> </div>You are the one that chose to use &quot;a lot&quot;. I just answered you using the same language you used in the question.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Heroic Fantasy&quot; is not a panacea to fill in all your arguments with. The fact that you're down to citing this repeatedly only goes further to suggest your intentions are not what you state they are. </div> </div> </div>You seem to be desperately searching for ill-intention. There is no evil conspiracy to make you look bad on the internet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This logic is still faulty:<br /> <br /> 1) Balance is not the only consideration to value. Initiative gating in combat isn't about game balance, it's about structuring the feel of combat to make it more what Exalted tries to be. </div> </div> </div>Go read up on 2e paranoia combat and then tell me again that it isn't about game balance. Look and feel certainly factored into the form they chose, but game balance was the overriding reason why the core combat mechanics changed between editions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Gating doesn't inherently limit character options unless the gates are on character options (such as mortals not being able to craft things Exalts can). It inherently limits pacing. Facing a gate like cxp doesn't say, &quot;you can't do X,&quot; is says, &quot;you have to put in more effort to do X.&quot; That might make X unappealing for a given situation, but so can lots of things that aren't mechanical getting. </div> </div> </div>Your first sentence is a tautological. &quot;X doesn't do Y unless X does Y&quot;. It distracts from the rest of your statements here, though it is irrelevant to the point you are trying to make.<br /> <br /> Why do Major projects need pacing control?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The big point though, is that a siege engineer in 1e couldn't just come across a battle, and whip out a catapult. The mechanical gating required them to work for days on such a project, rolling out busy work to jump through hoops.<br /> <br /> So, tell me, which would you rather have to deal with:<br /> <br /> 1) &quot;I want to craft the thing because it would be helpful to do ASAP!&quot; &quot;OK, here's a three step project, you'll need 7 successes total with 1 day intervals to plan it out, an hour and a roll of picking out the best materials, and 10 successes with an interval of 2 hours to actually build it.&quot;<br /> <br /> vs.<br /> <br /> 2) &quot;I want to craft the thing because it would be helpful to do ASAP!&quot; &quot;OK, it would be a difficulty 4 Major project that will take about 10 hours to finish, but you don't have 10 sxp yet, so lets see if there's some Basic projects your character can do to help out first and then we'll do the big thing.&quot;<br /> <br /> Because the second's a lot nicer to, &quot;I want to make a catapult right now,&quot; in practice even if the feel doesn't work for everyone. </div> </div> </div>Or we could go with 3) &quot;I want to craft the thing because it would be helpful to do ASAP!&quot; &quot;Okay. That's a difficulty 4 Major project that will take about 10 hours to finish.&quot; That one's even better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because catapults aren't quarterstaves. Making them is a huge deal, and enough of a time commitment that lives being on the line easily means you don't have the time to make catapults.<br /> <br /> Even if you took sxp out, 10 hours to make one (and that's fucking generous as hell to build a catapult from scratch with the tech level of the game) would make it difficult to accomplish in a meaningful amount of time.<br /> <br /> So, no, it's still not a good trump card. It's also still not a situation with a &quot;trump card,&quot; mister &quot;I object to being labeled guilty of excluding the middle!&quot; one situations nor working well doesn't demonstrate the whole thing is bad, especially if more data points say otherwise. </div> </div> </div>Craftsman Needs No Tools was in play, so the time commitment wasn't a factor. Even if it hadn't been in play, the situation was a siege, not a frontal assault. Time was precious, not non-existent.<br /> <br /> One situation not working well demonstrates there is a problem. The severity of the problem, the frequency of it arising, and so on can be debated, but the problem is real.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, you reread your own work, and you felt that you expressed yourself well the first time. I think I just felt thousands of editors cringe. If you cared, you'd ask fresh eyes to look things over before doubling down on this. Reality isn't kind, but our brains love to tell us we're the special ones. </div> </div> </div>When you don't have an editor, rereading your own work is the only thing you can do. Unless of course you're offering.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your definition is that the logic is fault. My definition is the the facts are faulty. The difference seems fairly obvious. </div> </div> </div>You did not claim the facts are faulty. You claimed the facts were being misused. World of difference. You accuse me of consistency problems, but this is actually a real one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you aren't going to engage with other people's premises, you're not trying to understand them, because you're forcing all of their statements into your baseline instead of analyzing them in their intended context. Fighting over the frame shouldn't be the goal of discussing mechanics in a RPG. Acknowledging them should be about moving past them, which you have repeatedly expressed no interest in. </div> </div> </div>The only coherent thought I can extract from this statement is &quot;I don't like the way you argue&quot;, but it seems clear you are trying to convey more than that. What additional thought I should take away is ill-defined.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Games having learning curves doesn't mean there's a problem with the system. And then there's people like me were my behaviors only changed in the sense of motivation to engage with a system that rewarded my playstyle instead of punishing it/ignoring it. </div> </div> </div>No, it doesn't mean there's necessarily a problem. It does mean the incentive structure in the system is effective in influencing behavior.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because you're not listening to us when we say that this is how we want things to work. We're not &quot;routing around an issue,&quot; we're playing and finding that what you say is an issue isn't actually there, and playing the system is actually fun. </div> </div> </div>You want to only think in terms of Basic projects until you have enough silver points to do a Major project?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not. Insisting I am is lying. </div> </div> </div>Generic &quot;You&quot;, not you specifically. Blame English. Stop accusing me of lying every third line.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It could be used in that fashion. You're using it to obscure relevant details that undermine your points and make irrelevant details look legitimate. That's the problem with hypothetical scenarios, we can construct them however we want to do hide or highlight what we want, and aren't forced by anything other than conversation pushback (aka this) to stop doing so in a nonconstructive fashion. </div> </div> </div>What relevant detail am I obscuring? What irrelevant detail am I legitimizing? The character has a mundane non-Superior craft project they would like to do. They are capable of accomplishing the project. The system says no anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did you know that you might not even be aware that you've responded to a logical argument that out debated your own with an irrational defensive set of posts designed solely to allow you to think you're &quot;winning&quot; so you don't have to face the discomfort of your preconceptions being challenged significantly? </div> </div> </div>That's actually a fairly common trap for the human mind, as proved by a variety of science. The Dunning-Kruger Effect is a related phenomenon. I don't believe I am in that situation, especially when it appears you are giving my arguments the weakest, most uncharitable reading possible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorry, but you're just proving my point. You clearly don't seem to realize I'm the universe's arbiter of Heroic fantasy, so appealing to genre is just admitting I'm right. Sucks for you to not have that advantage though. </div> </div> </div>Your sarcasm isn't an argument against making use of the high level of abstraction in a fantasy game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When I say &quot;my behavior was X,&quot; and you say, &quot;your behavior was Y.&quot; is when you've moved into lying. You're claiming something that isn't true, as true. You're rationalizations don't change that. </div> </div> </div>And if your behavior was both X and Y? Two things can be true at the same time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I don't think you know what disingenuous means... are we going to have to go over another word? </div> </div> </div>&quot;<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:Roboto\">not candid or sincere, typically by pretending that one knows less about something than one really does.&quot;<br /> Would you prefer &quot;insulting&quot;, &quot;libelous&quot;, &quot;defamatory&quot;, &quot;reprehensible&quot; or some other variation thereof? I'm getting tired of the baseless personal attacks.</span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you accept my statements in good faith, you have to at least accept that your position on any problems in the system are not accurate, and require significant refinement if not outright revision, because you have data that doesn't fit your model.<br /> <br /> You might still find the gating too strong after that, but right now you're just doubling down and continuing to assert your &quot;assessments&quot; as more valid than my own statements of my personal experiences. </div> </div> </div>You have only just barely provided data that does not fit my model and it only doesn't fit my model in your specific case. You don't need any incentive to craft all the time - crafting is its own incentive for you. Anything else is icing on the cake. Your behavior alone doesn't disprove the general pattern present.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Strawman. I cited verisimilitude, pacing, and ST-player interactions. I never stated that the game needs to simulate reality (and verisimilitude is simulating genre conceits anyway). </div> </div> </div>Taking a single statement away from context and calling it a straw man is itself a straw man.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Obviously not true. NPCs can be run on handwaving and shortcuts, or full rules, as the ST feels appropriate. And even then, plenty of the rules still fully apply to NPCs. </div> </div> </div>When the king calls for a new regiment to be outfitted, do you make sure to note that the NPC master blacksmiths forging arms and armor take regular breaks to do the tasks that their apprentices would otherwise be assumed to handle so they can make sure to recharge their creative batteries? Or do they just pump out swords and armor until the job is done?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Exalted explicitly establishes tiers of appropriateness. What is appropriate for a mortal smith heroic fantasy character in Exalted is not the same as what is appropriate for Solar smith heroic fantasy character in Exalted. </div> </div> </div>That doesn't make any sense. If both the mortal and the Solar are PCs, they use the same rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How much work it takes to do things. Again, you might not like the specific form. You're obnoxious dedication to excluding the middle constantly leads you to these stupid needles and barbs of, &quot;oh Heavy Arms, you think the system is perfect as written you're so precious,&quot; nonsense. Gating in crafting isn't perfect, but it's better. See above about 1e crafting. </div> </div> </div>The existence of a worse system is not an argument for the existing system. Monarchy is better than Communism in most cases, but that isn't an argument for Monarchy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Specifically no, in spirit yes. </div> </div> </div>Your Storyteller was terrible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T03:03:09-07:00","id":1232339,"title":"","content":"Well only one thing in that whole mess worth responding too:<br /> <br /> If you don't want to be told you're lying every three lines, stop lying so damned much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T04:29:25-07:00","id":1232343,"title":"","content":"Re: Catapults.<br /> I had an issue like this in 2nd edition several years before Scroll of Kings came out. So I had to look up the essence based artillery in Wonders of the Lost Age and eyeball something appropriate from that.<br /> <br /> I think I'd have been way better prepared for it with the Ex3 core because there were only 3 weapon profiles (excluding magical) that aren't trying to simulate actual damage and the tag system to customise things. I did a thought exercise on artillery a while back and for the mundane stuff I got pretty close what was in Arms of the Chosen.<br /> <br /> Now doing that as a Basic Project? While the Improvised tag says &quot;usable with both Brawl and Melee&quot; but I think it works pretty well for throwing a big rock at someone. Or in this case firing it from an oversized slingshot.<br /> <br /> My guiding star on my rulings is basically &quot;if average school kids can do it with the appropriate supplies your PC doesn't need a warm up&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 07-04-2018, 07:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T05:26:05-07:00","id":1232348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232339#post1232339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well only one thing in that whole mess worth responding too:<br /> <br /> If you don't want to be told you're lying every three lines, stop lying so damned much.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seriously? You finally start saying something interesting, I try to highlight that, and your response is to retreat into delusion?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T08:06:47-07:00","id":1232382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232244#post1232244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Glad to hear it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Something I've been considering but haven't worked out yet in terms of Charm design when considering how I'd improve Solar Crafts is something that I could see Alchemicals taking instead to give them a more distinct flavor on the bread and butter Craft Charms:<br /> <br /> Craft slots as Charm currency. This would come in two forms, mostly aligning to the current Momentum and Power trees. First would be bonuses to Charms based on how many Slots have projects in them. The more stuff you're doing at once, the cheaper and/or stronger Charms that do things like increase rewards for completing projects, do tricks like producing an extra item, etc. are. An Alchemical in this model would likely embody a workshop a bit more literally than the Solar Charm names evoke. While you need to be careful around tonal issues with mass production, the &quot;do awesome things&quot; here would all stem conceptually from producing more.<br /> <br /> Second would be bonuses to Charms based on dedicating unused slots too them. This is for when you're seriously crafting something and putting all your energy into it. Incidentally making whatever slot generating Charms remain after the culling very valuable for synergy here making them less boring as they boost the efficiency of your dice-trick Charms, freeing up room for &quot;do awesome things&quot; Charms at the top of the tree instead of ever more potent/complicated dice tricks. And of course here we're producing &quot;better.&quot; Though replicating that for anything but Artifacts is difficult with 3e's abstraction of equipment. Though nothing technically stops there from being Charms that let an Exalt add a Tag to a weapon/armor (though it's dangerous territory to go overboard with), which would be cool for demonstrating the higher tech level of the citizens of Autocthon as something that's not super high up the tree but an example of &quot;better&quot; that's not just more cxp/slots.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Oh man, I gotta say, I really like these ideas and plan to steal them. That really fits with Alchemicals focusing more on mass production without letting them churn out artifacts like candy!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T15:31:38-07:00","id":1232491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232343#post1232343\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or in this case firing it from an oversized slingshot.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is also the possible miscommunication on exactly what one is talking about with a catapult. It's an imprecise term and all that.<br /> <br /> But then again, it's one of the reasons I don't like the current project definition style. A catapult could be as simple as an oversized slightshot with a latch, or a complicate bit of engineering that requires accurate construction of ratcheting systems, counterweights or tension source, and structural supports, where even small flaws can potentially rip the thing apart with enough force to kill anyone standing to close.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My guiding star on my rulings is basically &quot;if average school kids can do it with the appropriate supplies your PC doesn't need a warm up&quot; </div> </div> </div>These sorts of &quot;common sense&quot; style things always frustrate me, because i can never apply them. &quot;Average school kids&quot; just adds a layer of confusion and source of disagreement. I wouldn't trust the average kid at the high school I graduated from (being a very suburban prep-school with no classes like tech ed, shop, or the like) to build me a chair from IKEA. My wife went to a high school in a very rural area, where a rather significant number of them (with modern tools and all) could build a decent chair from a log you dragged into the shop class workroom. And those schools are in the same state about 2 hours apart.<br /> <br /> How do I even start on figuring out what's &quot;average&quot; here vs. a rather divergent set of experiences with just two people?<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232348#post1232348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Seriously?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep (the delusion comment didn't help!).<br /> <br /> I'm tempted to be pithy and all, but a part of me is still holding out, so I'll give this one last shot and all:<br /> <br /> As far as I'm concerned, my two most primary goals (as already stated no less) have been as met as I feel our conversation is capable of: anything interesting I have to say to people besides the two of us might get something out of seems spent, and I don't see any new or pressing challenges to my own positions to bother countering with. When I feel like more people are going to like my posts because of dismissive and mocking comments than anything of substance I might say, I take that as a big red flag to just stop. To the secondary goals of refining my own design ideas, you've blown me off on that so I'm not going to get anything there, and you've continued to do everything I've said is convincing me that you don't want to enter into this in good faith, so a goal of trying to understand each other is dead as well.<br /> <br /> So I'm basically down to the following choices:<br /> <br /> 1) Continue snarking at each other until one of us finally steps out of bounds and the mods step in.<br /> <br /> 2) Hold to my impression that you are either arguing in bad faith, or arguing in a fashion indistinguishable to me as bad faith, and see the post I didn't have a big response to as a single there is no intention to change this. Since I see nothing to gain, and nothing to look forward to if I continue, I just stop engaging in that debate.<br /> <br /> <br /> If anything, from a purely tactical perspective this is the best time for me to do this. Since asking, and then saying, and then saying very harshly, all failed and such, leverage is the next reasonable tool. If you want to hear what I might have to say on the stuff you were so intrigued by, it's now stuck behind you needing to put in the effort to convince me to want to engage by giving me a reason to reexamine my estimation of your intentions.<br /> <br /> Or, more simply, why should I indulge your desires when you've made it clear you don't care that you're creative a negative experience for me? I shouldn't, so I'm not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T21:44:46-07:00","id":1232555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232491#post1232491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If anything, from a purely tactical perspective this is the best time for me to do this.</div> </div> </div> </div>The page where the OP posted his regret over having even started this thread might have also been a good place to stop.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-04T22:58:53-07:00","id":1232561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232122#post1232122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On another note, I'm also homebrewing up some Alchemicals. Does anyone have some suggestions on how to write craft charms that aren't just boring cxp generators and/or slot charms? Or point to a good thread for re-written solar craft charms?</div> </div> </div> </div>WFHW is a pretty good case study, though DB Craft has the \u201cadvantage\u201d of not being allowed many of the boring kinds of Craft charm in the first place so it <i>had</i> to make do with more interesting ones. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-05T00:02:26-07:00","id":1232568,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232555#post1232555\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The page where the OP posted his regret over having even started this thread might have also been a good place to stop.</div> </div> </div> </div>*stares into the middle distance*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229925-hello-i-did-up-a-scavenger-lands-map-based-on-3e\" target=\"_blank\">3e Scavenger Lands map</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/a/NQewCv0\" target=\"_blank\">3e Combat tracker card</a><br /> <a href=\"https://imgur.com/uGrZk2r\" target=\"_blank\">2e Nexus map</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1079-megapope","name":"Megapope","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-05T04:56:51-07:00","id":1232592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232491#post1232491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or, more simply, why should I indulge your desires when you've made it clear you don't care that you're creative a negative experience for me? I shouldn't, so I'm not.</div> </div> </div> </div>From my perspective, the one who created the negative experience was yourself. I still don't know why you started calling my arguments dishonest all the way back on page three.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-05T15:24:22-07:00","id":1232727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232592#post1232592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> From my perspective, the one who created the negative experience was yourself. I still don't know why you started calling my arguments dishonest all the way back on page three.</div> </div> </div> </div>You've been calling any &quot;Basic&quot; projects as being &quot;insignificant&quot;. It's really telling, because it shows you're not engaging with the Crafting rules as they are actually written.<br /> <br /> When you base your arguments on what looks like a deliberate misinterpretation of the rules... Yeah, it comes across as dishonest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/851-paradim","name":"Paradim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-05T17:36:21-07:00","id":1232757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1231749#post1231749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I considered doing an actual play write-up but both my players are new to Exalted and weren't sure how they felt about their actions coming under the scrutiny of more experienced players.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> A shame we dont have more of those. I think a big problem is that people dont know how to be a cool crafter without doing artifacts, and it could help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12483-ale137","name":"ale137","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-07T05:33:48-07:00","id":1233034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232491#post1232491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do I even start on figuring out what's &quot;average&quot; here vs. a rather divergent set of experiences with just two people?</div> </div> </div> </div>You're overthinking this and ended up comparing trained vs. untrained which is reflected by a different aspect of the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ale137</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1232757#post1232757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A shame we dont have more of those. I think a big problem is that people dont know how to be a cool crafter without doing artifacts, and it could help.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hopefully she'll get more comfortable later on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-07T05:48:59-07:00","id":1233036,"title":"","content":"When I feel I have the time, I may try and do a log for Spider.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-07T09:11:59-07:00","id":1233055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1233034#post1233034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're overthinking this and ended up comparing trained vs. untrained which is reflected by a different aspect of the system.</div> </div> </div> </div>I kinda wish that was the case? When I first started STing, my groups were a large mix as I mentioned, and my thoughts about &quot;common sense&quot; and such frequently failed in the light of differing expectations. It took awhile before I recognized that a lot of stupid moments in my games were because I was expecting everyone to consider &quot;average&quot; the same way I would; a bit colored by the first groups I played with all being from my middle and high school days where we had much more consistent and shared experiences.<br /> <br /> I mean, I didn't think you were talking about &quot;in the system&quot; here, because you mentioned appropriateness based on average school kids, which doens't really work from within the system (most kids in Creation don't go to school and all) as a metric. But my whole point is that what average school kids are trained in varies significantly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-10T06:09:21-07:00","id":1233673,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meianno Yuurei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1229942#post1229942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exalted 3e crafting system replacements are something of a dime a dozen. It depends on what you don't like about what is. Some want to chop down the Solar charm tree to like 10 charms, some want to eliminate what they see as 'grind' (integrating a crafter's story as a crafter into the narrative of the game so there's more than just &quot;I feel like making a legendary daiklave even though I haven't made so much as a butter knife on screen&quot;), etc. Have you determined what you don't like about the 3e system?<br /> <br /> V: and yeah, this. Craft in core is designed to reward the guy who looks at every situation and thinks, &quot;what can I make to make things better?&quot;. If the preference is &quot;I never craft unless I'm making something that is Big and Important and the Focus Of An Entire Story&quot; it won't work as well for you, so that's a thing to tackle. (I conceptually at least love the idea of it so, I never had a big issue with it)<br /> <br /> Anything that creates an emotional intimacy in someone, gets you a material benefit, or serves one of your intimacies, will get you craft XP. Without limit. Once you realize this, it goes from &quot;I have to make ten thousand stupid things to get anywhere&quot; to &quot;holy crap I am swimming in craft XP by making the world a better or worse place as befits my concept&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Intent and story potential like this would be something I'd like to see in a storyteller's guide.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1188890-momentum-exalted\" target=\"_blank\">Momentum Exalted!</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6192-simon-darkstep","name":"Simon Darkstep","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-10T23:47:45-07:00","id":1233912,"title":"","content":"Update with personal experience with Exalted 3e craft: First session, two basic and a major craft project later [turning sand into a simple ornamental glass flower to trade for a new friend's purchases, repairing a wooden wall smashed up in a fight, constructing a wall around the village to fend off raiders], I have 5s 9g laying around. After one session. At this pace, in 6 sessions I'll have enough to try an artifact if i want, even if I need to try and use all five rolls [spoilers I probably won't need to]<br /> <br /> No grinding toothpicks, no making five hundred spoons, just &quot;Oh, hey, I can do a thing! I'mma do the thing! *does the thing*&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","user":"Meianno Yuurei","date":". 07-11-2018, 02:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T00:51:23-07:00","id":1233919,"title":"","content":"Ah, but did your eyes undergo a gruesome transformation, such that you could no longer see a beautiful and nuanced setting, <i>only</i> nails for your craft-hammer?<br /> <br /> If that didn't occur, I think we can safely put the argument to bed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 07-11-2018, 05:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T10:37:04-07:00","id":1234044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meianno Yuurei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1233912#post1233912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Update with personal experience with Exalted 3e craft: First session, two basic and a major craft project later [turning sand into a simple ornamental glass flower to trade for a new friend's purchases, repairing a wooden wall smashed up in a fight, constructing a wall around the village to fend off raiders], I have 5s 9g laying around. After one session. At this pace, in 6 sessions I'll have enough to try an artifact if i want, even if I need to try and use all five rolls [spoilers I probably won't need to]<br /> <br /> No grinding toothpicks, no making five hundred spoons, just &quot;Oh, hey, I can do a thing! I'mma do the thing! *does the thing*&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I am coming into this argument late but your experience is about on par with mine.<br /> <br /> The crafting system looks hard to deal with but in practice it really isn't that big of a deal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2302,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2302-snakesandsuns","user":"Snakesandsuns","date":". 07-11-2018, 01:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2302-snakesandsuns","name":"Snakesandsuns","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2302_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"224"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T17:25:59-07:00","id":1234119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meianno Yuurei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1233912#post1233912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Update with personal experience with Exalted 3e craft: First session, two basic and a major craft project later [turning sand into a simple ornamental glass flower to trade for a new friend's purchases, repairing a wooden wall smashed up in a fight, constructing a wall around the village to fend off raiders], I have 5s 9g laying around. After one session. At this pace, in 6 sessions I'll have enough to try an artifact if i want, even if I need to try and use all five rolls [spoilers I probably won't need to]<br /> <br /> No grinding toothpicks, no making five hundred spoons, just &quot;Oh, hey, I can do a thing! I'mma do the thing! *does the thing*&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Out of curiosity, what are your crafts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12483-ale137","name":"ale137","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T18:18:28-07:00","id":1234128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1229959#post1229959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I currently use the system mostly as written with the definition of basic projects <a href=\"https://ericminton.wordpress.com/2015/10/29/exalted-3e-sample-basic-projects/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> save for a few key differences.<br /> - Craft Xp is renamed Inspiration because that's less confusing and gives me an excuse to reward a bunch of it to a player who'd been struggling to get involved.<br /> - The existence of Project Slots is ignored until Superior and Legendary.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>So much this. My impression over grievances with crafting systems seems to be that nine times out of ten it's just drama about confusion with terminology blown out of proportion. Tweaking a term here and there in 3e truly makes wonders for the clarity of a bunch of systems.<br /> <br /> But on making wonders, sorcerous workings. they can make wonders for those who just want the occasional crazy arcane item as an event but not really get into a nitty-gritty experimentalist thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T21:02:21-07:00","id":1234169,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ale137</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1234119#post1234119\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Out of curiosity, what are your crafts?</div> </div> </div> </div>Weaving 3, Architecture 1, Metalworking 1, Woodworking 1. Used Metalworking for the glass flower with permission [and +2 difficulty] as per the &quot;For mundane crafts projects, Storytellers are encouraged to be generous in interpreting logical edge cases for uses of Craft. If a character needs to repair a horseshoe, but only has Craft (Armoring), it\u2019s reasonable to declare that<br /> the character\u2019s Craft rating encompasses basic metal fabrication and to allow her to use her Craft rating anyway, perhaps at +1 difficulty.&quot; rule on p151. Excellency and a good basic attribute goes a long way to shoring up low initial dots, which I plan on raising a variety of [we're using flat XP, yay].","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-13T03:50:39-07:00","id":1234576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1233055#post1233055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I kinda wish that was the case? When I first started STing, my groups were a large mix as I mentioned, and my thoughts about &quot;common sense&quot; and such frequently failed in the light of differing expectations. It took awhile before I recognized that a lot of stupid moments in my games were because I was expecting everyone to consider &quot;average&quot; the same way I would; a bit colored by the first groups I played with all being from my middle and high school days where we had much more consistent and shared experiences.<br /> <br /> I mean, I didn't think you were talking about &quot;in the system&quot; here, because you mentioned appropriateness based on average school kids, which doens't really work from within the system (most kids in Creation don't go to school and all) as a metric. But my whole point is that what average school kids are trained in varies significantly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Its a guideline I came up with to help my player wrap her head around Basic Projects without giving her a page of examples. If that helps things click for other people, that's great. If not *shrugs*.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-14T16:27:31-07:00","id":1235107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meianno Yuurei</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1234169#post1234169\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Weaving 3, Architecture 1, Metalworking 1, Woodworking 1. Used Metalworking for the glass flower with permission [and +2 difficulty] as per the &quot;For mundane crafts projects, Storytellers are encouraged to be generous in interpreting logical edge cases for uses of Craft. If a character needs to repair a horseshoe, but only has Craft (Armoring), it\u2019s reasonable to declare that<br /> the character\u2019s Craft rating encompasses basic metal fabrication and to allow her to use her Craft rating anyway, perhaps at +1 difficulty.&quot; rule on p151. Excellency and a good basic attribute goes a long way to shoring up low initial dots, which I plan on raising a variety of [we're using flat XP, yay].</div> </div> </div> </div>Forgive me, but that seems like a little bit more generous than a 'logical edge case.' If metalworking and glass crafting share the same basic principles in such a way, especially when Craft skills are so finely cut-up, why aren't we just using Craft: Fire? I mean, before the Ask the Devs thread cast down the rule about needing a skill, the biggest complaint locally was 'Gee, wouldn't it be nice to spend eckspees and training time on something other than buying every craft to one so I can reliably do stuff,' but that just sounds a bit much.<br /> <br /> And, aside, I do have to note that I'm getting awfully tired of seeing artifacts dragged into these discussions. The only people I've ever seen citing the fact that you can't sit in your workshop and churn out Beloveds Adorei by the cartload are the people <i>defending</i> the system - the critics, or at least the ones I've encountered, just want to know if they can outfit this village militia for the Dawn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2444-lindhrive","name":"Lindhrive","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-14T18:12:55-07:00","id":1235124,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lindhrive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1235107#post1235107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Forgive me, but that seems like a little bit more generous than a 'logical edge case.' If metalworking and glass crafting share the same basic principles in such a way, especially when Craft skills are so finely cut-up, why aren't we just using Craft: Fire? I mean, before the Ask the Devs thread cast down the rule about needing a skill, the biggest complaint locally was 'Gee, wouldn't it be nice to spend eckspees and training time on something other than buying every craft to one so I can reliably do stuff,' but that just sounds a bit much.<br /> <br /> And, aside, I do have to note that I'm getting awfully tired of seeing artifacts dragged into these discussions. The only people I've ever seen citing the fact that you can't sit in your workshop and churn out Beloveds Adorei by the cartload are the people <i>defending</i> the system - the critics, or at least the ones I've encountered, just want to know if they can outfit this village militia for the Dawn.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> -checks arms dealer craft supernal-<br /> yes.yes you can.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-14T19:25:53-07:00","id":1235138,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lindhrive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1235107#post1235107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Forgive me, but that seems like a little bit more generous than a 'logical edge case.' If metalworking and glass crafting share the same basic principles in such a way, especially when Craft skills are so finely cut-up, why aren't we just using Craft: Fire?</div> </div> </div> </div>Edge cases are invoked based on the task that's being attempted not the craft that would be employed. I assume when she's talking about a glass flower she means something like this.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/0szfLv1a_oE/maxresdefault.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/> </div></div> </div> </div> This is something that visitors make at a glass museum, it's not hard and is mostly a matter of melting the glass to make an interesting shape.<br /> <br /> If it was something like this <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <img src=\"https://images-na.ssl-images-amazon.com/images/I/71GFenFYzXL._SY679_.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div> justifying it with knowledge of metalworking would be pretty bullshit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 07-14-2018, 10:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-14T21:15:18-07:00","id":1235176,"title":"","content":"Yah, pretty much the former, a pretty little simple bauble, not ornate glasswork or anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-15T04:54:42-07:00","id":1235245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lindhrive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1235107#post1235107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Forgive me, but that seems like a little bit more generous than a 'logical edge case.' If metalworking and glass crafting share the same basic principles in such a way, especially when Craft skills are so finely cut-up, why aren't we just using Craft: Fire?</div> </div> </div> </div>When I first saw the 3rd edition, I wondered why they'd split it up so much. But I later realised that the current Craft skills better reflect actual characters; no-one learns to be a Cook/leather-worker or a carpenter/paper-maker/flower arranger. Whereas people might learn, for example, to be a weapons-maker, which would include both forging metal and using wood and leather for bindings, hafts, etc.<br /> <br /> Which is not to say the new system is perfect, because it relies a lot on having a generous GM who you constantly have to ask &quot;what difficulty is it if I use this Craft instead of that Craft?&quot; which slows the game down.<br /> (Except fixing my warstrider. The book says specifically I can use Craft Artefact at +3 difficulty instead of having to buy Craft First Age Artefacts and be a sorcerer, so I don't have to ask him about that.)<br /> Plus, I don't think either of my STs actually understand the Craft system anyway...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, aside, I do have to note that I'm getting awfully tired of seeing artifacts dragged into these discussions. The only people I've ever seen citing the fact that you can't sit in your workshop and churn out Beloveds Adorei by the cartload are the people <i>defending</i> the system </div> </div> </div>Well, yes, because that's what this Crafting system was specifically designed for.<br /> But they may be talking about artefacts because I've brought up artefacts when criticising the system.<br /> <br /> Which is not theorycraft, I have seen quite a few players who have had quite a lot of difficulty making artefacts, though for different reasons:<br /> <br /> In fact, thinking about it;<br /> <br /> Cid the essence 4 Twilight Airship engineer has managed a single non-artefact superior project (with help from another crafter).<br /> <br /> Dupree the Twilight crafter who played enough sessions in 3rd edition to get from 250xp to well over 400xp, and had 7 craft abilities at 5, made... one artefact, a soulsteel/moonsilver wrackstaff (with the soul of Chejop Kejak) for his Lunar mate. At the beginning this is because he didn't have enough Craft XP, later he had enough (because we got him to buy Supreme Perfection of Craft) but he just couldn't be bothered with all the book-keeping hassle and fiddlyness of the system.<br /> <br /> Lao the evil sorcerer, who has done things like deforest an entire mountain to produce a ton of basic projects, has made... 0 artefacts. He just doesn't have enough gold xp. And when I told him how to do it, what charms to get to help, what charms to get to give him enough dice to succeed at making artefacts, his player said &quot;this is stupidly complicated, I can't be arsed to make my party magic weapons after all, I'm just going to do workings instead.&quot;<br /> <br /> I've made a blue jade hand, and a pair of blue jade daiklaives. So I'm doing fine.* (And I have Supreme Perfection of Craft)<br /> <br /> Basically, I think I'm the only player who copes with the Craft system. Everyone else I play with seems to consider it an enormous headache (except one player who thinks it's fine, but isn't interested in playing a Crafter so it doesn't really matter).<br /> <br /> For some people, this system seems to work fine. Plenty of people have given examples of that on this forum. But for others it's either too difficult, especially considering how much XP it costs to be good at it. And some players just get fed up with all the book-keeping and fiddlyness of the system, and compare it negatively to the more straightforward workings system.<br /> <br /> *Saying this, I do feel that what I've got out of it is not really worth the amount of XP I've put into Craft. I'd have been better off buying 12 charms and more dots in other abilities, tbh. Then I wouldn't have needed to replace my severed hand. But maybe I'll get more out of it in future.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - the critics, or at least the ones I've encountered, just want to know if they can outfit this village militia for the Dawn. </div> </div> </div>This isn't a great example, but I do agree with your overall point.<br /> As Epee102 says, you can outfit the village militia with spears, basic bows and arrows, maybe boiled leather jerkins.<br /> The reason is because those are all basic projects. (I don't feel the book is very clear about this though, I'm basing the fact they're basic projects on things the dev Eric Minton wrote on his blog; my initial impression on reading the book was that weapons are all major projects)<br /> <br /> But yes, the main problem I've actually seen with the system isn't the gating of artefacts (which should be gated), but the gating of major projects. It's just very weird when the player says &quot;my amazing smith, the greatest smith in all the land, wants to make 10 fine swords for my bodyguards and friends&quot; and the ST says &quot;do you have 100 silver xp? Nope? Well you can't do it. For some reason that doesn't really have any in-game explanation. You have to go and sell like 50 basic projects first. Or do 50 things connected to something you care about. Or sell 32 things connected to things you care about. Oh, and technically you're supposed to roll for each of those projects, in case you fail, and if you roll well you might only need 32 rolls instead of 50 before you can make those 10 nice swords!&quot;<br /> <br /> With artefacts, they're magic, so if the ST says &quot;you have to give 20 good-quality swords to people you like, before you can make a magic sword&quot; then, hey, it's magic. Maybe that's how magic swords work. But it's weird for major projects, which are not magic.<br /> <br /> In the end, I just house-ruled that you could do Major projects as Basic projects. If you don't have enough silver points, that shouldn't stop you from doing a major project, you just won't get gold xp from it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 07-15-2018, 07:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-15T10:17:22-07:00","id":1235285,"title":"","content":"If nothing else there's always the option of &quot;I outfit them with Improvised weapons and armor as a basic project. This isn't a thousand-folded katana, this is one of those cheap poured uruk-hai scimitars, but it'll last the battle, hopefully.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-15T13:34:14-07:00","id":1235322,"title":"","content":"I think a lot of this comes down to the book not giving enough space to talk about certain concepts with Craft.<br /> <br /> Like, the element Crafts in 2e were kinda dumb as noted above, as they don't reflect any specific training all that well. Going back to individual crafts makes sense (even if the implementation is still questionable to plenty of us). But the books clearly doesn't spend enough time setting expectations for what makes a good craft for the game, and then people just plop in something close to their concept without thinking about how it'll work in play.<br /> <br /> Like, I made a shipwright because that's what I wanted to do... but it turned out to be an obnoxiously good craft choice for the 3e system, as it provides ample Basic, and Major projects to work on, as well as mundane Superiors before you get into Artifact boats. It involves carpentry, fiber work (ropes and sails), some basic minor engineering, and even some weapons if the ST is feeling generous.<br /> <br /> If you make a cooper... well... it's gonna kinda suck if you were expecting to do a lot of crazy things with it.<br /> <br /> The book doesn't really give you any guidance on this. It's not that every craft has to be something like shipwright or blacksmith that's really applicable and works on multiple project levels, but prompting someone looking at Craft to consider broadening their choice to make it more fun in play, or making sure to have some complimentary crafts (such as one that's more Basic/some Major focused, and another that's more Major/Superior focused) would probably have done the game a lot of good here.<br /> <br /> There's also the weird thing where the game could have really used to address &quot;batching.&quot;<br /> <br /> Why is cooking a feast for 40 people one project (even if it isn't forty projects, why not one per course?), but arming a squad of ten guard with swords ten projects? There's not a lot of consistency on such things, but we're not supposed to subdivide them either. The biggest thing wrong with the system here is that the game doesn't actually talk about it to even know what the expectations really are, and what fixes might be appropriate.<br /> <br /> And, of course, just as the game explains the reasoning for the withering/decisive split (which doesn't make sense in-character), more writing on why the gating and what it's meant to look like could only help things (even for people that disagree and want to change it so they would understand the baseline better).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-15T14:27:22-07:00","id":1235340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1235322#post1235322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think a lot of this comes down to the book not giving enough space to talk about certain concepts with Craft.<br /> <br /> Like, the element Crafts in 2e were kinda dumb as noted above, as they don't reflect any specific training all that well. Going back to individual crafts makes sense (even if the implementation is still questionable to plenty of us). But the books clearly doesn't spend enough time setting expectations for what makes a good craft for the game, and then people just plop in something close to their concept without thinking about how it'll work in play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it helps that two of of the example crafts are Weaponsmithing and Armoursmithing, which gives you the impression that the divisions are very fine. I'm not sure they're supposed to be.<br /> <br /> <br /> [/QUOTE]There's also the weird thing where the game could have really used to address &quot;batching.&quot;<br /> <br /> Why is cooking a feast for 40 people one project (even if it isn't forty projects, why not one per course?), but arming a squad of ten guard with swords ten projects? There's not a lot of consistency on such things, but we're not supposed to subdivide them either. The biggest thing wrong with the system here is that the game doesn't actually talk about it to even know what the expectations really are, and what fixes might be appropriate.<br /> <br /> And, of course, just as the game explains the reasoning for the withering/decisive split (which doesn't make sense in-character), more writing on why the gating and what it's meant to look like could only help things (even for people that disagree and want to change it so they would understand the baseline better).[/QUOTE]<br /> Hopefully the ST guide will go into some of this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-15T16:38:51-07:00","id":1235360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1229940-crafting-system?p=1235245#post1235245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But yes, the main problem I've actually seen with the system isn't the gating of artefacts (which should be gated), but the gating of major projects. It's just very weird when the player says &quot;my amazing smith, the greatest smith in all the land, wants to make 10 fine swords for my bodyguards and friends&quot; and the ST says &quot;do you have 100 silver xp? Nope? Well you can't do it. For some reason that doesn't really have any in-game explanation. You have to go and sell like 50 basic projects first. Or do 50 things connected to something you care about. Or sell 32 things connected to things you care about. Oh, and technically you're supposed to roll for each of those projects, in case you fail, and if you roll well you might only need 32 rolls instead of 50 before you can make those 10 nice swords!&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm mostly okay with that.<br /> A well-made sword could easily take two weeks to make, so this proposition is almost half a year's worth of work to a mortal craftsman. The only part about this that I find kind of weird is that you need to somehow generate 10 silver xp to get the ball rolling. If there's insufficient wish-fulfilment for a Twilight Caste swordsmith the problem lies with Solar Craft.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}